by an opt-in flag rather than a change to the shared rule.\n\nAlso revises the security note that said the partner PUT must refuse anything\nbut POSTPONED. The sibling route already accepts IN_REVIEW, and refusing it here\nwould re-create a smaller version of the dead end this phase removes.\n\n* fix(doe): take the channel options off the wire contract\n\nThe design said \"neither of these is a field on either wire contract, and that\nis the point\". It was true of the partner DTO — omitted, whitelisted, rebuilt\nfrom a literal, and pinned by a spec — and false of the shared CreateReportDto,\nwhich is the @Body() of POST /api/v1/reports/salary. Both options carried\n@ApiOptionalBoolean there, so IsOptional + IsBoolean survived whitelisting and\nany authenticated applicant could send withdrawPostponedSibling: true to retire\ntheir own deliberate postponement, or postponeUnexplainedOutliers: true to file\nPOSTPONED where the service means to answer 400.\n\nSame company throughout, so never a cross-tenant hole. What makes it blocking is\nthat the claim was false on the one surface nothing checked, and the partner\nspec asserting the opposite is what would have stopped anyone looking.\n\nThey are a separate argument now — CreateSalaryOptions, mirroring\nSubmitSalaryOptions one layer up — so the claim holds by construction. The new\nspec asserts it on class-validator metadata rather than on a rejected request,\nbecause doe-api runs whitelist without forbidNonWhitelisted: an unknown field is\nstripped silently, so there is no 400 to catch, and what made these settable was\nthe decorator registering validation for them. Mutation-checked by re-adding a\ndecorated field.\n\nThree more from the same review:\n\nThe sibling withdrawal was keyed on who is filing rather than on what is being\nreplaced, so a payroll vendor''s submission would retire a POSTPONED report the\nemployer had deliberately deferred on island.is — with no signal to either\nparty, since the vendor cannot see that report and the employer''s 409 naming it\nwould be gone. It now matches providerType, and the docblock that claimed\nisland.is was untouched says which half of that it meant.\n\neditOutliers wrote status: SUBMITTED with no status predicate. That was\nunreachable until this phase let a POSTPONED sibling be withdrawn; now a\nsubmission carrying withdrawPostponedSibling can retire the report while its\nexplanations are being written, and an unconditional write would resurrect a\nWITHDRAWN row as SUBMITTED — two in-flight salary reports for one company, the\ninvariant the company lock exists to hold. It is a compare-and-set, and zero\nrows affected is a 409.\n\nThe guide contradicted itself in five places and stated one thing that was never\ntrue. B5 still told vendors to send outliersPostponed: true (now a 400) and said\nthe partner API had no route out of POSTPONED (this PR adds one); the B6 body\ntable still listed the removed field; the sibling policy still said POSTPONED\ngives 409. And the new B7 text claimed outliersPostponed \"stays true after B9\" —\nit is derived from status === POSTPONED, so it flips to false exactly when B9\nsucceeds, which would have had a vendor conclude its PUT had not applied.\n\nAlso: the replay specs mocked a row with no status, so status: existing.status\npassed through untested while toEqual ignored it; the partner contract spec\nrestated the validation options as literals instead of importing the app''s, and\nmoves to the app so it can; and the new route had nothing pinning its scope.\n\nNot fixed, and it needs a running API: clientConfig.json still declares the old\nCreateReportResponseDto. It is curled from doe-api rather than generated from\nsource, so it cannot be written by hand without fabricating generator output.\nRecorded in the plan''s checklist, including that the generated-files CI gate\ndoes not cover that file.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsphCCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6SIQADM86Eo6ZfgdQWqemDsXfGFj\nh8gfdPgxB8Hrq7XzU2Lw4ANM19c3WvOts9/G78Vn/mupLJ1ldwNXGFdJambfrtpT\nYlNT3mUyvoBpyNC+QYa9RCL4OhX3T9PvOo76NBTLDKn/RGlym4OkhLggvOcUthYh\nOqqfolHyig7vwND63nUF4mIOfQHYquHmqveAWkYmOG2uOe7ODXQ/ZblysvTGZunt\nPMop0NO7GHUo2JQ0l2uhnjX1hQ2+G4v+CAssfFICmf9L/IMbTeRBJi7CvRKM+DIw\ndpkkYy3FRGFEgZQ8YNEjoOim+2L+kUL1g+vARGe+QPdKGueCQUdtCYrr0oN8dW+C\nHQ7tWUW30ddcxaOcqB3n+lsfdRTHCGcE5RGduDgoRMRMbUvw8IrJ4OvTol7lmBHl\nBesUdYPWVTlh3giTzWRJ8+7UPXv4cD3XxmHZothpyouZa32Yg7+OyuUGkhwIdOds\nnDVnW+D519EK10vpmA2Ck4hPofKdw0rOLP+Bo8ST5QP876dGQ8ONzTlH0LMlVSLj\nTFjBdi7Dn20Fi4/lRRrSzHTwvcJc6GZJytWFfJq5cgFBcsx3YXOptS7q6I0QaOyH\ntehqR1yxadNKOdAaLESKRrgda1m5ZbVHXLExAPOtpIsMJJ9KSUhpPlZYwjR5b32s\nPhCX1Ng/3Nip+9Lut6mG\n=JUml\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T15:01:23Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/a9527ec32770fa147c3f064165b8f720877694d3","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKGE5NTI3ZWMzMjc3MGZhMTQ3YzNmMDY0MTY1YjhmNzIwODc3Njk0ZDM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/2aff0f30315fd1cd4869207e171bbc2ffba7f41b","sha":"2aff0f30315fd1cd4869207e171bbc2ffba7f41b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/2aff0f30315fd1cd4869207e171bbc2ffba7f41b"}],"sha":"a9527ec32770fa147c3f064165b8f720877694d3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/a9527ec32770fa147c3f064165b8f720877694d3"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/2aff0f30315fd1cd4869207e171bbc2ffba7f41b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T15:01:20Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T15:01:20Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(doe): partner self-service web app skeleton (#1537)\n\n* feat(doe): scaffold the partner self-service web app\n\nAdds apps/directorate-of-equality-partner-web, a company-facing Next.js app\nauthenticated through a new island.is IDS client. It exists so a company can\nreach its API keys without an open application: today the island.is key screen\nis only reachable from one, so a company that has lost its key and has nothing\nin flight has no route but a support ticket.\n\nThis is the skeleton. It proves the auth and deployment path; the key screen is\na stub and issues nothing. Delegation and vendor clients are out of scope, as is\nanything in the two APIs.\n\nAuth deliberately inverts doe-web''s. doe-web''s signIn callback is an allowlist\nfor Jafnrettisstofa staff -- it calls getMyUser and bounces anyone unregistered\nto access-denied. Here any company that authenticates belongs, so the only gates\nare a present id_token and a nationalId claim. The session carries `actor`\nalongside `companyNationalId`: under procuration the id token''s nationalId is\nthe company and `actor` is the human, and the API''s ISLAND_IS issuance path\nneeds the latter to satisfy doe_api_key_created_actor_chk. Nothing reads it\nyet; wiring it now is cheaper than retrofitting it.\n\nmiddleware.ts calls the shared createAuthMiddleware rather than duplicating it\nthe way doe-web does, and passes skipDefaultUrlCheck because the protected page\nserves `/`, which the helper otherwise exempts from auth outright. The matcher\nexcludes `error` so a real IDS failure renders instead of being redirected to\nthe sign-in page and read as an ordinary session expiry.\n\nNextAuth routes ten codes to the sign-in page and everything else, including\nAccessDenied, to pages.error -- so the procuration explanation lives on /error\nand the generic retry on /innskraning. Getting this backwards silently blames\nisland.is for a user who simply lacks procuration.\n\nNot yet in the deploy matrices, mirroring the existing exclusion of\ndirectorate-of-equality-partner-api: neither ECS service exists in the\nJafnrettisstofa terragrunt tree, and a name there fails `aws ecs wait\nservices-stable` on every deploy. Re-add both with that terragrunt PR.\n\nclientConfig.json and src/gen are not generated yet -- they need a running\ndirectorate-of-equality-api -- so the app does not build from this commit alone.\n\n* feat(doe): add the generated partner-web API contract\n\nclientConfig.json curled from directorate-of-equality-api''s Application swagger\ndocument, which is where the self-service key routes live:\n\n  /api/v1/application/api-keys        (company from the authenticated context)\n  /api/v1/application/api-keys/{id}\n\nNot the internal document doe-web generates from -- that one carries\n/api/v1/companies/{companyId}/api-keys, the ADMIN route behind AdminGuard, which\na company user cannot call. Generating from the wrong group would have produced\nan SDK whose only key methods are unusable here, and it would have typechecked.\n\nThe SDK generated from this exposes getApplicationApiKeys, issueApplicationApiKey\nand revokeApplicationApiKey. Nothing calls them yet; the screen is still a stub.\n\nGenerated against main at 2057c7b86, which narrowed three of the submit DTOs this\ndocument describes.\n\n* chore(shared): ignore the CLAUDE.md Next 16 generates per app\n\nNext 16.3.5 writes both AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md into an app directory on dev\nand build, controlled by next.config''s `agentRules`. AGENTS.md was already\nignored here; CLAUDE.md is the same generated pair and was missed, so it turns\nup untracked after anyone runs a web app. No CLAUDE.md is tracked anywhere in\nthis repo, so nothing is masked by this.\n\n* fix(doe): address review of the partner web scaffold\n\nEight findings from an external review, six fixed here and two decided.\n\nThe lockfile was never regenerated for the new workspace, so `yarn install\n--immutable` would have failed CI and nx could not resolve the project at all.\n\nA refused sign-in looped. Returning `false` rejects the NextAuth sign-in but\nleaves the IDS session alive, so retrying SSOs the same individual straight back\ninto the same refusal with no way out but clearing cookies. The sign-in now ends\nthe upstream session first, which means logoutHint comes back -- it was dropped\nearlier on the reasoning that the refusal path was gone. Only the allowlist was\ngone; this app still refuses, just for a different reason.\n\nEnding that session cost the error message, twice over. `signIn` returning a\nstring takes next-auth''s `typeof isAllowed === \"string\"` branch, which redirects\nwith no error param at all -- only `false` appends `?error=AccessDenied` -- and\nthe round trip through IDS does not preserve a query string either. So the\nperson who signed in without procuration was told island.is had failed. The\nreason now travels in a cookie the sign-in page reads and clears, the way\ndoe-web already does it.\n\nThat redirect returns to the bare origin rather than `${BASE_URL}/error`. IDS\nrefuses an unregistered post_logout_redirect_uri, so the subpath would have\nstranded the user on an island.is error page unless someone remembered to\nregister it; the origin lands on `/`, which now requires auth and forwards to\nthe sign-in page -- where the message is.\n\n`update-openapi-schema` ran curl and prettier under `parallel: true`, so\nprettier could format a half-downloaded file. Fixed in both DoE apps. The same\nrace exists in the four legal-gazette and official-journal webs and is left for\na separate change rather than widening this one across products.\n\nbindSdk and its five types were a byte-identical copy in both DoE webs; extracted\nto @dmr.is/trpc with both apps migrated. Its client parameter is generic because\nthe lib has no generated client of its own to import, and the body only forwards\nit.\n\nAlso dropped a session.invalid effect in the protected error boundary that\nduplicated the shared Header''s identical one rendered above it -- two concurrent\nOAuth redirects on the same tick -- and an unused publicProcedure whose guard the\ntRPC context had already made unreachable.\n\nDecided rather than fixed: the shared createAuthMiddleware refreshes on\naccess-token expiry only, while this app authenticates to the API with the id\ntoken. Constraining the new IDS client''s id_token lifetime is cheaper than an\nid-token branch in a helper five apps share; middleware.ts records why, and what\nto do if that configuration ever changes.\n\n* chore(doe): record why this app sends the id token alone\n\nReplaces a comment that asserted a constraint on the IDS client''s token\nlifetimes. Decoded tokens from the doe-partner-web dev client show the\nconstraint was unnecessary and the reasoning behind it wrong.\n\nBoth tokens are issued with the same 300s lifetime and the same exp, so the\nID token cannot expire before the access token and an ID-token branch in the\nshared middleware would be unreachable. An earlier commit added one; it is\ndropped.\n\nThe access token carries no identity at all -- scope, client_id, sub, sid,\nidp, acr, jti and nothing more. nationalId, name and subjectType are ID-token\nclaims. Since CompanyResourceGuard resolves the company from user.nationalId,\nand TokenJwtAuthGuard builds request.user from the FIRST token while lifting\nonly name and actor off the second, sending both tokens would leave\nnationalId undefined and 401 every request. Sending the ID token alone is not\ndrift from the two-token form the guard supports -- for this API it is the\nonly arrangement that works.\n\n* fix(doe): refuse an individual who signed in without procuration\n\nThe sign-in gate refused only an id token with no `nationalId` at all. An\nindividual signing in as themselves has one -- their own kennitala -- so they\npassed, while the comment above the gate, the comment on the error page and the\nIcelandic copy on both error screens all said that case was refused.\n\nNothing was exploitable: the router is a stub and nothing calls doe-api. It\nbecomes exploitable the moment key issuance is wired, because\nCompanyResourceGuard resolves -- and on annotated routes provisions -- a company\nfrom whatever `user.nationalId` it is handed, with no person-vs-company check of\nits own. A personal kennitala would mint a company row and a live API key.\n\nThe gate is now the id token''s `subjectType`: `legalEntity` for a session acting\nfor a company, `person` for someone signed in as themselves. Confirmed against\ndecoded tokens from this client rather than assumed.\n\nRefusing on a missing `actor` would have been wrong. A company can authenticate\nas itself, with no delegation and therefore no actor, and resolve-actor.ts is\nwritten to expect exactly that -- so that gate would have refused a legitimate\ncaller.\n\nThe predicate lives in its own module because importing authOptions pulls in\nnext-auth and jose, which jest cannot parse. That gave the app its first test\ntarget, modelled on legal-gazette-web''s; the spec covers the individual, the\ncompany via procuration, and the company acting for itself, and was checked\nagainst the old condition to confirm it fails there.\n\nAlso makes getServerClient''s token required. The optional form defaulted to a\nsession lookup and cast a possibly-undefined id token to string, so a caller\nwithout a session would have sent `Authorization: Bearer undefined` -- an auth\nbug at the API rather than a missing credential here.\n\n* feat(doe): authenticate to the API with the access token\n\nRequests the `@jafnretti.is/doe-partner-web` API resource scope and sends\n`session.accessToken` to directorate-of-equality-api instead of the id token.\n\nUntil now this app sent the id token, because DoE''s clients requested only\n`openid offline_access profile` and the resulting access token carried no\nidentity at all -- no nationalId, no actor -- while CompanyResourceGuard\nresolves a company from `user.nationalId`. Requesting an API resource scope\nchanges that: IDS then issues the access token for that resource and includes\nthe identity claims. Verified on decoded tokens from this client, on both an\nindividual and a procuration login: the access token gains `nationalId` (the\ncompany) alongside `actor.nationalId` (the human), which is what\nresolve-actor.ts needs to attribute an issued key to a person rather than the\ncompany.\n\nThe scope must be enabled for delegation on the IDS side. When it is not, a\nprocuration login still succeeds and IDS simply omits the scope and the claim,\nso nothing errors and the company silently goes missing -- that failure cost an\nhour to find, hence the comment in identityServerConfig.ts.\n\nThe id token is still issued, still stored on the session and still used: the\nsign-in gate reads its `subjectType`, the browser shows its `name`, and logout\nneeds it as `id_token_hint`. It is only no longer sent to the API.\n\nRefreshing on access-token expiry alone is therefore straightforwardly correct\nnow, rather than correct-by-coincidence because both tokens share a 300s\nlifetime.\n\nNote for deployment: the prod IDS client needs this scope, with delegation\nenabled, before the app is first deployed. Without it every request resolves no\ncompany.\n\n* fix(doe): correct what the sign-in and error pages say about AccessDenied\n\nBoth pages attributed `AccessDenied` to the refusal this app actually performs.\nIt never produces that code. The refusal returns a string from `signIn` so it\ncan end the IDS session first, and NextAuth attaches no `?error=` to a string\nreturn -- the reason travels in the `doe-partner.signin_error` cookie instead.\n`AccessDenied` comes only from the two `return false` branches, which are\nhandshake failures: no id_token, or a provider/access_token mismatch.\n\nThe comment on innskraning contradicted itself inside one block, stating the\nwrong attribution in its first paragraph and the right explanation in its\nsecond. Both were also stale twice over after the gate moved from `nationalId`\nto `subjectType`.\n\nThe error page is the part that mattered to a user: its AccessDenied copy said\nthe login was not connected to a company and told them to sign in with\nprocuration. Anyone reaching that page has a broken handshake, so it sent them\nafter a problem they do not have. The per-code branch is gone rather than\nreworded -- the real refusal cannot arrive there, so one message for a failed\nhandshake with the code shown for support is both correct and one less thing to\ndrift.","tree":{"sha":"043175a6962e62aea602f9f20990c83854370ecd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/043175a6962e62aea602f9f20990c83854370ecd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/2aff0f30315fd1cd4869207e171bbc2ffba7f41b","verification":{"payload":"tree 043175a6962e62aea602f9f20990c83854370ecd\nparent 1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1790089280 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790089280 +0000\n\nfeat(doe): partner self-service web app skeleton (#1537)\n\n* feat(doe): scaffold the partner self-service web app\n\nAdds apps/directorate-of-equality-partner-web, a company-facing Next.js app\nauthenticated through a new island.is IDS client. It exists so a company can\nreach its API keys without an open application: today the island.is key screen\nis only reachable from one, so a company that has lost its key and has nothing\nin flight has no route but a support ticket.\n\nThis is the skeleton. It proves the auth and deployment path; the key screen is\na stub and issues nothing. Delegation and vendor clients are out of scope, as is\nanything in the two APIs.\n\nAuth deliberately inverts doe-web''s. doe-web''s signIn callback is an allowlist\nfor Jafnrettisstofa staff -- it calls getMyUser and bounces anyone unregistered\nto access-denied. Here any company that authenticates belongs, so the only gates\nare a present id_token and a nationalId claim. The session carries `actor`\nalongside `companyNationalId`: under procuration the id token''s nationalId is\nthe company and `actor` is the human, and the API''s ISLAND_IS issuance path\nneeds the latter to satisfy doe_api_key_created_actor_chk. Nothing reads it\nyet; wiring it now is cheaper than retrofitting it.\n\nmiddleware.ts calls the shared createAuthMiddleware rather than duplicating it\nthe way doe-web does, and passes skipDefaultUrlCheck because the protected page\nserves `/`, which the helper otherwise exempts from auth outright. The matcher\nexcludes `error` so a real IDS failure renders instead of being redirected to\nthe sign-in page and read as an ordinary session expiry.\n\nNextAuth routes ten codes to the sign-in page and everything else, including\nAccessDenied, to pages.error -- so the procuration explanation lives on /error\nand the generic retry on /innskraning. Getting this backwards silently blames\nisland.is for a user who simply lacks procuration.\n\nNot yet in the deploy matrices, mirroring the existing exclusion of\ndirectorate-of-equality-partner-api: neither ECS service exists in the\nJafnrettisstofa terragrunt tree, and a name there fails `aws ecs wait\nservices-stable` on every deploy. Re-add both with that terragrunt PR.\n\nclientConfig.json and src/gen are not generated yet -- they need a running\ndirectorate-of-equality-api -- so the app does not build from this commit alone.\n\n* feat(doe): add the generated partner-web API contract\n\nclientConfig.json curled from directorate-of-equality-api''s Application swagger\ndocument, which is where the self-service key routes live:\n\n  /api/v1/application/api-keys        (company from the authenticated context)\n  /api/v1/application/api-keys/{id}\n\nNot the internal document doe-web generates from -- that one carries\n/api/v1/companies/{companyId}/api-keys, the ADMIN route behind AdminGuard, which\na company user cannot call. Generating from the wrong group would have produced\nan SDK whose only key methods are unusable here, and it would have typechecked.\n\nThe SDK generated from this exposes getApplicationApiKeys, issueApplicationApiKey\nand revokeApplicationApiKey. Nothing calls them yet; the screen is still a stub.\n\nGenerated against main at 2057c7b86, which narrowed three of the submit DTOs this\ndocument describes.\n\n* chore(shared): ignore the CLAUDE.md Next 16 generates per app\n\nNext 16.3.5 writes both AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md into an app directory on dev\nand build, controlled by next.config''s `agentRules`. AGENTS.md was already\nignored here; CLAUDE.md is the same generated pair and was missed, so it turns\nup untracked after anyone runs a web app. No CLAUDE.md is tracked anywhere in\nthis repo, so nothing is masked by this.\n\n* fix(doe): address review of the partner web scaffold\n\nEight findings from an external review, six fixed here and two decided.\n\nThe lockfile was never regenerated for the new workspace, so `yarn install\n--immutable` would have failed CI and nx could not resolve the project at all.\n\nA refused sign-in looped. Returning `false` rejects the NextAuth sign-in but\nleaves the IDS session alive, so retrying SSOs the same individual straight back\ninto the same refusal with no way out but clearing cookies. The sign-in now ends\nthe upstream session first, which means logoutHint comes back -- it was dropped\nearlier on the reasoning that the refusal path was gone. Only the allowlist was\ngone; this app still refuses, just for a different reason.\n\nEnding that session cost the error message, twice over. `signIn` returning a\nstring takes next-auth''s `typeof isAllowed === \"string\"` branch, which redirects\nwith no error param at all -- only `false` appends `?error=AccessDenied` -- and\nthe round trip through IDS does not preserve a query string either. So the\nperson who signed in without procuration was told island.is had failed. The\nreason now travels in a cookie the sign-in page reads and clears, the way\ndoe-web already does it.\n\nThat redirect returns to the bare origin rather than `${BASE_URL}/error`. IDS\nrefuses an unregistered post_logout_redirect_uri, so the subpath would have\nstranded the user on an island.is error page unless someone remembered to\nregister it; the origin lands on `/`, which now requires auth and forwards to\nthe sign-in page -- where the message is.\n\n`update-openapi-schema` ran curl and prettier under `parallel: true`, so\nprettier could format a half-downloaded file. Fixed in both DoE apps. The same\nrace exists in the four legal-gazette and official-journal webs and is left for\na separate change rather than widening this one across products.\n\nbindSdk and its five types were a byte-identical copy in both DoE webs; extracted\nto @dmr.is/trpc with both apps migrated. Its client parameter is generic because\nthe lib has no generated client of its own to import, and the body only forwards\nit.\n\nAlso dropped a session.invalid effect in the protected error boundary that\nduplicated the shared Header''s identical one rendered above it -- two concurrent\nOAuth redirects on the same tick -- and an unused publicProcedure whose guard the\ntRPC context had already made unreachable.\n\nDecided rather than fixed: the shared createAuthMiddleware refreshes on\naccess-token expiry only, while this app authenticates to the API with the id\ntoken. Constraining the new IDS client''s id_token lifetime is cheaper than an\nid-token branch in a helper five apps share; middleware.ts records why, and what\nto do if that configuration ever changes.\n\n* chore(doe): record why this app sends the id token alone\n\nReplaces a comment that asserted a constraint on the IDS client''s token\nlifetimes. Decoded tokens from the doe-partner-web dev client show the\nconstraint was unnecessary and the reasoning behind it wrong.\n\nBoth tokens are issued with the same 300s lifetime and the same exp, so the\nID token cannot expire before the access token and an ID-token branch in the\nshared middleware would be unreachable. An earlier commit added one; it is\ndropped.\n\nThe access token carries no identity at all -- scope, client_id, sub, sid,\nidp, acr, jti and nothing more. nationalId, name and subjectType are ID-token\nclaims. Since CompanyResourceGuard resolves the company from user.nationalId,\nand TokenJwtAuthGuard builds request.user from the FIRST token while lifting\nonly name and actor off the second, sending both tokens would leave\nnationalId undefined and 401 every request. Sending the ID token alone is not\ndrift from the two-token form the guard supports -- for this API it is the\nonly arrangement that works.\n\n* fix(doe): refuse an individual who signed in without procuration\n\nThe sign-in gate refused only an id token with no `nationalId` at all. An\nindividual signing in as themselves has one -- their own kennitala -- so they\npassed, while the comment above the gate, the comment on the error page and the\nIcelandic copy on both error screens all said that case was refused.\n\nNothing was exploitable: the router is a stub and nothing calls doe-api. It\nbecomes exploitable the moment key issuance is wired, because\nCompanyResourceGuard resolves -- and on annotated routes provisions -- a company\nfrom whatever `user.nationalId` it is handed, with no person-vs-company check of\nits own. A personal kennitala would mint a company row and a live API key.\n\nThe gate is now the id token''s `subjectType`: `legalEntity` for a session acting\nfor a company, `person` for someone signed in as themselves. Confirmed against\ndecoded tokens from this client rather than assumed.\n\nRefusing on a missing `actor` would have been wrong. A company can authenticate\nas itself, with no delegation and therefore no actor, and resolve-actor.ts is\nwritten to expect exactly that -- so that gate would have refused a legitimate\ncaller.\n\nThe predicate lives in its own module because importing authOptions pulls in\nnext-auth and jose, which jest cannot parse. That gave the app its first test\ntarget, modelled on legal-gazette-web''s; the spec covers the individual, the\ncompany via procuration, and the company acting for itself, and was checked\nagainst the old condition to confirm it fails there.\n\nAlso makes getServerClient''s token required. The optional form defaulted to a\nsession lookup and cast a possibly-undefined id token to string, so a caller\nwithout a session would have sent `Authorization: Bearer undefined` -- an auth\nbug at the API rather than a missing credential here.\n\n* feat(doe): authenticate to the API with the access token\n\nRequests the `@jafnretti.is/doe-partner-web` API resource scope and sends\n`session.accessToken` to directorate-of-equality-api instead of the id token.\n\nUntil now this app sent the id token, because DoE''s clients requested only\n`openid offline_access profile` and the resulting access token carried no\nidentity at all -- no nationalId, no actor -- while CompanyResourceGuard\nresolves a company from `user.nationalId`. Requesting an API resource scope\nchanges that: IDS then issues the access token for that resource and includes\nthe identity claims. Verified on decoded tokens from this client, on both an\nindividual and a procuration login: the access token gains `nationalId` (the\ncompany) alongside `actor.nationalId` (the human), which is what\nresolve-actor.ts needs to attribute an issued key to a person rather than the\ncompany.\n\nThe scope must be enabled for delegation on the IDS side. When it is not, a\nprocuration login still succeeds and IDS simply omits the scope and the claim,\nso nothing errors and the company silently goes missing -- that failure cost an\nhour to find, hence the comment in identityServerConfig.ts.\n\nThe id token is still issued, still stored on the session and still used: the\nsign-in gate reads its `subjectType`, the browser shows its `name`, and logout\nneeds it as `id_token_hint`. It is only no longer sent to the API.\n\nRefreshing on access-token expiry alone is therefore straightforwardly correct\nnow, rather than correct-by-coincidence because both tokens share a 300s\nlifetime.\n\nNote for deployment: the prod IDS client needs this scope, with delegation\nenabled, before the app is first deployed. Without it every request resolves no\ncompany.\n\n* fix(doe): correct what the sign-in and error pages say about AccessDenied\n\nBoth pages attributed `AccessDenied` to the refusal this app actually performs.\nIt never produces that code. The refusal returns a string from `signIn` so it\ncan end the IDS session first, and NextAuth attaches no `?error=` to a string\nreturn -- the reason travels in the `doe-partner.signin_error` cookie instead.\n`AccessDenied` comes only from the two `return false` branches, which are\nhandshake failures: no id_token, or a provider/access_token mismatch.\n\nThe comment on innskraning contradicted itself inside one block, stating the\nwrong attribution in its first paragraph and the right explanation in its\nsecond. Both were also stale twice over after the gate moved from `nationalId`\nto `subjectType`.\n\nThe error page is the part that mattered to a user: its AccessDenied copy said\nthe login was not connected to a company and told them to sign in with\nprocuration. Anyone reaching that page has a broken handshake, so it sent them\nafter a problem they do not have. The per-code branch is gone rather than\nreworded -- the real refusal cannot arrive there, so one message for a failed\nhandshake with the code shown for support is both correct and one less thing to\ndrift.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsphACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAT6kQADiG1jspgwy/ih9rsGr50ca+\nM1WQ3Waupy7tPU1V8BzcQoJt9P88g8qMDOwNyeT/KqNorHEYed8eY1iYjiR7nHNP\nhNmoPouZpcGJXE1gXnOkRT+zTiPGlaiRqa0TNc+kIeqZ/pQsqjeiVhp/4IfT5Jw2\nc5NflIdgamPhcbSmIhsEjiBmq0/LkJsxl6sFEEM+tSD7UL8VU7Si7as0Nsu6K9yE\n4B44yuqBKTwvkCUqZfJcsNRT7B6ZmWmFFbENgZ8OmseghCOWKxHOedrrm7NuE3nB\nouzlLZQrj65uhDDSyrvAd0UWesVCJI19b9SqmfRX3qwhji4G9yc21fRQRtzQyU3R\nbU6RekqhC61zvpBt9kAHCxDVT0amSYeusCFLy/anIgGJyoCxYnQ9GihMlp51pKgO\nKcBq9zYeXCV8fS7zE0bn5PGP1zolLXa1ab6xKSBaUSJWgW/Z/izKnU1fF3NDEoRy\n9zGUKXTDLLiivril4aIF6h9/5TVlMesPphJw7zU71+nTdldx1NhKAJjfgl6pWxTx\nTJuXEUraOSddJSx5seY/HfFI+Czu+KeQsekZE+sQQdnxxK1Dda/izOmk/kvX2H1J\nn/YndMFfwhMb4nAbTRwDnDl1Sto15WsUG/FogDg2cxJhlBLfjDIvSgNh4bOOTy55\nZOg5yuIxNFug7zb6/ZWn\n=Q9UT\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T15:01:20Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/2aff0f30315fd1cd4869207e171bbc2ffba7f41b","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKDJhZmYwZjMwMzE1ZmQxY2Q0ODY5MjA3ZTE3MWJiYzJmZmJhN2Y0MWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12","sha":"1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12"}],"sha":"2aff0f30315fd1cd4869207e171bbc2ffba7f41b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/2aff0f30315fd1cd4869207e171bbc2ffba7f41b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92530555?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/jonbjarnio","id":92530555,"login":"jonbjarnio","node_id":"U_kgDOBYPnew","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T13:59:44Z","email":"dev.jonbjarni@gmail.com","name":"Jón Bjarni Ólafsson"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T13:59:44Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(doe): take the equality plan as a .docx upload (#1536)\n\n* feat(doe): convert an uploaded equality plan to HTML\n\nPhase 2, first half: the conversion itself, tested in isolation. Wiring the\nmultipart route onto it is the next commit.\n\nMost employers keep the jafnréttisáætlun in Word, so asking a vendor for HTML\npushed a conversion problem onto the party least equipped to solve it. This\nmoves it to us. `mammoth` was already in the tree — transitively, via two\npackages — so this promotes it to a declared dependency of the partner API\nrather than introducing one; the lockfile change is two lines, since 1.12.0 is\nthe version already resolved.\n\n**Bytes decide the format, not the declared content type.** Multer takes\n`file.mimetype` from the `Content-Type` the caller put on the part, so it is a\nclaim rather than evidence. The three formats worth naming are sniffed by\nsignature — ZIP for a `.docx`, `%PDF-` for a PDF, the OLE2 header for a legacy\n`.doc` — and then the archive must actually contain `word/document.xml`, which\nis what separates a `.docx` from an `.xlsx`, a `.pptx` or a zip of files. All\nfour open with the same signature and none of the others convert to anything.\n\n**A PDF is refused rather than converted.** It carries no structure, only\npositioned glyphs, so there is no honest conversion into the markup the PDF\nrenderer and the reviewer''s editor expect — and mangled output is worse than a\nrefusal, because it reaches a reviewer looking like the employer''s own work. The\nlegacy `.doc` case is detected separately because it is the mistake a user is\nmost likely to make by accident and the fix is one Save As away; a generic\nrefusal would leave them guessing.\n\nMammoth''s own errors are not passed through. They describe the file''s internals,\nhelp no caller, and would publish a dependency''s wording as contract.\n\nIts warnings *are* kept, and returned alongside the HTML for the caller to log\nrather than to answer: conversion quality lands on Jafnréttisstofa''s reviewers,\nand an unmapped paragraph style is not something a payroll system can act on.\nThat is the only signal anything was lost.\n\nThe specs build real `.docx` archives with jszip instead of mocking mammoth.\nMocking it would assert our own mapping of our own stub, and the \"zip but not a\nWord document\" case would be untestable — that one only means something against\nan archive mammoth genuinely reads. Includes an Icelandic round-trip, since the\ncharacters surviving the conversion is the whole point for this audience.\n\n---\n\n**Noted while reading the existing upload pattern, not fixed here:**\n`FileTypeValidationPipe` (`libs/shared/pipelines`) has two defects. Its\nsingle-file path ignores the `mimetype` option it was constructed with and\ncompares against the repo-wide `ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES`, so\n`new FileTypeValidationPipe({ mimetype: ALLOWED_PDF_MIME_TYPES })` does not in\nfact restrict to PDFs — which is how `official-journal-admin-api`''s advert-PDF\nreplacement route uses it. And its array path is `files.some(...)`, so a batch\npasses if any one file is allowed. Both are out of scope for this branch.\n\n* feat(doe): take the equality plan as a .docx upload\n\nThe plan is a document an employer already has, almost always in Word. Asking\na payroll vendor for HTML put a conversion problem on the party least able to\nsolve it, so the file now arrives as it exists and the conversion happens here.\n\nPOST /partner/reports/equality becomes multipart/form-data with two parts: a\nJSON `payload` and the `.docx` `document`. Two parts rather than fifteen form\nfields so nested company and subsidiaries[] keep their JSON encoding and every\nexisting validator applies unchanged. One request rather than presign/PUT/submit,\nwhich is the right shape for a megabyte payroll extract and two wasted round\ntrips plus a half-filed state for a Word document.\n\nequalityReportContent comes off SubmitPartnerEqualityReportDto entirely, so the\nDTO is a plain OmitType again. That also settles two findings deferred from the\n#1532 review rather than patching them: the @ApiHTML base64 transform on a field\ndocumented as plain HTML, and a spec pinning that DTO''s key set — both concerned\na field this commit removes. A third is settled by construction: \"required\" via\n@ApiHTML never rejected \"\" and the empty string reached a service error naming\nequalityReportPdf, a field this contract does not publish.\n\nJsonPartPipe is where the care went. A multipart part is a string, so the global\nValidationPipe would have waved any text through and the document route would\nhave accepted bodies the JSON routes reject — \"previews clean, rejected at\nsubmit\" for a fourth time, which the plan for this phase named as the thing not\nto add. The pipe owns no rules of its own: it parses, then delegates to a real\nValidationPipe built from PARTNER_VALIDATION_OPTIONS, now shared with bootstrap.\nwhitelist and forbidNonWhitelisted therefore apply inside the part exactly as\nthey do on a JSON body, and a spec pins that rather than trusting it.\n\nThe conversion sits in PartnerSubmissionService, not the shared service: it is\nchannel translation, and ApplicationService receives equalityReportContent\nexactly as the portal sends it, so nothing downstream can tell the channels\napart. Conversion warnings are logged, never returned — an unmapped paragraph\nstyle is not something a payroll system can act on, and a warning on a 201\ninvites a vendor to treat a filed report as failed.\n\nMulter''s file cap is per route, from MAX_EQUALITY_DOCUMENT_BYTES, and memory\nstorage keeps nothing on disk. The content checks stay in the converter, which\nsniffs magic bytes rather than trusting the filename or declared content type.\n\nGuide A3 rewritten around the two parts, with a curl example, the .pdf/.doc\nrefusals, and the 413. Status codes and quick reference updated.\n\n* docs(doe): record phase 2 as shipped\n\nThe converter and the route, what JsonPartPipe exists to prevent, and the three\n#1532 findings this phase settles by removing the field they were about.\n\nAlso corrects the branch stack: phase 2 was restacked from e86fb9de1 onto\n5ea4e8e0 before the route work, because it edits two files phase 1 changed after\nthat point. The recorded rebase command moves with it.\n\nThe calibration run is the one thing left on the phase, and it needs real\ndocuments rather than a test.\n\n* fix(doe): close the rest of the providerId read-back gap\n\nThe `/` bound was one value short of its own reasoning. Three more are rewritten\nby a URL before routing, so they file and then 404 exactly as a slash does:\n\n- `\\` — WHATWG URL parsing rewrites it to `/`, so it is the slash case in any\n  client following the URL standard.\n- `.` and `..` as the whole segment — RFC 3986 dot-segment removal is part of\n  URL resolution, applied by browsers, by curl without --path-as-is, and at\n  proxies. `…/reports/..` resolves to the parent and `…/reports/.` to the\n  collection, so neither reaches the handler.\n\nThe rule is anchored rather than a ban on the character, because a dot inside an\nid is one ordinary segment: `2026.Q1.042` and `...` still file and read back, and\nthe specs pin that alongside the refusals so this cannot drift into a charset\nallowlist.\n\nSecond half: the read path did not trim while the write path did, so a vendor\nwhose id reached us with a trailing space filed under the trimmed form and then\ngot a 404 fetching the form it holds. Both sides now share normaliseProviderId\nrather than calling .trim() in two places. ProviderIdParamPipe deliberately does\nnot re-check the pattern — those values cannot arrive as a path segment, so the\ncheck would be dead code that reads like a safeguard.\n\nGuide: the bound is stated in full, the trim is documented as applying to reads\ntoo, and the example that demonstrated it now differs between its two sides.\n\nAll three raised in the re-review of #1532.\n\n* docs(doe): flatten the stack and record what the re-review left\n\n#1532 merged, so phase 2 sits on main and the branch-stack section is history\nrather than instructions — kept for the lesson that cost a restack: a stacked\nbranch wants its parent''s tip, not whatever was current when it was created.\n\nAlso records the three re-review items still open, so the next session does not\nhave to re-read the PR thread to find them.\n\n* fix(doe): match the existing mammoth range\n\nThe converter commit pinned mammoth to 1.12.0 while @dmr.is/ojoi-modules\nalready declared ^1.8.0, and yarn''s rule 1 requires two workspaces declaring\none dependency to use identical ranges. `yarn constraints --fix` reports both\ndirections, since each workspace would update to the other''s range, so this\nneeded a deliberate pick rather than an autofix.\n\nTook the existing range. The resolution is 1.12.0 either way — that is what\n^1.8.0 already installed, which is how the converter could be written against\nit before anything declared it directly — and changing Official Journal''s\ndependency policy from inside a DoE pull request is not this PR''s call.\n\nThe lockfile collapses to a single descriptor as a result.\n\n* fix(doe): bound what an uploaded .docx can cost us\n\nThe multipart limits bounded the request and nothing else, and a .docx is a ZIP:\nthe ratio between what a caller uploads and what we allocate reading it is\ntheirs to choose. The review measured 144KB inflating to 200MB of HTML, and\n497KB to 94MB after 40 seconds of blocking CPU at 5GB RSS — all far under the\n10MB upload cap, on a public route, synchronously on the event loop. The\ncompressed cap was never a proxy for the inflated one.\n\nThree bounds now, because each catches what the others cannot:\n\n- MAX_INFLATED_DOCUMENT_BYTES reads the archive''s declared uncompressed sizes\n  from the central directory and refuses before inflating anything. This is the\n  discipline equality-content.ts already applies to a base64 body, and for the\n  same stated reason: a caller must not be able to make the API allocate past\n  the cap in order to discover it is past the cap.\n- MAX_CONVERTED_HTML_BYTES is measured on the result, because a ZIP header is\n  the archive''s own claim about itself. It also bounds what reaches storage:\n  that HTML lands in TEXT and is returned on every report read.\n- Images are no longer inlined. Mammoth defaults to base64 data URIs, so a plan\n  with photographs was re-encoding them into the stored HTML at four thirds of\n  their size — the single largest source of the amplification above, and a\n  permanent cost on every read.\n\nDropping the images also makes a promise true that could not fire before. The\nguide says a scanned plan is refused because a reviewer needs text; with images\ninlined, a page of scans converted to a page of <img> and read as non-empty. The\nempty-document gate now measures text content rather than markup length.\n\nSecond blocking finding: FileInterceptor''s limits set fileSize and files, which\nleft busboy''s fields and parts at Infinity — ten thousand text fields were\naccepted under a valid key. All four are set now, and fieldSize comes from\nMAX_PARTNER_JSON_BYTES, shared with express.json() so the JSON part of a\nmultipart submission gets the same bound as a JSON body on any other route. At\nbusboy''s 1MB default a large group submission would have failed with \"Field\nvalue too long\" while the route''s description promised it validates like any\nother body.\n\nAlso from the review: the multipart schema wraps its $ref in allOf, since\nOpenAPI 3.0 requires a $ref''s siblings to be ignored and the dropped sibling was\nthe sentence stating the strict-validation contract; the published size used a\nbinary divisor against a decimal constant and read 9.5367431640625MB; jszip and\n@types/multer were resolving by hoisting and are now declared; and the plan cited\ntwo commit hashes the rebase had rewritten.\n\nNew specs build a real deflated bomb and a real image-only .docx rather than\nasserting against mocks, and pin that the bomb is a fraction of the upload cap —\nif that stops holding, the fixture stopped being a bomb and the test stopped\ntesting anything. ProviderIdParamPipe gains the spec it shipped without, and the\nJSON part pipe now also runs against the real DTO rather than only a local one.\n\n* test(doe): stop the zip-bomb fixture timing out on CI\n\nThe fixture builds tens of megabytes and deflates it, which took 11.6s on a CI\nrunner against Jest''s 5s default — green locally, red in CI.\n\nBuilt once in beforeAll now rather than per test, at compression level 1 rather\nthan 9, and sized just past the bound instead of double it. The ratio is\nenormous at any level on a megabyte of one repeated character, so level 9 was\nbuying nothing but CPU. 0.9s locally.\n\n* fix(doe): bound the document by what it inflates to, not what it claims\n\nTwo blockers from the re-review, both mine, both invisible to CI.\n\nparts: 2 rejected every valid upload. Busboy raises the limit when the counter\n*reaches* it — `if (++parts === partsLimit)` — so parts: N permits N−1, and a\nlegitimate two-part request died with \"Too many parts\". parts: 3 restores it and\nloses nothing: fields: 1 is what refuses a flood, and it is checked before the\ncounter moves. Verified against busboy''s source rather than inferred.\n\nThe inflation bound was set above what the process survives, and trusted a\nheader to enforce it. Both halves were wrong:\n\n- 40MB was picked as a ratio against the upload cap, which measured the wrong\n  thing. Mammoth does not hold the XML — it builds a DOM and then an HTML\n  string, and the peak runs tens of times the XML. 38MB of declared XML reached\n  3.6GB RSS, about 96x, from a 109KB archive: a fatal OOM on the 1152MB task,\n  not a 400. The bound is now 4MB, derived from the heap rather than from the\n  upload cap, which keeps the peak near a third of it and is still several\n  hundred pages of an equality plan.\n- Reading the size from the central directory trusts a number the attacker\n  writes. An archive that understates itself passed the gate and inflated\n  anyway, and the cost was paid before anything noticed the mismatch. The\n  document part is now measured as it inflates, over JSZip''s stream, with the\n  stream paused the moment the count goes over — the refusal happens instead of\n  the allocation rather than after it.\n\nThe declared-size check stays as a cheap pre-filter for the honest-but-enormous,\ndemoted to what it actually is. The output check stays as a backstop and is now\nexported and tested directly, because with the input bounded at 4MB of\nWordprocessingML nothing that converts can reach it, and a bound no fixture can\nexercise is a bound that quietly stops working.\n\nAlso from the review:\n\n- The image fix had no teeth. Removing MAMMOTH_OPTIONS left the suite green,\n  because the only fixture with an image is refused for having no text either\n  way. There is now a plan with text *and* a picture, asserting the text\n  survives and the image bytes do not — mutation-tested, it fails without the\n  option.\n- encoding.payload.contentType is set, which @ApiBody supports directly, so a\n  generated client sends the JSON part as JSON instead of guessing \"file\" and\n  being refused by multer. type: ''object'' sits alongside the allOf for\n  generators that do not resolve it.\n- Five hashes in the plan pointed at pre-squash commits from #1532 that stopped\n  resolving for anyone else the moment it merged — they survive only in a local\n  object store. All now cite 2057c7b8. The caveat above them says to check with\n  `merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main`, not `cat-file -e`, which is the check\n  that would have caught this.\n- The guide said the 4MB bound refuses \"unread\", which was true of the header\n  gate and not of the output one.\n\n* fix(doe): budget the whole archive, not one entry in it\n\nThe streamed bound was the right mechanism pointed at the wrong scope. It\nguarded word/document.xml, and mammoth also reads [Content_Types].xml, both\n_rels parts, styles, numbering, footnotes, endnotes and comments — each an\nunconditional full inflate. Eight of the nine parts were behind nothing but the\ndeclared-size pass, whose own docblock says why that is not a defence: the\nheader is the caller''s to write. A 6.8MB upload with one forged entry reached\n2167MB RSS against a 1536MB task, which is a container kill rather than a 400.\n\nIt is now one budget spent across every part, in order, measured as each\ninflates and refused the moment the running total goes over. Archive-wide rather\nthan per entry, because nine parts each just under a per-entry cap would be nine\ntimes the memory one of them is allowed.\n\nBudgeting the archive rather than enumerating mammoth''s reads is deliberate.\nWhich parts it opens is its business and can change with a version bump, so a\nlist of them here would be a defence that silently stops covering what it names.\nThe pass runs before convertToHtml, which is what covers the parts read first —\n[Content_Types].xml and the _rels are opened before anything structural is\nvalidated, so a guard on the document alone was already too late.\n\nword/media is the one exemption, and it earns it: convertImage answers without\nreading the image part, so those bytes are never inflated by anything. Counting\nthem here would spend the budget on the one kind of part a real plan\nlegitimately fills, to protect against an attack that costs nothing because\nnothing opens it.\n\nThree specs, all mutation-checked against reverting the scope: a bomb in\nstyles.xml declared honestly and under the declared filter, so only the budget\ncan refuse it; three parts each half the budget, which must still add up to a\nrefusal; and an oversized image, which must not.","tree":{"sha":"903a1e4e32c079fd116d626881bfb46ee2146c63","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/903a1e4e32c079fd116d626881bfb46ee2146c63"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12","verification":{"payload":"tree 903a1e4e32c079fd116d626881bfb46ee2146c63\nparent 2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c\nauthor Jón Bjarni Ólafsson <dev.jonbjarni@gmail.com> 1790085584 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790085584 +0000\n\nfeat(doe): take the equality plan as a .docx upload (#1536)\n\n* feat(doe): convert an uploaded equality plan to HTML\n\nPhase 2, first half: the conversion itself, tested in isolation. Wiring the\nmultipart route onto it is the next commit.\n\nMost employers keep the jafnréttisáætlun in Word, so asking a vendor for HTML\npushed a conversion problem onto the party least equipped to solve it. This\nmoves it to us. `mammoth` was already in the tree — transitively, via two\npackages — so this promotes it to a declared dependency of the partner API\nrather than introducing one; the lockfile change is two lines, since 1.12.0 is\nthe version already resolved.\n\n**Bytes decide the format, not the declared content type.** Multer takes\n`file.mimetype` from the `Content-Type` the caller put on the part, so it is a\nclaim rather than evidence. The three formats worth naming are sniffed by\nsignature — ZIP for a `.docx`, `%PDF-` for a PDF, the OLE2 header for a legacy\n`.doc` — and then the archive must actually contain `word/document.xml`, which\nis what separates a `.docx` from an `.xlsx`, a `.pptx` or a zip of files. All\nfour open with the same signature and none of the others convert to anything.\n\n**A PDF is refused rather than converted.** It carries no structure, only\npositioned glyphs, so there is no honest conversion into the markup the PDF\nrenderer and the reviewer''s editor expect — and mangled output is worse than a\nrefusal, because it reaches a reviewer looking like the employer''s own work. The\nlegacy `.doc` case is detected separately because it is the mistake a user is\nmost likely to make by accident and the fix is one Save As away; a generic\nrefusal would leave them guessing.\n\nMammoth''s own errors are not passed through. They describe the file''s internals,\nhelp no caller, and would publish a dependency''s wording as contract.\n\nIts warnings *are* kept, and returned alongside the HTML for the caller to log\nrather than to answer: conversion quality lands on Jafnréttisstofa''s reviewers,\nand an unmapped paragraph style is not something a payroll system can act on.\nThat is the only signal anything was lost.\n\nThe specs build real `.docx` archives with jszip instead of mocking mammoth.\nMocking it would assert our own mapping of our own stub, and the \"zip but not a\nWord document\" case would be untestable — that one only means something against\nan archive mammoth genuinely reads. Includes an Icelandic round-trip, since the\ncharacters surviving the conversion is the whole point for this audience.\n\n---\n\n**Noted while reading the existing upload pattern, not fixed here:**\n`FileTypeValidationPipe` (`libs/shared/pipelines`) has two defects. Its\nsingle-file path ignores the `mimetype` option it was constructed with and\ncompares against the repo-wide `ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES`, so\n`new FileTypeValidationPipe({ mimetype: ALLOWED_PDF_MIME_TYPES })` does not in\nfact restrict to PDFs — which is how `official-journal-admin-api`''s advert-PDF\nreplacement route uses it. And its array path is `files.some(...)`, so a batch\npasses if any one file is allowed. Both are out of scope for this branch.\n\n* feat(doe): take the equality plan as a .docx upload\n\nThe plan is a document an employer already has, almost always in Word. Asking\na payroll vendor for HTML put a conversion problem on the party least able to\nsolve it, so the file now arrives as it exists and the conversion happens here.\n\nPOST /partner/reports/equality becomes multipart/form-data with two parts: a\nJSON `payload` and the `.docx` `document`. Two parts rather than fifteen form\nfields so nested company and subsidiaries[] keep their JSON encoding and every\nexisting validator applies unchanged. One request rather than presign/PUT/submit,\nwhich is the right shape for a megabyte payroll extract and two wasted round\ntrips plus a half-filed state for a Word document.\n\nequalityReportContent comes off SubmitPartnerEqualityReportDto entirely, so the\nDTO is a plain OmitType again. That also settles two findings deferred from the\n#1532 review rather than patching them: the @ApiHTML base64 transform on a field\ndocumented as plain HTML, and a spec pinning that DTO''s key set — both concerned\na field this commit removes. A third is settled by construction: \"required\" via\n@ApiHTML never rejected \"\" and the empty string reached a service error naming\nequalityReportPdf, a field this contract does not publish.\n\nJsonPartPipe is where the care went. A multipart part is a string, so the global\nValidationPipe would have waved any text through and the document route would\nhave accepted bodies the JSON routes reject — \"previews clean, rejected at\nsubmit\" for a fourth time, which the plan for this phase named as the thing not\nto add. The pipe owns no rules of its own: it parses, then delegates to a real\nValidationPipe built from PARTNER_VALIDATION_OPTIONS, now shared with bootstrap.\nwhitelist and forbidNonWhitelisted therefore apply inside the part exactly as\nthey do on a JSON body, and a spec pins that rather than trusting it.\n\nThe conversion sits in PartnerSubmissionService, not the shared service: it is\nchannel translation, and ApplicationService receives equalityReportContent\nexactly as the portal sends it, so nothing downstream can tell the channels\napart. Conversion warnings are logged, never returned — an unmapped paragraph\nstyle is not something a payroll system can act on, and a warning on a 201\ninvites a vendor to treat a filed report as failed.\n\nMulter''s file cap is per route, from MAX_EQUALITY_DOCUMENT_BYTES, and memory\nstorage keeps nothing on disk. The content checks stay in the converter, which\nsniffs magic bytes rather than trusting the filename or declared content type.\n\nGuide A3 rewritten around the two parts, with a curl example, the .pdf/.doc\nrefusals, and the 413. Status codes and quick reference updated.\n\n* docs(doe): record phase 2 as shipped\n\nThe converter and the route, what JsonPartPipe exists to prevent, and the three\n#1532 findings this phase settles by removing the field they were about.\n\nAlso corrects the branch stack: phase 2 was restacked from e86fb9de1 onto\n5ea4e8e0 before the route work, because it edits two files phase 1 changed after\nthat point. The recorded rebase command moves with it.\n\nThe calibration run is the one thing left on the phase, and it needs real\ndocuments rather than a test.\n\n* fix(doe): close the rest of the providerId read-back gap\n\nThe `/` bound was one value short of its own reasoning. Three more are rewritten\nby a URL before routing, so they file and then 404 exactly as a slash does:\n\n- `\\` — WHATWG URL parsing rewrites it to `/`, so it is the slash case in any\n  client following the URL standard.\n- `.` and `..` as the whole segment — RFC 3986 dot-segment removal is part of\n  URL resolution, applied by browsers, by curl without --path-as-is, and at\n  proxies. `…/reports/..` resolves to the parent and `…/reports/.` to the\n  collection, so neither reaches the handler.\n\nThe rule is anchored rather than a ban on the character, because a dot inside an\nid is one ordinary segment: `2026.Q1.042` and `...` still file and read back, and\nthe specs pin that alongside the refusals so this cannot drift into a charset\nallowlist.\n\nSecond half: the read path did not trim while the write path did, so a vendor\nwhose id reached us with a trailing space filed under the trimmed form and then\ngot a 404 fetching the form it holds. Both sides now share normaliseProviderId\nrather than calling .trim() in two places. ProviderIdParamPipe deliberately does\nnot re-check the pattern — those values cannot arrive as a path segment, so the\ncheck would be dead code that reads like a safeguard.\n\nGuide: the bound is stated in full, the trim is documented as applying to reads\ntoo, and the example that demonstrated it now differs between its two sides.\n\nAll three raised in the re-review of #1532.\n\n* docs(doe): flatten the stack and record what the re-review left\n\n#1532 merged, so phase 2 sits on main and the branch-stack section is history\nrather than instructions — kept for the lesson that cost a restack: a stacked\nbranch wants its parent''s tip, not whatever was current when it was created.\n\nAlso records the three re-review items still open, so the next session does not\nhave to re-read the PR thread to find them.\n\n* fix(doe): match the existing mammoth range\n\nThe converter commit pinned mammoth to 1.12.0 while @dmr.is/ojoi-modules\nalready declared ^1.8.0, and yarn''s rule 1 requires two workspaces declaring\none dependency to use identical ranges. `yarn constraints --fix` reports both\ndirections, since each workspace would update to the other''s range, so this\nneeded a deliberate pick rather than an autofix.\n\nTook the existing range. The resolution is 1.12.0 either way — that is what\n^1.8.0 already installed, which is how the converter could be written against\nit before anything declared it directly — and changing Official Journal''s\ndependency policy from inside a DoE pull request is not this PR''s call.\n\nThe lockfile collapses to a single descriptor as a result.\n\n* fix(doe): bound what an uploaded .docx can cost us\n\nThe multipart limits bounded the request and nothing else, and a .docx is a ZIP:\nthe ratio between what a caller uploads and what we allocate reading it is\ntheirs to choose. The review measured 144KB inflating to 200MB of HTML, and\n497KB to 94MB after 40 seconds of blocking CPU at 5GB RSS — all far under the\n10MB upload cap, on a public route, synchronously on the event loop. The\ncompressed cap was never a proxy for the inflated one.\n\nThree bounds now, because each catches what the others cannot:\n\n- MAX_INFLATED_DOCUMENT_BYTES reads the archive''s declared uncompressed sizes\n  from the central directory and refuses before inflating anything. This is the\n  discipline equality-content.ts already applies to a base64 body, and for the\n  same stated reason: a caller must not be able to make the API allocate past\n  the cap in order to discover it is past the cap.\n- MAX_CONVERTED_HTML_BYTES is measured on the result, because a ZIP header is\n  the archive''s own claim about itself. It also bounds what reaches storage:\n  that HTML lands in TEXT and is returned on every report read.\n- Images are no longer inlined. Mammoth defaults to base64 data URIs, so a plan\n  with photographs was re-encoding them into the stored HTML at four thirds of\n  their size — the single largest source of the amplification above, and a\n  permanent cost on every read.\n\nDropping the images also makes a promise true that could not fire before. The\nguide says a scanned plan is refused because a reviewer needs text; with images\ninlined, a page of scans converted to a page of <img> and read as non-empty. The\nempty-document gate now measures text content rather than markup length.\n\nSecond blocking finding: FileInterceptor''s limits set fileSize and files, which\nleft busboy''s fields and parts at Infinity — ten thousand text fields were\naccepted under a valid key. All four are set now, and fieldSize comes from\nMAX_PARTNER_JSON_BYTES, shared with express.json() so the JSON part of a\nmultipart submission gets the same bound as a JSON body on any other route. At\nbusboy''s 1MB default a large group submission would have failed with \"Field\nvalue too long\" while the route''s description promised it validates like any\nother body.\n\nAlso from the review: the multipart schema wraps its $ref in allOf, since\nOpenAPI 3.0 requires a $ref''s siblings to be ignored and the dropped sibling was\nthe sentence stating the strict-validation contract; the published size used a\nbinary divisor against a decimal constant and read 9.5367431640625MB; jszip and\n@types/multer were resolving by hoisting and are now declared; and the plan cited\ntwo commit hashes the rebase had rewritten.\n\nNew specs build a real deflated bomb and a real image-only .docx rather than\nasserting against mocks, and pin that the bomb is a fraction of the upload cap —\nif that stops holding, the fixture stopped being a bomb and the test stopped\ntesting anything. ProviderIdParamPipe gains the spec it shipped without, and the\nJSON part pipe now also runs against the real DTO rather than only a local one.\n\n* test(doe): stop the zip-bomb fixture timing out on CI\n\nThe fixture builds tens of megabytes and deflates it, which took 11.6s on a CI\nrunner against Jest''s 5s default — green locally, red in CI.\n\nBuilt once in beforeAll now rather than per test, at compression level 1 rather\nthan 9, and sized just past the bound instead of double it. The ratio is\nenormous at any level on a megabyte of one repeated character, so level 9 was\nbuying nothing but CPU. 0.9s locally.\n\n* fix(doe): bound the document by what it inflates to, not what it claims\n\nTwo blockers from the re-review, both mine, both invisible to CI.\n\nparts: 2 rejected every valid upload. Busboy raises the limit when the counter\n*reaches* it — `if (++parts === partsLimit)` — so parts: N permits N−1, and a\nlegitimate two-part request died with \"Too many parts\". parts: 3 restores it and\nloses nothing: fields: 1 is what refuses a flood, and it is checked before the\ncounter moves. Verified against busboy''s source rather than inferred.\n\nThe inflation bound was set above what the process survives, and trusted a\nheader to enforce it. Both halves were wrong:\n\n- 40MB was picked as a ratio against the upload cap, which measured the wrong\n  thing. Mammoth does not hold the XML — it builds a DOM and then an HTML\n  string, and the peak runs tens of times the XML. 38MB of declared XML reached\n  3.6GB RSS, about 96x, from a 109KB archive: a fatal OOM on the 1152MB task,\n  not a 400. The bound is now 4MB, derived from the heap rather than from the\n  upload cap, which keeps the peak near a third of it and is still several\n  hundred pages of an equality plan.\n- Reading the size from the central directory trusts a number the attacker\n  writes. An archive that understates itself passed the gate and inflated\n  anyway, and the cost was paid before anything noticed the mismatch. The\n  document part is now measured as it inflates, over JSZip''s stream, with the\n  stream paused the moment the count goes over — the refusal happens instead of\n  the allocation rather than after it.\n\nThe declared-size check stays as a cheap pre-filter for the honest-but-enormous,\ndemoted to what it actually is. The output check stays as a backstop and is now\nexported and tested directly, because with the input bounded at 4MB of\nWordprocessingML nothing that converts can reach it, and a bound no fixture can\nexercise is a bound that quietly stops working.\n\nAlso from the review:\n\n- The image fix had no teeth. Removing MAMMOTH_OPTIONS left the suite green,\n  because the only fixture with an image is refused for having no text either\n  way. There is now a plan with text *and* a picture, asserting the text\n  survives and the image bytes do not — mutation-tested, it fails without the\n  option.\n- encoding.payload.contentType is set, which @ApiBody supports directly, so a\n  generated client sends the JSON part as JSON instead of guessing \"file\" and\n  being refused by multer. type: ''object'' sits alongside the allOf for\n  generators that do not resolve it.\n- Five hashes in the plan pointed at pre-squash commits from #1532 that stopped\n  resolving for anyone else the moment it merged — they survive only in a local\n  object store. All now cite 2057c7b8. The caveat above them says to check with\n  `merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main`, not `cat-file -e`, which is the check\n  that would have caught this.\n- The guide said the 4MB bound refuses \"unread\", which was true of the header\n  gate and not of the output one.\n\n* fix(doe): budget the whole archive, not one entry in it\n\nThe streamed bound was the right mechanism pointed at the wrong scope. It\nguarded word/document.xml, and mammoth also reads [Content_Types].xml, both\n_rels parts, styles, numbering, footnotes, endnotes and comments — each an\nunconditional full inflate. Eight of the nine parts were behind nothing but the\ndeclared-size pass, whose own docblock says why that is not a defence: the\nheader is the caller''s to write. A 6.8MB upload with one forged entry reached\n2167MB RSS against a 1536MB task, which is a container kill rather than a 400.\n\nIt is now one budget spent across every part, in order, measured as each\ninflates and refused the moment the running total goes over. Archive-wide rather\nthan per entry, because nine parts each just under a per-entry cap would be nine\ntimes the memory one of them is allowed.\n\nBudgeting the archive rather than enumerating mammoth''s reads is deliberate.\nWhich parts it opens is its business and can change with a version bump, so a\nlist of them here would be a defence that silently stops covering what it names.\nThe pass runs before convertToHtml, which is what covers the parts read first —\n[Content_Types].xml and the _rels are opened before anything structural is\nvalidated, so a guard on the document alone was already too late.\n\nword/media is the one exemption, and it earns it: convertImage answers without\nreading the image part, so those bytes are never inflated by anything. Counting\nthem here would spend the budget on the one kind of part a real plan\nlegitimately fills, to protect against an attack that costs nothing because\nnothing opens it.\n\nThree specs, all mutation-checked against reverting the scope: a bomb in\nstyles.xml declared honestly and under the declared filter, so only the budget\ncan refuse it; three parts each half the budget, which must still add up to a\nrefusal; and an oversized image, which must not.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsonQCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAjV0QADgJ9Zqu7zx/WY/KMGm4S3De\nbDxctkDrkT9sy/VbQ11iYlCc/1gYRgNilOkdSJWK8Avvyy9begqG2XaotxRGuPfn\nmdmmapGbniJIFNw+y3qt5Bz5JsZgxJYccKjGiazgo1LrWVx1ruzvFt3q8UNV+K2Q\npizKUl+1BK3kkzXuMKUvpwHxhPmszAbPo9wjxl+xP0U8hqZgpY9kmfIB/rkcYvSk\nWYYa0AhlTCRIXL1ZbWn/AjEJkGcMs6pOqDxGJCK+lv5b7T78fMDjBi3ayZLz8sAF\naWnP4TDqdhyKuuz/yqYBsQijrBdqgprW6bDTyzUsRd246wCSsVora8vreL5OKhFx\nen5RQqnis3V3XX1LC3tl7ouSKBdoxhxP33+KVhN/c+ZpIPl/o5tcezyXPyxM2y1W\naUU7tMZU891LMEPjpv1u752iaME0H24pqCAg7B/aazy1NireibO31Bo2dx3hWgrI\nsWLoFT3ME3uO5HppGbtCcg9b2/gTt2Yy82C+Pw+uYmcWH6+6Vl8OE9AXhrS1UxE0\nZGp3gy2HsdkqqFIT3ac9kvHrjXge+nNmxZV7VH0nwvufnslCFUxUXBHhwY9EVfKr\nVDegDaLKQ0w5skrv73XAUU+YrwWwma6W3OUgnefMWkPJO/UEPSE+IQf/WmkzVOT/\nMLOrS5llaBvVkWa+f+Xp\n=bE4y\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T13:59:45Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKDFlNDdhYzlmYWVmNmU2YTk4M2IwMmI0Mzk4ZTcxYzJkMTM3OWNhMTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c","sha":"2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c"}],"sha":"1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/1e47ac9faef6e6a983b02b4398e71c2d1379ca12"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92530555?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/jonbjarnio","id":92530555,"login":"jonbjarnio","node_id":"U_kgDOBYPnew","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:54:32Z","email":"dev.jonbjarni@gmail.com","name":"Jón Bjarni Ólafsson"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:54:32Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(doe): narrow the partner API contract (#1532)\n\n* fix(doe): stop requiring a UUID of the caller''s own submission id\n\n`providerId` is the identifier the *caller* knows a submission by, and it was\n`@ApiUUID()` on both submit DTOs. Nothing reads it as a UUID — it is compared\nfor equality and nothing else — so the format only ever cost a vendor whose ids\nare not UUIDs a mapping table kept purely to satisfy us. The guide published\n`2026-Q1-042` as the shape of a vendor''s own id in one section and documented\nthe `400` that rejected it in another.\n\nNow `@ApiString({ minLength: 1, maxLength: 256 })`. What the field needs is to\nexist and to be bounded, because it reaches the partial unique index\n`report_provider_type_provider_id_unique_idx`. The safety was never the format:\nuniqueness is `(provider_type, provider_id)`, and the partner channel prefixes\nthe authenticated company''s kennitala from the verified API key, so a vendor can\nneither collide with another employer nor file under one. 256 matches the only\nstring bound this library already had, in `import-key.dto.ts`.\n\nOne change covers both channels: `SubmitPartnerSalaryReportDto` inherits the\nfield through `OmitType(SubmitSalaryReportDto)` and the partner controller takes\n`SubmitEqualityReportDto` directly.\n\nNothing else moves. It is a widening, so every payload accepted before is still\naccepted; island.is keeps sending its application UUID. `provider_id` is already\n`TEXT` and the index is untyped, so there is no migration. The committed\nisland.is client carries `providerId` as a plain `{\"type\": \"string\"}` with no\n`format: uuid`, so the generated type does not change and no regen is needed.\n\n`CreateDraftReportDto.providerId` deliberately stays a UUID: only\n`directorate-of-equality-api` exposes it, the partner API has no draft routes,\nand on that path the value really is the island.is application''s own UUID.\n\nAdds `provider-id.spec.ts`, which had no precedent for these DTOs — the bounds\nwere pinned by nothing. It runs both classes through class-validator and asserts\nthe loosening directly, so restoring the UUID rule fails CI rather than\nsurfacing as a vendor''s 400: a UUID, `2026-Q1-042`, an id containing a colon\n(which `toClientProviderId` already round-trips, since it strips the namespace\nby prefix rather than splitting) and a single character all pass, while empty,\nover-bound and non-string values are refused.\n\n* docs(doe): plan the partner API contract narrowing\n\nToday''s design work, in the form the repo tracks. Four phases, ordered so the\ncheap contract fixes ship without waiting on the migration: format and route\nremovals first, then the equality document upload, then moving outlier detection\ninto the submit, then repurposing the analysis route as a playground.\n\nThe three findings worth having in the repo rather than a chat log:\n\n**`mammoth@1.12.0` is already installed**, pulled in transitively by two\npackages. Earlier notes recorded that nothing here converts `.docx` to HTML and\nused that to defer the whole document question. It converts; the work is\npromoting a transitive dependency to a direct one.\n\n**The outlier round trip is a human one** — a vendor has to ask the employer why\ntwo equally-scored people are paid differently, which is days. Every shape that\nassumed it fits inside one request was wrong, and that is why detection moves\ninto the submission rather than sitting in front of it. It also disposes of the\npayload being sent twice, and of the warning that editing anything between the\ntwo calls reshuffles the detected set.\n\n**`POSTPONED` has no exit on this channel**, so we currently publish an option\nthat strands whoever takes it. `PUT .../outliers` closes it.\n\nEach phase carries its blocked decisions inline rather than in a separate list,\nbecause every one of them changes what gets written: whether a pending report is\nreplaceable or blocking decides the sibling policy, and whether the document is\nthe only accepted form decides if `equalityReportContent` survives at all.\n\nSecurity notes are specific to what these phases add — a public upload route\nwants magic-byte checks and a per-route multer cap rather than trust in a\nfilename, and the two new outlier routes want tenant-isolation specs, since a\ncredential that can leave a report pending can now also resolve one.\n\n`.github/planning/doe/` is new; shared was the only product with a folder.\n\n* docs(doe): settle four decisions in the partner API contract plan\n\n**A submission with unexplained outliers goes straight to `POSTPONED`.** The\nplan previously proposed a distinct pending state plus an explicit postpone\nroute, so nothing could land postponed by accident. Reversed, and the reversal\nis the better answer: island.is has always filed `POSTPONED` with one default\nempty group over every detected outlier, so a new state would have diverged the\ntwo channels for no gain — and it would have cost an enum migration, a\nreviewer-queue exclusion, compliance changes and its own reaper. `POSTPONED`\nalready means \"filed with unexplained outliers, a reviewer cannot pick it up\".\nPhase 3 now needs no schema change at all, and `POST .../postpone` is dropped\nsince omitting groups *is* the postpone.\n\n**The `409` on a prior `POSTPONED` report stays.** Recorded with the cost\nattached rather than as a bare decision, because it is now reachable by ordinary\nuse: a vendor who submits, lands `POSTPONED`, then finds a payroll error cannot\nre-submit — the only way out is explaining outliers in a payload they know is\nwrong. Making it replaceable was rejected because the sibling policy is shared\nwith the channel employers already use, and making it replaceable only for\n`provider_type = OTHER` is the one-policy-two-meanings divergence that keeps\nbiting here. If support load proves real, the fix is a withdraw route on a\nreport this channel filed, not a branch in the policy.\n\n**`company.nationalId` comes out of the body.** It is validated to equal the\nauthenticated company, so it can only ever hold one value. Noted what this gives\nup: once vendor clients land it would have cross-checked the snapshot against\nthe `X-Company-National-Id` header. If that matters it belongs in the delegation\nguard, not in a field the caller supplies.\n\n**The equality plan arrives only as a document, on this channel only.**\n`equalityReportContent` comes off the partner DTO; island.is keeps it, because\nits editor already produces HTML. Each channel takes the form its users hold and\nneither has two ways to send one thing.\n\nOnly the playground''s scope is still blocked, and `PUT .../outliers` picks up two\ntenant-isolation conditions rather than one: not another company''s report, and\nnot a report that is already `SUBMITTED` or `IN_REVIEW`.\n\n* docs(doe): apply the renewal-window and dry-run decisions to the plan\n\nThe six-month renewal window is dead code on the partner channel:\n`application.service.ts:189` wraps the gate in `API_ENV === ''prod''`, but\n`API_ENV` is declared only in the sibling app''s schema and `varlock-run.sh`\nunsets every variable an app''s own schema omits. So the eligibility route\nadvertises `RENEWAL_WINDOW_NOT_OPEN` while the submission accepts the filing\nanyway. Decided the window does apply here; phase 1 declares `API_ENV`, which\ntakes the partner API''s required-variable count to 13.\n\nThe analysis route becomes an optional dry run rather than a sandbox: locked like\nevery other endpoint on `salary:submit`, and taking the submission''s own\n`{ scoringModelId, employees }` rather than an inline criteria tree, because a\ndry run against different input is not a dry run. Criteria experimentation was\nalready served by the 16 scoring-model routes. Carries a hard requirement that it\nshare the submission''s validation path — otherwise it is the fourth instance of\n\"previews clean, rejected at submit\" here.\n\n* feat(doe): narrow the partner API contract\n\nPhase 1 of `.github/planning/doe/plan-partner-api-contract.md`: everything that\nneeded no migration. Six of the seven remaining items; 1.4 is deferred, see\nbelow.\n\n**`GET /reports/equality/active` is gone.** It returned the company''s in-force\nequality report and nothing could be done with the answer — the salary\nsubmission resolves that report server-side and has carried no\n`equalityReportId` since #1483. Its last use was correlating a `providerId` back\nto a vendor''s records, which does not justify a route on either flow. A1''s\n`reportStatus` and `nextEqualityReportDueAt` already answer \"do they owe one\".\n\n**`company.nationalId` is gone.** The submission carried it and then refused any\nvalue that was not the authenticated company''s, so the only accepted value was\nthe one we already held and the field could only ever be wrong. The snapshot now\nreads it from the authenticated company, which makes the two structurally unable\nto disagree rather than merely checked. This surfaced a second call site the\ntype checker found for me: `report-draft-submit.service.ts` had the identical\nmapping and the identical dead check.\n\nTwo specs asserted the removed rejection. Replaced rather than deleted, with the\nguarantee that took its place — that the snapshot''s kennitala comes from the\nauthenticated company and the payload cannot influence it. The draft-submit one\nis dropped outright, since that path shares the mapping the application specs now\npin.\n\n**`salaryDataPeriod` is refused when the basis is `AVERAGE`**, instead of being\nsilently cleared. The check sits in `PartnerSubmissionService`, not in the shared\n`resolveSalaryDataBasis`, and the distinction matters: that resolver also serves\nthe draft PATCH, where a portal user switching a draft from MONTH to AVERAGE\nlegitimately sends the stale month and expects it dropped. Rejecting there would\nrefuse an ordinary edit. A partner submission is one shot built by a program, so\na month arriving with AVERAGE can only be a misread field — worth saying so on\nthe channel where that is true. It runs before the expansion, so a rejected\nsubmission does not first pay to expand a payroll extract.\n\n**`SubmitPartnerEqualityReportDto`**, mirroring the salary DTO''s `OmitType`\nshape. It drops the two base64-PDF fields, which exist for the island.is client\nand offered a vendor a second way to send one thing with the exclusivity rule\ninvisible in the schema — three optional content fields where exactly one was\npermitted, enforced a layer down in `resolveEqualityContent`. `equalityReportContent`\nis re-added as **required**, since with no PDF there is nothing left for it to be\nexclusive with, and requiring it means a missing plan fails in the document\nrather than reaching a service error that names a field this contract does not\nhave. Isolating the content fields into their own class is also what makes the\ndocument upload a small change later.\n\n**`API_ENV` declared on the partner API**, so the salary renewal-window gate\nactually runs here. It lives in `ApplicationService`, which both apps share,\nbehind `process.env.API_ENV === ''prod''` — and `varlock-run.sh` unsets every\nvariable an app''s own schema omits, so while it was undeclared the window was\ndead code on this surface: `GET /reports/salary/eligibility` answered\n`RENEWAL_WINDOW_NOT_OPEN` while the submission accepted the filing anyway. Takes\nthe partner API''s required-variable count to 13, and it must be set in ECS\nbefore deploy.\n\n**Guide:** A2 kept as a removal note rather than deleted, since integrators who\nread an earlier draft will look for it; section B renumbered to close the gap;\nA4 no longer tells callers to carry an equality report id the contract removed;\nthe catalog''s description no longer claims a submission carries a criteria tree,\nwhich stopped being true in #1508.\n\n**1.4 deferred.** Moving the catalog route beside the scoring-model routes needs\nthe catalog data moved out of the application module first: `ScoringModelApiModule`\ndeliberately does not boot `ApplicationCoreModule` — \"authoring a scoring model\ntouches none of the submission pipeline\" — and the catalog is still served from\n`ApplicationService`. Injecting it there to serve reference data would undo that\non purpose. The route keeps its path with a comment saying why, and the grouping\nis documentation-only for now.\n\n* docs(doe): record phase 1 as shipped\n\nSix items done in `2a66fb537`, 1.4 deferred with the reason attached: moving\nthe catalog route needs its data out of `ApplicationService` first, because\n`ScoringModelApiModule` deliberately does not boot `ApplicationCoreModule`.\n\nAlso notes what the plan had not anticipated — `report-draft-submit.service.ts`\ncarried the same dead parent-kennitala check that `application.service.ts` did,\nwhich the type checker found rather than the plan.\n\n* fix(doe): act on the reviews of #1532\n\nOne real bug, four untrue comments, two test gaps.\n\n**`providerId` was not trimmed, and it is the idempotency key.** `ApiString`\nemits `IsString`/`MinLength`/`MaxLength` and nothing else, so loosening the\nformat quietly widened the key: `\"abc\"` and `\"abc \"` became two, and a retry\ndiffering only by whitespace would file a second report — the loss the\n`replayed` flag exists to make visible. `@ApiUUID()` made that unreachable for\nfree; a bounded string has to say so. Caught by kthorri.\n\nThe fix goes in a shared `@ApiProviderId()` in `@dmr.is/decorators`, which also\nanswers thordurhhh''s finding: the decorator and a nine-line rationale were\ncopy-pasted verbatim into both submit DTOs, so the rule and its reasoning could\ndrift apart. Modelled on `ApiNationalId`, which normalises then validates for\nthe same reason. Trimming also means `MinLength(1)` refuses a whitespace-only\nid instead of storing one.\n\n**The pasted rationale was false on one of the two channels it served.** It said\nthe kennitala namespacing carries the safety — true of the partner channel,\nwhere `EXTERNAL_PROVIDER_CHANNEL` prefixes the authenticated company, and not of\nisland.is, where `buildProviderId` is identity and the value is stored raw.\nkthorri''s reading is right: on that channel this widening removes the only\ncross-caller guarantee there was, and the comment I wrote papered over it. The\nshared decorator now says so plainly — it is acceptable because one system mints\nthose ids, not because the type system still prevents a collision.\n\nThree more comments this PR falsified or left stale:\n\n- `api-key.service.ts` still said \"no DoE app schema declares `API_ENV`\". Both\n  now do.\n- `.env.schema`''s new rationale cited `scripts/varlock-run.sh`, the local-dev\n  wrapper, so \"dead code on this surface\" was established for local only — a\n  deployed container runs `varlock run` with no scrub. Narrowed to what is\n  actually true, and the real justification stated: declaring it makes the\n  behaviour deterministic rather than dependent on what happens to be in the\n  environment.\n- `deploy-containers.yml`''s terragrunt brief still said twelve required\n  variables with one new. Thirteen and two. That comment exists to prevent an\n  `aws ecs wait services-stable` timeout, and it is where the next person looks\n  — the PR body was the wrong place to record it.\n- The salary-submit description still routed vendors to\n  `GET /reports/equality/active`, deleted in this PR, and `salaryDataPeriod`\n  still documented itself as \"ignored for `AVERAGE`\" while the partner API now\n  refuses it. The guide was corrected and the DTO was not, so the generated\n  client disagreed with the document.\n\n**Two test gaps.** `report-draft-submit.service.ts` builds its own company\nsnapshots rather than sharing `ApplicationService`''s, so dropping the deleted\nmismatch test left that path''s kennitala unpinned — I had argued the application\nspecs covered it and they do not cover this method. Its replacement injects the\nhostile value at the JS level, since `nationalId` is gone from the TypeScript\ntype and a typed fixture cannot express the case. And the `providerId` spec\ngained the exact-256 accept, the whitespace-only reject and the trim cases.\n\n1553 / 82 / 179 / 5 green, format and lint clean.\n\n* docs(doe): record what the partner API plan did not know\n\nFour things a fresh session would otherwise have to rediscover, or lose:\n\n- Phase 2 is half-done. The .docx -> HTML converter is committed on\n  feat/doe-equality-document (6853deedd) with 12 cases, doing the magic-byte\n  sniffing, the 10 MB cap and the .pdf/.doc refusals this phase asked for. Only\n  the multipart route is unwired, and the plan showed the whole phase as\n  untouched.\n- The `/` bound on providerId is promised in a public comment on #1532 and not\n  written. It is a defect this branch introduced: 2026/Q1/042 files and then\n  matches no route on the way back.\n- The stack rebase. feat/doe-equality-document branches at e86fb9de1, not the\n  tip, so a squash-merge of #1532 strands it. The command is now in the file\n  rather than in one branch''s reflog.\n- The DoE app guide and the .claude symlinks that load it are uncommitted in\n  hxm-claude and local-only here, so the plan file and the PR bodies are the\n  whole handover that travels to another machine.\n\nAlso fixes the MD040 fence the #1532 review flagged.\n\n* docs(doe): the guide and its symlinks are committed now\n\nThe section listing them as uncommitted was written before they were. Both\nlanded in hxm-claude: the DoE app guide with the rules stub that loads it\n(1e9abf6), and the symlink script that no longer assumes the two repos are\nsiblings (f163ce9). A fresh clone is one script away from the context rather\nthan dependent on one machine''s hand-made links.\n\n* fix(doe): refuse a providerId that cannot be read back\n\nGET /partner/reports/:providerId is the only handle this API gives a vendor on\na filed report, and it is one path segment. Loosening providerId from a UUID to\nany non-empty string let 2026/Q1/042 file happily and then match no route, so\nthe report became unreadable by the id its own filer chose. That is a defect\nthe loosening introduced, which is why this was promoted out of Optional in the\n#1532 review rather than left as a nice-to-have.\n\nPercent-encoding is not a dependable way out: %2F is normalised by proxies and\nclients on the way in, and this API sits behind a shared ALB.\n\nOnly the slash. Every other awkward character round-trips reliably once\nencoded, and bounding further would re-impose a format for taste — the thing\nloosening this field set out to undo. The specs pin both halves, so a later\ntightening to a \"safe\" charset fails rather than quietly costing a vendor the\nids it already mints.\n\nLives in @ApiProviderId beside the trim, so all three writers of\nreport.provider_id inherit it, and the pattern reaches the generated client via\nApiProperty rather than only the guide.\n\n* docs(doe): the promised providerId bound is written\n\nCloses the last open item from the #1532 review in the plan: the `/` bound\nlanded in d761a481, so the section records what was decided and why the scope\nstops at the slash rather than listing it as owed.\n\n* docs(doe): stop claiming API_ENV makes the renewal gate run\n\nDeclaring the variable made its value deterministic. It did not make the gate\nfire: the gate is `=== ''prod''`, so outside prod it stays off while\nGET /reports/salary/eligibility evaluates the window unconditionally — the\neligibility-says-no / submit-says-yes divergence the comment opened by claiming\nto fix persists everywhere a vendor actually integrates.\n\nRaised in the re-review of #1532. The varlock-run.sh finding in the middle\nparagraph is untouched, since that is the fact that justifies the declaration\nand it survives whatever happens to the window. Also notes that ApiKeyService\nreads API_ENV too, advisory and with a fallback, so the variable has a reader\nthat outlives the gate.\n\nMaking the two paths agree is a decision about the gate, not about this schema.","tree":{"sha":"99e2d5100f904c9fbed8c95f519cc5e759ad6037","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/99e2d5100f904c9fbed8c95f519cc5e759ad6037"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c","verification":{"payload":"tree 99e2d5100f904c9fbed8c95f519cc5e759ad6037\nparent 8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b\nauthor Jón Bjarni Ólafsson <dev.jonbjarni@gmail.com> 1790074472 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790074472 +0000\n\nfeat(doe): narrow the partner API contract (#1532)\n\n* fix(doe): stop requiring a UUID of the caller''s own submission id\n\n`providerId` is the identifier the *caller* knows a submission by, and it was\n`@ApiUUID()` on both submit DTOs. Nothing reads it as a UUID — it is compared\nfor equality and nothing else — so the format only ever cost a vendor whose ids\nare not UUIDs a mapping table kept purely to satisfy us. The guide published\n`2026-Q1-042` as the shape of a vendor''s own id in one section and documented\nthe `400` that rejected it in another.\n\nNow `@ApiString({ minLength: 1, maxLength: 256 })`. What the field needs is to\nexist and to be bounded, because it reaches the partial unique index\n`report_provider_type_provider_id_unique_idx`. The safety was never the format:\nuniqueness is `(provider_type, provider_id)`, and the partner channel prefixes\nthe authenticated company''s kennitala from the verified API key, so a vendor can\nneither collide with another employer nor file under one. 256 matches the only\nstring bound this library already had, in `import-key.dto.ts`.\n\nOne change covers both channels: `SubmitPartnerSalaryReportDto` inherits the\nfield through `OmitType(SubmitSalaryReportDto)` and the partner controller takes\n`SubmitEqualityReportDto` directly.\n\nNothing else moves. It is a widening, so every payload accepted before is still\naccepted; island.is keeps sending its application UUID. `provider_id` is already\n`TEXT` and the index is untyped, so there is no migration. The committed\nisland.is client carries `providerId` as a plain `{\"type\": \"string\"}` with no\n`format: uuid`, so the generated type does not change and no regen is needed.\n\n`CreateDraftReportDto.providerId` deliberately stays a UUID: only\n`directorate-of-equality-api` exposes it, the partner API has no draft routes,\nand on that path the value really is the island.is application''s own UUID.\n\nAdds `provider-id.spec.ts`, which had no precedent for these DTOs — the bounds\nwere pinned by nothing. It runs both classes through class-validator and asserts\nthe loosening directly, so restoring the UUID rule fails CI rather than\nsurfacing as a vendor''s 400: a UUID, `2026-Q1-042`, an id containing a colon\n(which `toClientProviderId` already round-trips, since it strips the namespace\nby prefix rather than splitting) and a single character all pass, while empty,\nover-bound and non-string values are refused.\n\n* docs(doe): plan the partner API contract narrowing\n\nToday''s design work, in the form the repo tracks. Four phases, ordered so the\ncheap contract fixes ship without waiting on the migration: format and route\nremovals first, then the equality document upload, then moving outlier detection\ninto the submit, then repurposing the analysis route as a playground.\n\nThe three findings worth having in the repo rather than a chat log:\n\n**`mammoth@1.12.0` is already installed**, pulled in transitively by two\npackages. Earlier notes recorded that nothing here converts `.docx` to HTML and\nused that to defer the whole document question. It converts; the work is\npromoting a transitive dependency to a direct one.\n\n**The outlier round trip is a human one** — a vendor has to ask the employer why\ntwo equally-scored people are paid differently, which is days. Every shape that\nassumed it fits inside one request was wrong, and that is why detection moves\ninto the submission rather than sitting in front of it. It also disposes of the\npayload being sent twice, and of the warning that editing anything between the\ntwo calls reshuffles the detected set.\n\n**`POSTPONED` has no exit on this channel**, so we currently publish an option\nthat strands whoever takes it. `PUT .../outliers` closes it.\n\nEach phase carries its blocked decisions inline rather than in a separate list,\nbecause every one of them changes what gets written: whether a pending report is\nreplaceable or blocking decides the sibling policy, and whether the document is\nthe only accepted form decides if `equalityReportContent` survives at all.\n\nSecurity notes are specific to what these phases add — a public upload route\nwants magic-byte checks and a per-route multer cap rather than trust in a\nfilename, and the two new outlier routes want tenant-isolation specs, since a\ncredential that can leave a report pending can now also resolve one.\n\n`.github/planning/doe/` is new; shared was the only product with a folder.\n\n* docs(doe): settle four decisions in the partner API contract plan\n\n**A submission with unexplained outliers goes straight to `POSTPONED`.** The\nplan previously proposed a distinct pending state plus an explicit postpone\nroute, so nothing could land postponed by accident. Reversed, and the reversal\nis the better answer: island.is has always filed `POSTPONED` with one default\nempty group over every detected outlier, so a new state would have diverged the\ntwo channels for no gain — and it would have cost an enum migration, a\nreviewer-queue exclusion, compliance changes and its own reaper. `POSTPONED`\nalready means \"filed with unexplained outliers, a reviewer cannot pick it up\".\nPhase 3 now needs no schema change at all, and `POST .../postpone` is dropped\nsince omitting groups *is* the postpone.\n\n**The `409` on a prior `POSTPONED` report stays.** Recorded with the cost\nattached rather than as a bare decision, because it is now reachable by ordinary\nuse: a vendor who submits, lands `POSTPONED`, then finds a payroll error cannot\nre-submit — the only way out is explaining outliers in a payload they know is\nwrong. Making it replaceable was rejected because the sibling policy is shared\nwith the channel employers already use, and making it replaceable only for\n`provider_type = OTHER` is the one-policy-two-meanings divergence that keeps\nbiting here. If support load proves real, the fix is a withdraw route on a\nreport this channel filed, not a branch in the policy.\n\n**`company.nationalId` comes out of the body.** It is validated to equal the\nauthenticated company, so it can only ever hold one value. Noted what this gives\nup: once vendor clients land it would have cross-checked the snapshot against\nthe `X-Company-National-Id` header. If that matters it belongs in the delegation\nguard, not in a field the caller supplies.\n\n**The equality plan arrives only as a document, on this channel only.**\n`equalityReportContent` comes off the partner DTO; island.is keeps it, because\nits editor already produces HTML. Each channel takes the form its users hold and\nneither has two ways to send one thing.\n\nOnly the playground''s scope is still blocked, and `PUT .../outliers` picks up two\ntenant-isolation conditions rather than one: not another company''s report, and\nnot a report that is already `SUBMITTED` or `IN_REVIEW`.\n\n* docs(doe): apply the renewal-window and dry-run decisions to the plan\n\nThe six-month renewal window is dead code on the partner channel:\n`application.service.ts:189` wraps the gate in `API_ENV === ''prod''`, but\n`API_ENV` is declared only in the sibling app''s schema and `varlock-run.sh`\nunsets every variable an app''s own schema omits. So the eligibility route\nadvertises `RENEWAL_WINDOW_NOT_OPEN` while the submission accepts the filing\nanyway. Decided the window does apply here; phase 1 declares `API_ENV`, which\ntakes the partner API''s required-variable count to 13.\n\nThe analysis route becomes an optional dry run rather than a sandbox: locked like\nevery other endpoint on `salary:submit`, and taking the submission''s own\n`{ scoringModelId, employees }` rather than an inline criteria tree, because a\ndry run against different input is not a dry run. Criteria experimentation was\nalready served by the 16 scoring-model routes. Carries a hard requirement that it\nshare the submission''s validation path — otherwise it is the fourth instance of\n\"previews clean, rejected at submit\" here.\n\n* feat(doe): narrow the partner API contract\n\nPhase 1 of `.github/planning/doe/plan-partner-api-contract.md`: everything that\nneeded no migration. Six of the seven remaining items; 1.4 is deferred, see\nbelow.\n\n**`GET /reports/equality/active` is gone.** It returned the company''s in-force\nequality report and nothing could be done with the answer — the salary\nsubmission resolves that report server-side and has carried no\n`equalityReportId` since #1483. Its last use was correlating a `providerId` back\nto a vendor''s records, which does not justify a route on either flow. A1''s\n`reportStatus` and `nextEqualityReportDueAt` already answer \"do they owe one\".\n\n**`company.nationalId` is gone.** The submission carried it and then refused any\nvalue that was not the authenticated company''s, so the only accepted value was\nthe one we already held and the field could only ever be wrong. The snapshot now\nreads it from the authenticated company, which makes the two structurally unable\nto disagree rather than merely checked. This surfaced a second call site the\ntype checker found for me: `report-draft-submit.service.ts` had the identical\nmapping and the identical dead check.\n\nTwo specs asserted the removed rejection. Replaced rather than deleted, with the\nguarantee that took its place — that the snapshot''s kennitala comes from the\nauthenticated company and the payload cannot influence it. The draft-submit one\nis dropped outright, since that path shares the mapping the application specs now\npin.\n\n**`salaryDataPeriod` is refused when the basis is `AVERAGE`**, instead of being\nsilently cleared. The check sits in `PartnerSubmissionService`, not in the shared\n`resolveSalaryDataBasis`, and the distinction matters: that resolver also serves\nthe draft PATCH, where a portal user switching a draft from MONTH to AVERAGE\nlegitimately sends the stale month and expects it dropped. Rejecting there would\nrefuse an ordinary edit. A partner submission is one shot built by a program, so\na month arriving with AVERAGE can only be a misread field — worth saying so on\nthe channel where that is true. It runs before the expansion, so a rejected\nsubmission does not first pay to expand a payroll extract.\n\n**`SubmitPartnerEqualityReportDto`**, mirroring the salary DTO''s `OmitType`\nshape. It drops the two base64-PDF fields, which exist for the island.is client\nand offered a vendor a second way to send one thing with the exclusivity rule\ninvisible in the schema — three optional content fields where exactly one was\npermitted, enforced a layer down in `resolveEqualityContent`. `equalityReportContent`\nis re-added as **required**, since with no PDF there is nothing left for it to be\nexclusive with, and requiring it means a missing plan fails in the document\nrather than reaching a service error that names a field this contract does not\nhave. Isolating the content fields into their own class is also what makes the\ndocument upload a small change later.\n\n**`API_ENV` declared on the partner API**, so the salary renewal-window gate\nactually runs here. It lives in `ApplicationService`, which both apps share,\nbehind `process.env.API_ENV === ''prod''` — and `varlock-run.sh` unsets every\nvariable an app''s own schema omits, so while it was undeclared the window was\ndead code on this surface: `GET /reports/salary/eligibility` answered\n`RENEWAL_WINDOW_NOT_OPEN` while the submission accepted the filing anyway. Takes\nthe partner API''s required-variable count to 13, and it must be set in ECS\nbefore deploy.\n\n**Guide:** A2 kept as a removal note rather than deleted, since integrators who\nread an earlier draft will look for it; section B renumbered to close the gap;\nA4 no longer tells callers to carry an equality report id the contract removed;\nthe catalog''s description no longer claims a submission carries a criteria tree,\nwhich stopped being true in #1508.\n\n**1.4 deferred.** Moving the catalog route beside the scoring-model routes needs\nthe catalog data moved out of the application module first: `ScoringModelApiModule`\ndeliberately does not boot `ApplicationCoreModule` — \"authoring a scoring model\ntouches none of the submission pipeline\" — and the catalog is still served from\n`ApplicationService`. Injecting it there to serve reference data would undo that\non purpose. The route keeps its path with a comment saying why, and the grouping\nis documentation-only for now.\n\n* docs(doe): record phase 1 as shipped\n\nSix items done in `2a66fb537`, 1.4 deferred with the reason attached: moving\nthe catalog route needs its data out of `ApplicationService` first, because\n`ScoringModelApiModule` deliberately does not boot `ApplicationCoreModule`.\n\nAlso notes what the plan had not anticipated — `report-draft-submit.service.ts`\ncarried the same dead parent-kennitala check that `application.service.ts` did,\nwhich the type checker found rather than the plan.\n\n* fix(doe): act on the reviews of #1532\n\nOne real bug, four untrue comments, two test gaps.\n\n**`providerId` was not trimmed, and it is the idempotency key.** `ApiString`\nemits `IsString`/`MinLength`/`MaxLength` and nothing else, so loosening the\nformat quietly widened the key: `\"abc\"` and `\"abc \"` became two, and a retry\ndiffering only by whitespace would file a second report — the loss the\n`replayed` flag exists to make visible. `@ApiUUID()` made that unreachable for\nfree; a bounded string has to say so. Caught by kthorri.\n\nThe fix goes in a shared `@ApiProviderId()` in `@dmr.is/decorators`, which also\nanswers thordurhhh''s finding: the decorator and a nine-line rationale were\ncopy-pasted verbatim into both submit DTOs, so the rule and its reasoning could\ndrift apart. Modelled on `ApiNationalId`, which normalises then validates for\nthe same reason. Trimming also means `MinLength(1)` refuses a whitespace-only\nid instead of storing one.\n\n**The pasted rationale was false on one of the two channels it served.** It said\nthe kennitala namespacing carries the safety — true of the partner channel,\nwhere `EXTERNAL_PROVIDER_CHANNEL` prefixes the authenticated company, and not of\nisland.is, where `buildProviderId` is identity and the value is stored raw.\nkthorri''s reading is right: on that channel this widening removes the only\ncross-caller guarantee there was, and the comment I wrote papered over it. The\nshared decorator now says so plainly — it is acceptable because one system mints\nthose ids, not because the type system still prevents a collision.\n\nThree more comments this PR falsified or left stale:\n\n- `api-key.service.ts` still said \"no DoE app schema declares `API_ENV`\". Both\n  now do.\n- `.env.schema`''s new rationale cited `scripts/varlock-run.sh`, the local-dev\n  wrapper, so \"dead code on this surface\" was established for local only — a\n  deployed container runs `varlock run` with no scrub. Narrowed to what is\n  actually true, and the real justification stated: declaring it makes the\n  behaviour deterministic rather than dependent on what happens to be in the\n  environment.\n- `deploy-containers.yml`''s terragrunt brief still said twelve required\n  variables with one new. Thirteen and two. That comment exists to prevent an\n  `aws ecs wait services-stable` timeout, and it is where the next person looks\n  — the PR body was the wrong place to record it.\n- The salary-submit description still routed vendors to\n  `GET /reports/equality/active`, deleted in this PR, and `salaryDataPeriod`\n  still documented itself as \"ignored for `AVERAGE`\" while the partner API now\n  refuses it. The guide was corrected and the DTO was not, so the generated\n  client disagreed with the document.\n\n**Two test gaps.** `report-draft-submit.service.ts` builds its own company\nsnapshots rather than sharing `ApplicationService`''s, so dropping the deleted\nmismatch test left that path''s kennitala unpinned — I had argued the application\nspecs covered it and they do not cover this method. Its replacement injects the\nhostile value at the JS level, since `nationalId` is gone from the TypeScript\ntype and a typed fixture cannot express the case. And the `providerId` spec\ngained the exact-256 accept, the whitespace-only reject and the trim cases.\n\n1553 / 82 / 179 / 5 green, format and lint clean.\n\n* docs(doe): record what the partner API plan did not know\n\nFour things a fresh session would otherwise have to rediscover, or lose:\n\n- Phase 2 is half-done. The .docx -> HTML converter is committed on\n  feat/doe-equality-document (6853deedd) with 12 cases, doing the magic-byte\n  sniffing, the 10 MB cap and the .pdf/.doc refusals this phase asked for. Only\n  the multipart route is unwired, and the plan showed the whole phase as\n  untouched.\n- The `/` bound on providerId is promised in a public comment on #1532 and not\n  written. It is a defect this branch introduced: 2026/Q1/042 files and then\n  matches no route on the way back.\n- The stack rebase. feat/doe-equality-document branches at e86fb9de1, not the\n  tip, so a squash-merge of #1532 strands it. The command is now in the file\n  rather than in one branch''s reflog.\n- The DoE app guide and the .claude symlinks that load it are uncommitted in\n  hxm-claude and local-only here, so the plan file and the PR bodies are the\n  whole handover that travels to another machine.\n\nAlso fixes the MD040 fence the #1532 review flagged.\n\n* docs(doe): the guide and its symlinks are committed now\n\nThe section listing them as uncommitted was written before they were. Both\nlanded in hxm-claude: the DoE app guide with the rules stub that loads it\n(1e9abf6), and the symlink script that no longer assumes the two repos are\nsiblings (f163ce9). A fresh clone is one script away from the context rather\nthan dependent on one machine''s hand-made links.\n\n* fix(doe): refuse a providerId that cannot be read back\n\nGET /partner/reports/:providerId is the only handle this API gives a vendor on\na filed report, and it is one path segment. Loosening providerId from a UUID to\nany non-empty string let 2026/Q1/042 file happily and then match no route, so\nthe report became unreadable by the id its own filer chose. That is a defect\nthe loosening introduced, which is why this was promoted out of Optional in the\n#1532 review rather than left as a nice-to-have.\n\nPercent-encoding is not a dependable way out: %2F is normalised by proxies and\nclients on the way in, and this API sits behind a shared ALB.\n\nOnly the slash. Every other awkward character round-trips reliably once\nencoded, and bounding further would re-impose a format for taste — the thing\nloosening this field set out to undo. The specs pin both halves, so a later\ntightening to a \"safe\" charset fails rather than quietly costing a vendor the\nids it already mints.\n\nLives in @ApiProviderId beside the trim, so all three writers of\nreport.provider_id inherit it, and the pattern reaches the generated client via\nApiProperty rather than only the guide.\n\n* docs(doe): the promised providerId bound is written\n\nCloses the last open item from the #1532 review in the plan: the `/` bound\nlanded in d761a481, so the section records what was decided and why the scope\nstops at the slash rather than listing it as owed.\n\n* docs(doe): stop claiming API_ENV makes the renewal gate run\n\nDeclaring the variable made its value deterministic. It did not make the gate\nfire: the gate is `=== ''prod''`, so outside prod it stays off while\nGET /reports/salary/eligibility evaluates the window unconditionally — the\neligibility-says-no / submit-says-yes divergence the comment opened by claiming\nto fix persists everywhere a vendor actually integrates.\n\nRaised in the re-review of #1532. The varlock-run.sh finding in the middle\nparagraph is untouched, since that is the fact that justifies the declaration\nand it survives whatever happens to the window. Also notes that ApiKeyService\nreads API_ENV too, advisory and with a fallback, so the variable has a reader\nthat outlives the gate.\n\nMaking the two paths agree is a decision about the gate, not about this schema.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsl5oCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAftsQAHcrI6sWzkeqGvPQx0wd3lk5\n3V+23VWSjc4CqBpPtzXYyVvZ4cLwVNbuLXbIIXSy0ORrVizuet5YvOry4TEEYoEV\nd2Bgr0SaFxksuCelut5Shg/rSq7/3RCQluNgbCx7Jqq0ZUE9vjlggBRr6vOTwfZW\n9/fLUtKE05z30EM1VdlsaY0gk+pj0PclPvPcG6+IRUTH1J40dlqJa9N1u1Lnv989\n3Rek3xaUqrSfrK/TzR6PPArONh31MraDAkglaYRPMcrGaXIb+WHceEFjvTX02gwb\ncEsfMfGXAxS8H1o4MpAYvQGvHl+pB2uvUq4t97zQxTBSrGBaknb0Bqslnju4pX0G\nYAxk1w7olWlVb+txXulyP17U4zE1FC6Vx2+zjvs5Xt5aEQqeW2KRyjlPkIYVxGj+\nZL1TiyAm8RcMeTKKaVUrOXyupEtnd62EKvTGxeMtywB1Lcf+fFbLcIPMU3CzdZ/l\nPwDV/imv/BLFzUIHGdvf9VZmw5oqD2Uhwifl3KvavtD33mcIDQoTuDr52viKDIM5\ncMGKZLbptYJG2W6Frd/iQayHWfEnXgRZ/ay507ieesY5jbyqc1hbn7k1mwLjy0xF\nK1YtFz5SgHwcdIlx7PTtpQrCtfE44/el4QnZ3D5hoCTpGZlLv537NTARgzXGngib\nrVaSO0ysca/nrL+5Btf1\n=06wY\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T10:54:33Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKDIwNTdjN2I4NjEzNGZiOTYwZTlmMjljMDhmZTI1MDE0YmZhZjA2NWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b","sha":"8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b"}],"sha":"2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/2057c7b86134fb960e9f29c08fe25014bfaf065c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:20:47Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T09:20:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(shared): make modal and drawer ids deterministic without a provider (#1534)\n\n* fix(shared): make modal and drawer ids deterministic without a provider\n\nModal and Drawer both defaulted baseId to '''', and reakit resolves it with\n`initialBaseId || generateId()`. An empty string is falsy, so the default\nfell straight through to reakit''s Math.random() generator: every modal and\ndrawer that did not pass an explicit baseId got an id that differed between\nthe server and the client.\n\nDefault to useId() instead, which is stable across both. This makes a modal\ndeterministic on its own rather than only when it happens to sit under the\nReakitIdProvider added in #1533, and leaves the explicit baseId call sites\nuntouched.\n\nMeasured without any provider mounted: the old shape returns a different id\non each render (id-g2qc09, id-9f6bgt), the new one returns _R_0_ both times,\nand an explicit baseId still wins in both shapes.\n\nSame empty-string trap as the instanceId=\"\" one fixed in the Select wrapper\non #1530.\n\n* fix(ojoi): stop two advert dialogs sharing one DOM id\n\nAdvertPreview and AdvertDisplay both passed baseId=\"myDialog\" to ModalBase,\nand CaseFields renders them on the same screen: AdvertPreview unconditionally,\nAdvertDisplay through AdvertFields in the accordion below it. ModalBase feeds\nbaseId to both the element id and aria-controls, so /ritstjorn/[...uid] served\na duplicate DOM id and a disclosure whose aria-controls resolved ambiguously.\n\nGive each one useId(), which is unique per instance and stable across server\nand client. Nothing referenced the literal \"myDialog\".","tree":{"sha":"3c7a584a9c78eb78850aec120d3d765b8b2a6104","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/3c7a584a9c78eb78850aec120d3d765b8b2a6104"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b","verification":{"payload":"tree 3c7a584a9c78eb78850aec120d3d765b8b2a6104\nparent db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1790068847 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790068847 +0000\n\nfix(shared): make modal and drawer ids deterministic without a provider (#1534)\n\n* fix(shared): make modal and drawer ids deterministic without a provider\n\nModal and Drawer both defaulted baseId to '''', and reakit resolves it with\n`initialBaseId || generateId()`. An empty string is falsy, so the default\nfell straight through to reakit''s Math.random() generator: every modal and\ndrawer that did not pass an explicit baseId got an id that differed between\nthe server and the client.\n\nDefault to useId() instead, which is stable across both. This makes a modal\ndeterministic on its own rather than only when it happens to sit under the\nReakitIdProvider added in #1533, and leaves the explicit baseId call sites\nuntouched.\n\nMeasured without any provider mounted: the old shape returns a different id\non each render (id-g2qc09, id-9f6bgt), the new one returns _R_0_ both times,\nand an explicit baseId still wins in both shapes.\n\nSame empty-string trap as the instanceId=\"\" one fixed in the Select wrapper\non #1530.\n\n* fix(ojoi): stop two advert dialogs sharing one DOM id\n\nAdvertPreview and AdvertDisplay both passed baseId=\"myDialog\" to ModalBase,\nand CaseFields renders them on the same screen: AdvertPreview unconditionally,\nAdvertDisplay through AdvertFields in the accordion below it. ModalBase feeds\nbaseId to both the element id and aria-controls, so /ritstjorn/[...uid] served\na duplicate DOM id and a disclosure whose aria-controls resolved ambiguously.\n\nGive each one useId(), which is unique per instance and stable across server\nand client. Nothing referenced the literal \"myDialog\".","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqskhvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA5aUQADL/woJSeod2RZMiLfTOP6VZ\n+JhHQSnrGDcnPqy/r7P7W62+QdvcdjAneEP/b7VlVeqW1O/Pmu+xEtxeabSj21Jb\nuY9BmgaVWJ6Eg4TNAZRGi53fOMDIHJSuo3//6MgKp6R5HrNXDRMgzU/ptOOQtX7q\nJX74SI62A3Xvc5dZOlbU0sZpqhbp/H8LzPuTzMlnj1Ic31nthaYbE2hAWHCA1phn\nXO7OP0LiRiqwlZcXOuSpoZy9KB9e09nliLfg1vhoXzThR2kli2iI9pG/0SJTgaqJ\n3bbAA9nNw7BTrDvVLvqInLXfXfWI2/JjY6NdyTqodJUGIaFS1r9tmnYcB81eBU6A\n4CJH92sQN2uUa1FHx9k7r/mmUENIIok6f3zj/GBhmoq7Vg1TCkFk7skj1jCuLDkw\nC/6llxFoSpVv1xk2j8oCxeLAYJ+NB7emYdPYS79jgsDa8usa1xTN1xyKygaZ1j1B\nMj5os4KjrvMLOtw4YWvcDo+YAwhpLtqeu4KLSV7ILbIUkda2rGoLkZgue4qzORtj\nOZhreixWT+TU4lT7X7fvOfJi7Etx5/8XgwNvqng+kZW27fsTZqfIFg7CbeOcVsCZ\noDpOagrmh2RTghLZd7Cz656xCY7HhDH3UCf35QnW4Z6oEMF3PbU4LChAEgyaKNdi\nRh8eDHEdQ8bW/9rB84QM\n=vXEM\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T09:20:48Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKDgzNzBjODZlNjI1NjMyNGQ4ZDUwODQ4Yzg3OTE2MmUyNTBlOGFkOWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a","sha":"db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a"}],"sha":"8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/8370c86e6256324d8d50848c879162e250e8ad9b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T15:50:37Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T15:50:37Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(shared): give reakit deterministic ids so tabs hydrate consistently (#1533)\n\nisland-ui''s Tabs calls reakit''s useTabState without a baseId, so reakit\nfalls back to Math.random() for every base id. The server and the client\ngenerate different ones, and because React does not patch attribute\nmismatches, each tab''s aria-controls is left pointing at an id that is\nnot in the document.\n\nWrap each app''s client providers in reakit''s unstable_IdProvider, which\nswaps the random generator for a per-tree counter. Measured against a\nproduction build: dangling aria-controls references go from two to none,\nboth immediately after hydration and after activating a tab. The counter\nalso stays aligned under streaming SSR with an out-of-order Suspense\nboundary, where the client follows the server''s stream order.\n\nCovers directorate-of-equality-web as well as the legal gazette and\nofficial journal webs; all three render the same component.\n\nUnder StrictMode the development-only double render still drifts reakit''s\nper-component id suffix. No app-level code can fix that: reakit increments\na ref inside a useState initializer, which bypasses this generator. It is\ndev-only noise, and it goes away with the move to ariakit.","tree":{"sha":"33e391a89afe673cffd6524f1ad585f65899fb97","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/33e391a89afe673cffd6524f1ad585f65899fb97"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a","verification":{"payload":"tree 33e391a89afe673cffd6524f1ad585f65899fb97\nparent 93f2fc651f7d74d82453dc1304e3bc16620ff37e\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1790005837 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790005837 +0000\n\nfix(shared): give reakit deterministic ids so tabs hydrate consistently (#1533)\n\nisland-ui''s Tabs calls reakit''s useTabState without a baseId, so reakit\nfalls back to Math.random() for every base id. The server and the client\ngenerate different ones, and because React does not patch attribute\nmismatches, each tab''s aria-controls is left pointing at an id that is\nnot in the document.\n\nWrap each app''s client providers in reakit''s unstable_IdProvider, which\nswaps the random generator for a per-tree counter. Measured against a\nproduction build: dangling aria-controls references go from two to none,\nboth immediately after hydration and after activating a tab. The counter\nalso stays aligned under streaming SSR with an out-of-order Suspense\nboundary, where the client follows the server''s stream order.\n\nCovers directorate-of-equality-web as well as the legal gazette and\nofficial journal webs; all three render the same component.\n\nUnder StrictMode the development-only double render still drifts reakit''s\nper-component id suffix. No app-level code can fix that: reakit increments\na ref inside a useState initializer, which bypasses this generator. It is\ndev-only noise, and it goes away with the move to ariakit.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsVJNCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAW9sQAJKuw7zmlFs2uxTE/rC99ZC1\n6KOSAywfIkk4gxJxOj1a0t8QWX2O5fE8mwHcldCQhNzkjg/Dx4J09Zup9QTC2bXy\nY1BYcKQqK2lEtFVgHEbUpxZQ8Raymz8wXL1/UvPKUHZuP223bCRFXadJgCHb4RQe\noB/DUsluDIN/kK6ohFv4B5FnNYNywhL58W8SILgi1baZVcgLh0uZ96biR5V/OjaA\ns6eAt33aEfSfNj5CCV71tfiLVq7VxxIk+dNQ4nzeTbaNr+E9O97J6dE+TK/8qMFN\nBLRS+9zrBulkyHbVk3ZWVGVn/ZaTPIcRLq4Vubb+OOtE5GgOD9wWCWX3qnDQwVDn\nhe7waf/2um+jdbtY7YXoOt67Qru7WnxuHMQrLamaujkCmPXvOTD5VIqEPaqqNmYs\nTgcKaOKZOCexmRIealJbX66Sjm1mPMwBUy2ZVvehpBCSnb3QYXADuU9Icxmvp1ke\nak8oimWnbz5CkwIvuCiD/cJKt4HkZcbBRRQVX77w6uySf8odR3h55800vFJZs3pf\nOAor0sOsNsfUFkwXElE8LtafyTMSNgJ4Y6hLG5sDrmmng5b3dRDPQi71Lc9ttNRu\n8/s75EwSJapOZ4rx8t7bYTWq93onGqpindQ3k1y7PymKVqQV8CSYH3QyLp3ZwdCd\nG//OUj/wGUIh8z4JS9nT\n=j6vU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T15:50:38Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKGRiNmQ3ODNlYjU1ZmViYzM3YTQ0OTExMGE4NWJkZDk1ZjAwNjg2N2E","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/93f2fc651f7d74d82453dc1304e3bc16620ff37e","sha":"93f2fc651f7d74d82453dc1304e3bc16620ff37e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/93f2fc651f7d74d82453dc1304e3bc16620ff37e"}],"sha":"db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/db6d783eb55febc37a449110a85bdd95f006867a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/93f2fc651f7d74d82453dc1304e3bc16620ff37e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T14:20:46Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T14:20:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(ojoi): make prefetched pages hydrate consistently (#1531)\n\n* fix(ojoi): make prefetched pages hydrate consistently\n\nSame page/container mismatch as #1529 and #1530, on the two Official Journal\npages that prefetch without awaiting.\n\n`prefetch()` left unawaited streams the page out before the data lands, which\nonly works when the consumer reads through `useSuspenseQuery`. Both of these\nread through `useQuery` and render `data ?? []`, so the server rendered empty\ntables and selects while the client hydrated populated ones. This is the quiet\nform of the bug - no console warning, just an empty first paint - which is why\nit went unnoticed.\n\n- `notendur` - `getUsers` and `getInvolvedPartiesByUser`, read in\n  `context/userContext.tsx` and rendered by `UsersTable` and `InstitutionTable`.\n- `yfirflokkar` - `getMainCategories`, `getCategories` and `getDepartments`,\n  read in `context/categoryContext.tsx`.\n\nBoth pages await through `Promise.all`, so the queries still run concurrently.\n\n`UsersTable` rendered `createdAt`/`updatedAt` through the app-local `formatDate`,\nwhich is date-fns `format` and resolves in the *runtime''s* timezone. Awaiting\nwithout addressing that would have traded the empty first paint for a\ntimezone-dependent mismatch, invisible in production but live on any dev machine\noff GMT. It now uses `formatDateInIceland` from #1530.\n\n`hefti` is left alone: it prefetches unawaited and reads through\n`useSuspenseQuery`, which is the pattern that pairing is built for.\n\nVerified there is nothing else timezone- or locale-dependent in either\nnewly-server-rendered subtree. `CreateMainCategory` seeds state with\n`new Date().toISOString()`, but only ever uses it as a React `key`, and keys are\nnot rendered into the HTML.\n\n* fix(ojoi): key the users prefetch to the URL and add loading fallbacks\n\nTwo review findings.\n\n`notendur` prefetched a hardcoded `{ page: 1, pageSize: 10 }` while\n`UserProvider` keys `getUsers` on five values read from the URL\n(`userContext.tsx:77-92`): page, pageSize, hlutverk, stofnun and leit. On a bare\nURL the keys hash equal, because react-query drops the undefined filters, but on\n`?page=2` or `?leit=jon` they diverge - so the page blocked on a fetch the\ncontainer then discarded, leaving the user waiting longer for the same empty\nfirst paint this branch set out to remove. The page took no `searchParams` prop\nat all, so it could not align.\n\nIt now accepts `searchParams` and parses them through a new `usersParamsCache`\nthat mirrors the provider''s five params, mapping null to undefined exactly as\nthe provider does.\n\nAwaiting also removed any instant feedback on client-side navigation, since this\napp has no `loading.tsx` at any level. Both newly-blocking routes get a\nfallback. Neither painted a skeleton before - they rendered empty and filled in -\nso this is also the first real loading state these routes have had.\n\n* fix(ojoi): give the notendur Tabs a real label\n\n`UsersPageClient` passed `label=\"\"` to island-ui''s `Tabs`, which feeds it to an\ninternal react-select as `name`. That becomes `id`, then `instanceId`, and\nreact-select resolves its element-id prefix as `instanceId || ++counter` - so an\nempty string falls through to a module-level counter whose value differs between\nthe server render and the client one.\n\nThat Select really does reach the HTML: it sits inside\n`hidden={tabs.length < 2 || showDesktopLayout}`, and `showDesktopLayout` is false\nduring SSR because `useWindowSize` has no window. Now that this page is awaited\nand genuinely server-rendered, it was a live `react-select-N-live-region`\nattribute mismatch on the very page this branch fixes - and React does not patch\nattribute mismatches up, so `aria-labelledby` and `aria-activedescendant` were\nleft pointing at ids that are not in the DOM.\n\nThe shared Select wrapper cannot help here: `Tabs` imports Select straight from\nisland-ui/core, not through it. A non-empty label is the fix. The matching\n`label=\"\"` on legal-gazette-web''s ritstjorn Tabs is fixed in #1530.\n\n* docs(ojoi): say what the Tabs label does and does not fix\n\nMirrors the wording agreed on #1530 so the two comments do not drift.\n\nThe label makes the internal react-select''s ids deterministic, which is what\nremoves the hydration mismatch. It does not make them correct: island-ui''s Tabs\npasses `name={label}` into a Select that defaults `id = name`, so the id is the\nraw label string and `aria-labelledby` parses its spaces as separate ids that do\nnot exist. It also renders as visible copy, not a screen-reader string, above\nthe Select the tab strip collapses into on narrow viewports - and only on the\nisAdmin branch, so it takes an admin session to see at all.","tree":{"sha":"e28026af5dce08edd6b2d36a64457dfb7146256e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/e28026af5dce08edd6b2d36a64457dfb7146256e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/93f2fc651f7d74d82453dc1304e3bc16620ff37e","verification":{"payload":"tree e28026af5dce08edd6b2d36a64457dfb7146256e\nparent 722c705d2999a00f0afa6d01ed69eba89040be51\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1790000446 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790000446 +0000\n\nfix(ojoi): make prefetched pages hydrate consistently (#1531)\n\n* fix(ojoi): make prefetched pages hydrate consistently\n\nSame page/container mismatch as #1529 and #1530, on the two Official Journal\npages that prefetch without awaiting.\n\n`prefetch()` left unawaited streams the page out before the data lands, which\nonly works when the consumer reads through `useSuspenseQuery`. Both of these\nread through `useQuery` and render `data ?? []`, so the server rendered empty\ntables and selects while the client hydrated populated ones. This is the quiet\nform of the bug - no console warning, just an empty first paint - which is why\nit went unnoticed.\n\n- `notendur` - `getUsers` and `getInvolvedPartiesByUser`, read in\n  `context/userContext.tsx` and rendered by `UsersTable` and `InstitutionTable`.\n- `yfirflokkar` - `getMainCategories`, `getCategories` and `getDepartments`,\n  read in `context/categoryContext.tsx`.\n\nBoth pages await through `Promise.all`, so the queries still run concurrently.\n\n`UsersTable` rendered `createdAt`/`updatedAt` through the app-local `formatDate`,\nwhich is date-fns `format` and resolves in the *runtime''s* timezone. Awaiting\nwithout addressing that would have traded the empty first paint for a\ntimezone-dependent mismatch, invisible in production but live on any dev machine\noff GMT. It now uses `formatDateInIceland` from #1530.\n\n`hefti` is left alone: it prefetches unawaited and reads through\n`useSuspenseQuery`, which is the pattern that pairing is built for.\n\nVerified there is nothing else timezone- or locale-dependent in either\nnewly-server-rendered subtree. `CreateMainCategory` seeds state with\n`new Date().toISOString()`, but only ever uses it as a React `key`, and keys are\nnot rendered into the HTML.\n\n* fix(ojoi): key the users prefetch to the URL and add loading fallbacks\n\nTwo review findings.\n\n`notendur` prefetched a hardcoded `{ page: 1, pageSize: 10 }` while\n`UserProvider` keys `getUsers` on five values read from the URL\n(`userContext.tsx:77-92`): page, pageSize, hlutverk, stofnun and leit. On a bare\nURL the keys hash equal, because react-query drops the undefined filters, but on\n`?page=2` or `?leit=jon` they diverge - so the page blocked on a fetch the\ncontainer then discarded, leaving the user waiting longer for the same empty\nfirst paint this branch set out to remove. The page took no `searchParams` prop\nat all, so it could not align.\n\nIt now accepts `searchParams` and parses them through a new `usersParamsCache`\nthat mirrors the provider''s five params, mapping null to undefined exactly as\nthe provider does.\n\nAwaiting also removed any instant feedback on client-side navigation, since this\napp has no `loading.tsx` at any level. Both newly-blocking routes get a\nfallback. Neither painted a skeleton before - they rendered empty and filled in -\nso this is also the first real loading state these routes have had.\n\n* fix(ojoi): give the notendur Tabs a real label\n\n`UsersPageClient` passed `label=\"\"` to island-ui''s `Tabs`, which feeds it to an\ninternal react-select as `name`. That becomes `id`, then `instanceId`, and\nreact-select resolves its element-id prefix as `instanceId || ++counter` - so an\nempty string falls through to a module-level counter whose value differs between\nthe server render and the client one.\n\nThat Select really does reach the HTML: it sits inside\n`hidden={tabs.length < 2 || showDesktopLayout}`, and `showDesktopLayout` is false\nduring SSR because `useWindowSize` has no window. Now that this page is awaited\nand genuinely server-rendered, it was a live `react-select-N-live-region`\nattribute mismatch on the very page this branch fixes - and React does not patch\nattribute mismatches up, so `aria-labelledby` and `aria-activedescendant` were\nleft pointing at ids that are not in the DOM.\n\nThe shared Select wrapper cannot help here: `Tabs` imports Select straight from\nisland-ui/core, not through it. A non-empty label is the fix. The matching\n`label=\"\"` on legal-gazette-web''s ritstjorn Tabs is fixed in #1530.\n\n* docs(ojoi): say what the Tabs label does and does not fix\n\nMirrors the wording agreed on #1530 so the two comments do not drift.\n\nThe label makes the internal react-select''s ids deterministic, which is what\nremoves the hydration mismatch. It does not make them correct: island-ui''s Tabs\npasses `name={label}` into a Select that defaults `id = name`, so the id is the\nraw label string and `aria-labelledby` parses its spaces as separate ids that do\nnot exist. It also renders as visible copy, not a screen-reader string, above\nthe Select the tab strip collapses into on narrow viewports - and only on the\nisAdmin branch, so it takes an admin session to see at all.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsT0+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAACwQQADXWZbqFKZDVSLlGAgqvD4xv\n2g/0uqL+BjsUGRuRJNRTbNoWauQmGVdNO6