ed69eba89040be51","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/722c705d2999a00f0afa6d01ed69eba89040be51"}],"sha":"93f2fc651f7d74d82453dc1304e3bc16620ff37e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/93f2fc651f7d74d82453dc1304e3bc16620ff37e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/722c705d2999a00f0afa6d01ed69eba89040be51/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T14:16:21Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T14:16:21Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(lg): make prefetched pages hydrate consistently (#1530)\n\n* fix(lg): make prefetched pages hydrate consistently\n\nFollow-up sweep to #1529, which fixed `/flokkar-og-tegundir`. The same\npage/container mismatch was on five more Legal Gazette pages.\n\n`prefetch()` left unawaited streams the page out before the data lands. That\nonly works when the consumer reads through `useSuspenseQuery`, which is the\npattern the repo''s `nextjs-page` skill documents. Paired with a `useQuery`, the\nserver renders the unloaded state and the client hydrates the loaded one, and\nReact discards the tree.\n\nAwaited, because each consumer branches on `isPending` and `DataTable` renders\na SkeletonLoader when `loading` is truthy:\n\n- `greidslur` (`getPayments`)\n- `stillingar/askrifendur` (`getSubscribers`)\n- `stillingar/ritstjorar` (`getUsers`)\n- lg-application-web `auglysingar/[type]/[id]` (`getApplicationAdvertPrice`,\n  and `getBaseEntities`, whose consumers build select options from\n  `baseEntities?.x ?? []` - the quiet version, an empty first paint rather than\n  a warning). `getBaseEntities` starts before the awaited `getApplicationById`\n  so the two still overlap.\n\nDropped rather than revived, because neither ever did anything:\n\n- `stillingar/tbr` prefetched `getTbrSettings({})` while the container queries\n  its own paging and filter defaults, and the page rendered no `HydrateClient`,\n  so nothing was dehydrated either way.\n- `ritstjorn` prefetched `getAdvertsCount({})` against a consumer that passes\n  the filter-context defaults. Reviving it would mean duplicating those\n  defaults into the page. Its sibling `getStatuses` prefetch is correct as-is\n  and stays unawaited - `PageContainer` reads that one through\n  `useSuspenseQuery`.\n\nAwaiting moves date rendering onto the server, and both affected tables\nformatted in the *runtime''s* timezone - `formatDate` is date-fns `format`, and\n`SubscribersTable` had a local `toLocaleDateString`. That would have traded a\nguaranteed mismatch for a timezone-dependent one: invisible in production, live\non any dev machine off GMT. So `formatDateInIceland` pins the wall clock to\nIceland, which observes GMT year-round. It is also the more correct reading -\nthese are Icelandic publication times, and a viewer abroad should not see them\nshifted into their own zone.\n\n`prefetch` returns its promise so callers can await it; `prefetchQuery` is\n`.then(noop).catch(noop)`, so awaiting cannot fail a render. `retry: false`\nstops a dead API holding a page through three backed-off retries.\n\n* fix(lg): give Select a stable id so it survives hydration\n\nAwaiting the prefetches server-renders tables that previously only ever\nappeared client-side, and that exposed a latent react-select bug in the page\nsize Select of every DataTable:\n\n  A tree hydrated but some attributes of the server rendered HTML didn''t\n  match the client properties. This won''t be patched up.\n  + id=\"react-select-3-live-region\"\n  - id=\"react-select-2-live-region\"\n\nreact-select falls back to an auto-incrementing counter for its internal\nelement ids when given no `instanceId`. island-ui takes `instanceId` from `id`,\nwhich defaults to `name` - and `DataTablePagination` passes neither, so the\ncounter decides. The counter depends on how many Selects the module has built,\nwhich differs between the server render and the client one.\n\nReact does not patch attribute mismatches up, so this is not only console\nnoise: the DOM keeps the server''s ids while `aria-labelledby` and\n`aria-activedescendant` point at the client''s, leaving the accessibility wiring\naimed at elements that do not exist.\n\nFixed in the shared wrapper rather than at the call site, because every\nconsumer that omits both `id` and `name` has the same latent bug - including\nthe OJOI selects, which spread props straight through. A caller that already\npasses `id` or `name` keeps exactly the id it had, so this only fills the gap\nwhere react-select was previously choosing.\n\n* fix(lg): treat an empty Select id as absent, not as an id\n\nreact-select resolves its element id prefix as `instanceId || ++counter`, so\nan empty string falls through to the counter exactly as `undefined` does. The\nfallback used `??`, which only catches null and undefined, so a Select handed\n`id=\"\"` or `name=\"\"` still got counter-derived ids and still mismatched on\nhydration.\n\nisland-ui''s own `Tabs` does precisely that - it renders an internal\n`<Select name={label} />`, and callers pass `label=\"\"`. That instance imports\nisland-ui''s Select directly rather than through this wrapper, so it is not\nfixed here, but any consumer of this wrapper with an empty id or name now is.\n\n* fix(lg): address review findings on the prefetch sweep\n\nThree real defects from review, all reproduced before fixing.\n\n`formatDateInIceland` was an hour off across the *viewer''s* DST transitions -\nthe one thing it exists to prevent. It nudged the instant by\n`getTimezoneOffset()` sampled at the original instant, but the shifted instant\ncan sit under a different offset. Under TZ=Europe/Berlin, 2026-03-29T01:30Z\nrendered 00:30 and 2026-10-25T01:30Z rendered 02:30. The single January fixture\nin the spec passed either way, so the PR''s claim that this was \"proven rather\nthan asserted\" was wrong.\n\nIt now reads the UTC fields directly - Iceland is GMT year-round, so those are\nthe Icelandic wall clock - and formats the date portion through a Date built at\nlocal noon, which is never inside a transition and so always survives the round\ntrip. The time portion never goes through a Date at all. The spec now covers\nboth Berlin transitions, a midnight boundary and Lord Howe''s 30-minute offset,\nand is run under four zones. Restoring the old implementation fails 5 of them\nunder Berlin while still passing all 87 under UTC - which is exactly why the\noriginal test missed it.\n\n`greidslur` prefetched `{ page, pageSize }` while the container keys on five\nvalues including `type`, `status` and `paid`. Unfiltered the keys hash equal\nbecause react-query drops undefined, but on any filtered URL they diverge - so\nthe page awaited a fetch of the unfiltered list and then discarded it, leaving\nthe user waiting longer for the same skeleton. It now parses with the existing\n`paymentsParamsCache`, which carries all five.\n\nAwaiting also removed the instant loading feedback on client-side navigation,\nsince this app has no `loading.tsx` at any level: a navigation held on the\nprevious page until the API answered. Each newly-blocking route gets a fallback\nmirroring the skeleton its container used to paint.\n\nAlso drops a now-pointless `async` on the tbr page and stops `SubscribersTable`\nparsing the same date string twice.\n\n* fix(lg): give ritstjorn''s Tabs a real label and correct the Select JSDoc\n\nThe JSDoc on the shared Select wrapper justified its truthiness check by citing\nisland-ui''s `Tabs`. That was wrong: `Tabs` imports Select straight from\nisland-ui/core, so this wrapper is not in that render path and the operator\ncannot help it. Left as written, the next person to hit the counter bug would\nread the comment and believe it was handled.\n\nReworded to say what is actually true - the empty-string arm guards this\nwrapper''s own callers, none of which pass an empty id or name today, so it is\ndefensive rather than load-bearing - and to point anyone with a `Tabs` problem\nat the real fix.\n\nWhich `ritstjorn` needed: it passed `label=\"\"`, and island-ui feeds that to the\ninternal react-select as `name`, which becomes `id` and then `instanceId`. Empty\nfalls through to `instanceId || ++counter`, and the counter differs between the\nserver and client renders. The Select is inside `hidden={... || showDesktopLayout}`\nand `showDesktopLayout` is false during SSR because `useWindowSize` has no\nwindow, so it really does reach the HTML.\n\nAlso drops a cast that claimed the sliced date-only format string was a member\nof the `dateFormats` union. It never was; that branch now calls date-fns\n`format` directly, which takes any format string.\n\n* docs(lg): say what the Tabs label does and does not fix\n\nThe comment implied the Select''s ids are now correct. They are deterministic,\nwhich removes the hydration mismatch, but they are not correct: island-ui''s\nTabs passes `name={label}` into a Select that defaults `id = name`, so the\nrendered id is the raw label string, space included. That is invalid for an\nHTML id, and `aria-labelledby` is an ID-reference list, so it parses as two\nids that do not exist. Not a regression - `label=\"\"` gave an equally useless\nempty reference - but the comment should not claim otherwise.\n\nIt also notes that island-ui renders this label as visible copy, not a\nscreen-reader string, above the Select the tab strip collapses into below the\ndesktop breakpoint. Both need an upstream island-ui fix: derive the Select''s id\nfrom a slug or `useId` rather than reusing the label.","tree":{"sha":"50c1b7ab04fe5144ee761a1271557418bb69e443","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/50c1b7ab04fe5144ee761a1271557418bb69e443"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/722c705d2999a00f0afa6d01ed69eba89040be51","verification":{"payload":"tree 50c1b7ab04fe5144ee761a1271557418bb69e443\nparent a99d51a944d8606cc8f3e347ae5b7ba45a4dbfdc\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1790000181 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790000181 +0000\n\nfix(lg): make prefetched pages hydrate consistently (#1530)\n\n* fix(lg): make prefetched pages hydrate consistently\n\nFollow-up sweep to #1529, which fixed `/flokkar-og-tegundir`. The same\npage/container mismatch was on five more Legal Gazette pages.\n\n`prefetch()` left unawaited streams the page out before the data lands. That\nonly works when the consumer reads through `useSuspenseQuery`, which is the\npattern the repo''s `nextjs-page` skill documents. Paired with a `useQuery`, the\nserver renders the unloaded state and the client hydrates the loaded one, and\nReact discards the tree.\n\nAwaited, because each consumer branches on `isPending` and `DataTable` renders\na SkeletonLoader when `loading` is truthy:\n\n- `greidslur` (`getPayments`)\n- `stillingar/askrifendur` (`getSubscribers`)\n- `stillingar/ritstjorar` (`getUsers`)\n- lg-application-web `auglysingar/[type]/[id]` (`getApplicationAdvertPrice`,\n  and `getBaseEntities`, whose consumers build select options from\n  `baseEntities?.x ?? []` - the quiet version, an empty first paint rather than\n  a warning). `getBaseEntities` starts before the awaited `getApplicationById`\n  so the two still overlap.\n\nDropped rather than revived, because neither ever did anything:\n\n- `stillingar/tbr` prefetched `getTbrSettings({})` while the container queries\n  its own paging and filter defaults, and the page rendered no `HydrateClient`,\n  so nothing was dehydrated either way.\n- `ritstjorn` prefetched `getAdvertsCount({})` against a consumer that passes\n  the filter-context defaults. Reviving it would mean duplicating those\n  defaults into the page. Its sibling `getStatuses` prefetch is correct as-is\n  and stays unawaited - `PageContainer` reads that one through\n  `useSuspenseQuery`.\n\nAwaiting moves date rendering onto the server, and both affected tables\nformatted in the *runtime''s* timezone - `formatDate` is date-fns `format`, and\n`SubscribersTable` had a local `toLocaleDateString`. That would have traded a\nguaranteed mismatch for a timezone-dependent one: invisible in production, live\non any dev machine off GMT. So `formatDateInIceland` pins the wall clock to\nIceland, which observes GMT year-round. It is also the more correct reading -\nthese are Icelandic publication times, and a viewer abroad should not see them\nshifted into their own zone.\n\n`prefetch` returns its promise so callers can await it; `prefetchQuery` is\n`.then(noop).catch(noop)`, so awaiting cannot fail a render. `retry: false`\nstops a dead API holding a page through three backed-off retries.\n\n* fix(lg): give Select a stable id so it survives hydration\n\nAwaiting the prefetches server-renders tables that previously only ever\nappeared client-side, and that exposed a latent react-select bug in the page\nsize Select of every DataTable:\n\n  A tree hydrated but some attributes of the server rendered HTML didn''t\n  match the client properties. This won''t be patched up.\n  + id=\"react-select-3-live-region\"\n  - id=\"react-select-2-live-region\"\n\nreact-select falls back to an auto-incrementing counter for its internal\nelement ids when given no `instanceId`. island-ui takes `instanceId` from `id`,\nwhich defaults to `name` - and `DataTablePagination` passes neither, so the\ncounter decides. The counter depends on how many Selects the module has built,\nwhich differs between the server render and the client one.\n\nReact does not patch attribute mismatches up, so this is not only console\nnoise: the DOM keeps the server''s ids while `aria-labelledby` and\n`aria-activedescendant` point at the client''s, leaving the accessibility wiring\naimed at elements that do not exist.\n\nFixed in the shared wrapper rather than at the call site, because every\nconsumer that omits both `id` and `name` has the same latent bug - including\nthe OJOI selects, which spread props straight through. A caller that already\npasses `id` or `name` keeps exactly the id it had, so this only fills the gap\nwhere react-select was previously choosing.\n\n* fix(lg): treat an empty Select id as absent, not as an id\n\nreact-select resolves its element id prefix as `instanceId || ++counter`, so\nan empty string falls through to the counter exactly as `undefined` does. The\nfallback used `??`, which only catches null and undefined, so a Select handed\n`id=\"\"` or `name=\"\"` still got counter-derived ids and still mismatched on\nhydration.\n\nisland-ui''s own `Tabs` does precisely that - it renders an internal\n`<Select name={label} />`, and callers pass `label=\"\"`. That instance imports\nisland-ui''s Select directly rather than through this wrapper, so it is not\nfixed here, but any consumer of this wrapper with an empty id or name now is.\n\n* fix(lg): address review findings on the prefetch sweep\n\nThree real defects from review, all reproduced before fixing.\n\n`formatDateInIceland` was an hour off across the *v