k1gHxZoFm\nKDD7swazgJxmyC2RmQa+PBEJa3cRKThQKwaAuOYF/3idcTaJdu55vgp5cvETci+e\numrvOf+Uhk6qCIDtPo/2\n=f5gz\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-18T12:16:46Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/7452473bdf65d12c52cc132a06c008d04b38a634","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKDc0NTI0NzNiZGY2NWQxMmM1MmNjMTMyYTA2YzAwOGQwNGIzOGE2MzQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/4df42226c5d90b6d266737bd6443ae25c9558076","sha":"4df42226c5d90b6d266737bd6443ae25c9558076","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/4df42226c5d90b6d266737bd6443ae25c9558076"}],"sha":"7452473bdf65d12c52cc132a06c008d04b38a634","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/7452473bdf65d12c52cc132a06c008d04b38a634"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/4df42226c5d90b6d266737bd6443ae25c9558076/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-18T11:57:45Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-18T11:57:45Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"ci(shared): cut quality.yml setup from 153s to 45s on the critical path (#1513)\n\n* ci(shared): let PRs restore the node_modules cache\n\nThe node_modules cache key carried the branch name and had no restore-keys,\nso every PR built a key that by construction had never existed. GitHub scopes\na cache to the branch that wrote it, so only pushes to main ever restored\nnode_modules; all six parallel quality.yml jobs paid a full yarn\nResolution -> Fetch -> Link on every PR, then ~20-25s each saving a ~330 MB\nentry nobody could read.\n\nMeasured on identical lockfiles, PR run 34956588995 vs main run 32485795485\n(constraints job both sides):\n\n                       PR (miss)   main (hit)\n  restore node_modules      0.3s        15.7s\n  yarn Resolution            19s         0.4s\n  yarn Link                  42s         8.9s\n  install step total       99.2s        55.6s\n  post-step cache save    20-25s           0s\n\nDropping the branch segment is enough on its own: the PR run''s key and main''s\nexisting entry shared the same lockfile hash 16bcefc6, so the miss was purely\nthe branch. restore-keys then covers PRs that do touch yarn.lock by relaxing\nthe lockfile hash, so they start from a near tree instead of nothing.\n\nThat fallback is only sound if `yarn install` reconciles a tree built from a\ndifferent lockfile. Verified rather than assumed, against c3002a50c..ea23b821a\n(the aws-sdk v2 removal, -149 lockfile entries, 2665 differing files): a tree\ninstalled at one commit, then installed again at the other under this action''s\nexact env and flags, is byte-identical to a clean install there -- 198239\nentries compared by path, size, exec bit, symlink target and SHA-1 content\nhash, zero differences, in both directions. node_modules/.yarn-state.yml, the\nledger the linker diffs against, travels inside the cache and was itself\nrewritten correctly. The comparison was canary-tested to confirm it detects a\nstray file, a missing file and a same-size content change.\n\nThe install-state cache loses the branch for the same reason but gets no\nrestore-keys: it is a resolution snapshot valid only for the lockfile that\nproduced it.\n\nSide effect worth having: 21 of the repo''s 24 node_modules cache entries were\nper-PR copies of an identical tree, holding the repo at its 10 GB cache cap\nwith ~24h of effective retention. PRs whose lockfile is unchanged now hit and\nwrite nothing.\n\n* ci(shared): fetch only the pinned commit of the island.is submodule\n\nThe tsc job checked out in 31s where every other quality.yml job takes 3-4s,\nand tsc is the critical path. The sparse-checkout step is not the cost -- that\nis 1.6s. 27.1s of it is actions/checkout cloning island.is in full because the\njob passes `submodules: true`; the main repo''s own `fetch-depth: 0` fetch is\n3.4s. Confirmed on three runs: 27.1s, 26.3s, 24.0s.\n\nisland.is is pinned to one commit and consumed through a sparse checkout of\nfive libs/ paths, so its history is never read in CI. Drop `submodules: true`\nand let checkout-submodules.sh fetch it at --depth 1 instead. Measured on one\nmachine back to back:\n\n  full clone                448.14 MiB   .git 491M\n  --depth 1                  45.96 MiB   .git  52M\n\n`fetch-depth: 0` stays on the main repo -- nrwl/nx-set-shas needs that history.\n\nTwo things measured on the way that are worth recording, since both look like\nimprovements and are not:\n\n--filter=blob:none through `git submodule update` is *worse* than no filter at\nall (87.41 MiB against 45.96 MiB). git checks out the default branch, then the\npinned SHA, and each checkout demand-fetches every blob in the tree because the\nsparse patterns are not set yet -- so the blobs are paid for twice. A blobless\nclone only pays (2.38 MiB) if the sparse patterns are in place before the first\ncheckout, and `git submodule update` gives no way to arrange that. Getting\nthere means hand-rolling the clone into the submodule path, which is not worth\n~3s of a ~227s critical path.\n\nOn a shallow clone `git log <sha>..origin/main` does not fail -- it silently\nlies. origin/main is grafted parentless, so the range degenerates to the tip\ncommit alone and --name-only then lists every file under the sparse paths as\n\"added\": exit 0, one commit, 977 paths, against a pin that is genuinely more\nthan 100 commits behind. The \"commits since\" listing is therefore skipped when\nthe clone is shallow, rather than printing a wrong answer. Local clones are\nunaffected: the shallow path is gated on CI and on the submodule not already\nexisting, so a developer keeps full history and a working listing.\n\n* ci(shared): drop --check-cache and scope yarn''s hardened mode to fork PRs\n\nTwo fixed costs paid by every job of every run, neither of which buys this\nrepo anything.\n\n--check-cache. Yarn''s own description: it \"will always refetch the packages\",\nand it \"is recommended as part of your CI workflow if you''re both following the\nZero-Installs model and accepting PRs from third-parties, as they''d otherwise\nhave the ability to alter the checked-in packages before submitting them\". We\ndo not follow Zero-Installs -- .yarn/cache is gitignored and no archive is\ntracked -- so the thing it protects does not exist here. It is why the Fetch\nstep measured ~18-20s whether every cache hit or every cache missed. Measured\nlocally, same lockfile and warm cache, back to back:\n\n  yarn install --immutable --check-cache    Fetch 30s 188ms\n  yarn install --immutable                  Fetch  1s 112ms\n\nThis does not weaken checksum validation: checksumBehavior already defaults to\n`throw`, so every install verifies package checksums against the lockfile.\n--immutable-cache is deliberately not used in its place -- it aborts if the\ncache folder would be modified, and the download cache restores through\nrestore-keys, so a PR that changes yarn.lock legitimately has to add archives\nto it. That flag would fail exactly the dependency-bump PRs.\n\nHardened mode. Yarn enables it automatically when it detects a pull request\nagainst a public repository (`enableHardenedMode` defaults to\n`isPR && isPublicRepository`) and says so in the log:\n\n  YN0000: Yarn detected that the current workflow is executed from a public\n          pull request. For safety the hardened mode has been enabled.\n\nIt forces --check-resolutions --refresh-lockfile, which queries the remote\nregistries to validate the lockfile. That is the whole of the gap between a PR\njob''s 18s Resolution step and a push-to-main job''s 0.4s -- not, as first\nassumed, anything to do with the install-state cache. Reproduced locally\nagainst a synthetic public-PR event:\n\n  hardened mode auto-on          Resolution 13s 319ms\n  YARN_ENABLE_HARDENED_MODE=0    Resolution  0s 243ms\n\ndmr.is is public because it is a government project, not because it takes\noutside contributions: every PR is opened from a branch in this repo, so its\nauthor already has write access and gains nothing by manipulating the lockfile.\nThe guard is kept for the case it is actually for, a PR opened from a fork, and\ndropped otherwise.\n\nWorth recording while it is in view: `yarn install` never uses the install\nstate to skip resolution -- install.ts calls\nrestoreInstallState({restoreResolutions: false}). That cache entry earns its\nkeep through linkersCustomData and build state, which is what moves the Link\nstep, not the Resolution step.\n\n* ci(shared): reword the submodule fetch comment for the depth-1 path\n\nThe guard''s stated reason was \"actions/checkout has already fetched\". It\nno longer does in quality.yml - `submodule update --init --depth 1` is\nwhat fetches the pin there - so say what the guard actually checks.\nBehaviour unchanged.\n\n* ci(shared): keep yarn''s hardened mode for PRs that touch the lockfile\n\nchecksumBehavior verifies a package against the checksum recorded in\nyarn.lock, so a diff that rewrites a resolution and its checksum\ntogether installs whatever it points at. --check-resolutions is what\ncatches that, and it only matters when the lockfile can have changed.\n\nDecide per run instead of per event: hardened for a fork PR or any PR\nwhose diff against its base touches yarn.lock or .yarnrc.yml, off for\nsame-repo PRs that leave both alone. Anything that stops the diff from\nbeing computed falls back to hardened. Dependabot''s PRs are same-repo\nbranches that touch the lockfile, so they stay hardened; every other PR\nstill saves the ~18s.\n\nDriven through all eight event shapes locally (push, workflow_dispatch,\nfork, deleted fork, no base SHA, unfetched base, clean same-repo,\nsame-repo with a lockfile diff).\n\n* ci(shared): fetch a drifted submodule pin directly on the shallow CI clone\n\n`submodule update` checks out the gitlink; the script then checks out\nconfig.json''s SHA. When the two drift, a full clone recovered via the\nexisting `fetch --prune`, but the depth-1 CI clone is shallow and\nsingle-branch, so that fetch honours its own boundary and a pin behind\nthe tip stays absent. `checkout` then died with \"unable to read tree\"\nunder set -e, failing tsc with an error naming neither cause.\n\nFetch the pinned commit itself when it is still missing after the\nbranch fetch - --depth 1 only when the clone is already shallow, so a\ndeveloper''s full clone is never turned shallow. Reproduced against a\ngitlink at island.is tip and config.json 297 commits behind: exit 128\nbefore, exit 0 with the pin checked out and the drift warning after.\nPlain bash 3.2 constructs, since git hooks run this under macOS /bin/bash.\n\n* ci(shared): put the --check-cache rationale back above the install step\n\nThe hardened-mode decide step was inserted between the --check-cache\ncomment and the install step it documents. Comments only; the action is\nbyte-identical with comments and blank lines stripped.","tree":{"sha":"79fa4906703218f425038d0b1766f9c07452061a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/79fa4906703218f425038d0b1766f9c07452061a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/4df42226c5d90b6d266737bd6443ae25c9558076","verification":{"payload":"tree 79fa4906703218f425038d0b1766f9c07452061a\nparent c36a740702a05d3d93764132f3a493fc74de1314\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1789732665 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789732665 +0000\n\nci(shared): cut quality.yml setup from 153s to 45s on the critical path (#1513)\n\n* ci(shared): let PRs restore the node_modules cache\n\nThe node_modules cache key carried the branch name and had no restore-keys,\nso every PR built a key that by construction had never existed. GitHub scopes\na cache to the branch that wrote it, so only pushes to main ever restored\nnode_modules; all six parallel quality.yml jobs paid a full yarn\nResolution -> Fetch -> Link on every PR, then ~20-25s each saving a ~330 MB\nentry nobody could read.\n\nMeasured on identical lockfiles, PR run 34956588995 vs main run 32485795485\n(constraints job both sides):\n\n                       PR (miss)   main (hit)\n  restore node_modules      0.3s        15.7s\n  yarn Resolution            19s         0.4s\n  yarn Link                  42s         8.9s\n  install step total       99.2s        55.6s\n  post-step cache save    20-25s           0s\n\nDropping the branch segment is enough on its own: the PR run''s key and main''s\nexisting entry shared the same lockfile hash 16bcefc6, so the miss was purely\nthe branch. restore-keys then covers PRs that do touch yarn.lock by relaxing\nthe lockfile hash, so they start from a near tree instead of nothing.\n\nThat fallback is only sound if `yarn install` reconciles a tree built from a\ndifferent lockfile. Verified rather than assumed, against c3002a50c..ea23b821a\n(the aws-sdk v2 removal, -149 lockfile entries, 2665 differing files): a tree\ninstalled at one commit, then installed again at the other under this action''s\nexact env and flags, is byte-identical to a clean install there -- 198239\nentries compared by path, size, exec bit, symlink target and SHA-1 content\nhash, zero differences, in both directions. node_modules/.yarn-state.yml, the\nledger the linker diffs against, travels inside the cache and was itself\nrewritten correctly. The comparison was canary-tested to confirm it detects a\nstray file, a missing file and a same-size content change.\n\nThe install-state cache loses the branch for the same reason but gets no\nrestore-keys: it is a resolution snapshot valid only for the lockfile that\nproduced it.\n\nSide effect worth having: 21 of the repo''s 24 node_modules cache entries were\nper-PR copies of an identical tree, holding the repo at its 10 GB cache cap\nwith ~24h of effective retention. PRs whose lockfile is unchanged now hit and\nwrite nothing.\n\n* ci(shared): fetch only the pinned commit of the island.is submodule\n\nThe tsc job checked out in 31s where every other quality.yml job takes 3-4s,\nand tsc is the critical path. The sparse-checkout step is not the cost -- that\nis 1.6s. 27.1s of it is actions/checkout cloning island.is in full because the\njob passes `submodules: true`; the main repo''s own `fetch-depth: 0` fetch is\n3.4s. Confirmed on three runs: 27.1s, 26.3s, 24.0s.\n\nisland.is is pinned to one commit and consumed through a sparse checkout of\nfive libs/ paths, so its history is never read in CI. Drop `submodules: true`\nand let checkout-submodules.sh fetch it at --depth 1 instead. Measured on one\nmachine back to back:\n\n  full clone                448.14 MiB   .git 491M\n  --depth 1                  45.96 MiB   .git  52M\n\n`fetch-depth: 0` stays on the main repo -- nrwl/nx-set-shas needs that history.\n\nTwo things measured on the way that are worth recording, since both look like\nimprovements and are not:\n\n--filter=blob:none through `git submodule update` is *worse* than no filter at\nall (87.41 MiB against 45.96 MiB). git checks out the default branch, then the\npinned SHA, and each checkout demand-fetches every blob in the tree because the\nsparse patterns are not set yet -- so the blobs are paid for twice. A blobless\nclone only pays (2.38 MiB) if the sparse patterns are in place before the first\ncheckout, and `git submodule update` gives no way to arrange that. Getting\nthere means hand-rolling the clone into the submodule path, which is not worth\n~3s of a ~227s critical path.\n\nOn a shallow clone `git log <sha>..origin/main` does not fail -- it silently\nlies. origin/main is grafted parentless, so the range degenerates to the tip\ncommit alone and --name-only then lists every file under the sparse paths as\n\"added\": exit 0, one commit, 977 paths, against a pin that is genuinely more\nthan 100 commits behind. The \"commits since\" listing is therefore skipped when\nthe clone is shallow, rather than printing a wrong answer. Local clones are\nunaffected: the shallow path is gated on CI and on the submodule not already\nexisting, so a developer keeps full history and a working listing.\n\n* ci(shared): drop --check-cache and scope yarn''s hardened mode to fork PRs\n\nTwo fixed costs paid by every job of every run, neither of which buys this\nrepo anything.\n\n--check-cache. Yarn''s own description: it \"will always refetch the packages\",\nand it \"is recommended as part of your CI workflow if you''re both following the\nZero-Installs model and accepting PRs from third-parties, as they''d otherwise\nhave the ability to alter the checked-in packages before submitting them\". We\ndo not follow Zero-Installs -- .yarn/cache is gitignored and no archive is\ntracked -- so the thing it protects does not exist here. It is why the Fetch\nstep measured ~18-20s whether every cache hit or every cache missed. Measured\nlocally, same lockfile and warm cache, back to back:\n\n  yarn install --immutable --check-cache    Fetch 30s 188ms\n  yarn install --immutable                  Fetch  1s 112ms\n\nThis does not weaken checksum validation: checksumBehavior already defaults to\n`throw`, so every install verifies package checksums against the lockfile.\n--immutable-cache is deliberately not used in its place -- it aborts if the\ncache folder would be modified, and the download cache restores through\nrestore-keys, so a PR that changes yarn.lock legitimately has to add archives\nto it. That flag would fail exactly the dependency-bump PRs.\n\nHardened mode. Yarn enables it automatically when it detects a pull request\nagainst a public repository (`enableHardenedMode` defaults to\n`isPR && isPublicRepository`) and says so in the log:\n\n  YN0000: Yarn detected that the current workflow is executed from a public\n          pull request. For safety the hardened mode has been enabled.\n\nIt forces --check-resolutions --refresh-lockfile, which queries the remote\nregistries to validate the lockfile. That is the whole of the gap between a PR\njob''s 18s Resolution step and a push-to-main job''s 0.4s -- not, as first\nassumed, anything to do with the install-state cache. Reproduced locally\nagainst a synthetic public-PR event:\n\n  hardened mode auto-on          Resolution 13s 319ms\n  YARN_ENABLE_HARDENED_MODE=0    Resolution  0s 243ms\n\ndmr.is is public because it is a government project, not because it takes\noutside contributions: every PR is opened from a branch in this repo, so its\nauthor already has write access and gains nothing by manipulating the lockfile.\nThe guard is kept for the case it is actually for, a PR opened from a fork, and\ndropped otherwise.\n\nWorth recording while it is in view: `yarn install` never uses the install\nstate to skip resolution -- install.ts calls\nrestoreInstallState({restoreResolutions: false}). That cache entry earns its\nkeep through linkersCustomData and build state, which is what moves the Link\nstep, not the Resolution step.\n\n* ci(shared): reword the submodule fetch comment for the depth-1 path\n\nThe guard''s stated reason was \"actions/checkout has already fetched\". It\nno longer does in quality.yml - `submodule update --init --depth 1` is\nwhat fetches the pin there - so say what the guard actually checks.\nBehaviour unchanged.\n\n* ci(shared): keep yarn''s hardened mode for PRs that touch the lockfile\n\nchecksumBehavior verifies a package against the checksum recorded in\nyarn.lock, so a diff that rewrites a resolution and its checksum\ntogether installs whatever it points at. --check-resolutions is what\ncatches that, and it only matters when the lockfile can have changed.\n\nDecide per run instead of per event: hardened for a fork PR or any PR\nwhose diff against its base touches yarn.lock or .yarnrc.yml, off for\nsame-repo PRs that leave both alone. Anything that stops the diff from\nbeing computed falls back to hardened. Dependabot''s PRs are same-repo\nbranches that touch the lockfile, so they stay hardened; every other PR\nstill saves the ~18s.\n\nDriven through all eight event shapes locally (push, workflow_dispatch,\nfork, deleted fork, no base SHA, unfetched base, clean same-repo,\nsame-repo with a lockfile diff).\n\n* ci(shared): fetch a drifted submodule pin directly on the shallow CI clone\n\n`submodule update` checks out the gitlink; the script then checks out\nconfig.json''s SHA. When the two drift, a full clone recovered via the\nexisting `fetch --prune`, but the depth-1 CI clone is shallow and\nsingle-branch, so that fetch honours its own boundary and a pin behind\nthe tip stays absent. `checkout` then died with \"unable to read tree\"\nunder set -e, failing tsc with an error naming neither cause.\n\nFetch the pinned commit itself when it is still missing after the\nbranch fetch - --depth 1 only when the clone is already shallow, so a\ndeveloper''s full clone is never turned shallow. Reproduced against a\ngitlink at island.is tip and config.json 297 commits behind: exit 128\nbefore, exit 0 with the pin checked out and the drift warning after.\nPlain bash 3.2 constructs, since git hooks run this under macOS /bin/bash.\n\n* ci(shared): put the --check-cache rationale back above the install step\n\nThe hardened-mode decide step was inserted between the --check-cache\ncomment and the install step it documents. Comments only; the action is\nbyte-identical with comments and blank lines stripped.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqrSc5CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAyM0QABVejCEPPCXko/zHbJ5HiK/E\n7PwvWg+nQDLBEhF6IS6MTi7mf+88UEzP9+fSPIcIzqrhl8NIyg//59xywSnITm7w\nmaUBnog8uug9Sa48eBjZJhpBwxwEbfziam54i7EgJQVnpxkaQAbUbRbhzkGOfqrb\nCkohv7NpNGFYf2EjsNkeWd6IAVfvwHU3PfEv/tlGu9wbT44F6lnOjG55KDREn3eu\nS64uF4/iGOMyVthHQXK1CFXljdBo11FypJ0Ahh+d4996dNvripTswG+F8y/BIvF1\nU9kTJ1Saog9uofijTaI86ip0Em5522W6Y5i5q3exOZ66IIDO8xRUBBwL698rUV9V\nQOZ0Fe7kjpG6g0aCmOwNJPNG5JdXYVMvVKq0NwqBg3cAZ0PpT6wKTWpXofw3b6Cr\nxFsc6o23Y2eu8zTwI6m9YyoL4Z8gYVZnKj0W14M3NpGYbjQwT6aKLYcttzvcKrGM\n7E3Uc5ct+TGHdgXvi9joTs1QDTdSEIT7sLGPMs5wv/gPX0az+HHNUy0nQiPKn1V0\nGAGhcQEILKi5JZJKtPZF2YLZmYe3XSKA4JRcS5+RQlR19b