ived_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/e897336f4da5ceb8ab03da882261f4f32fdca02b","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKGU4OTczMzZmNGRhNWNlYjhhYjAzZGE4ODIyNjFmNGYzMmZkY2EwMmI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/7f950cd6fb6f1457e8dd77c9ebc36639bfa7c1a6","sha":"7f950cd6fb6f1457e8dd77c9ebc36639bfa7c1a6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/7f950cd6fb6f1457e8dd77c9ebc36639bfa7c1a6"}],"sha":"e897336f4da5ceb8ab03da882261f4f32fdca02b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/e897336f4da5ceb8ab03da882261f4f32fdca02b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/7f950cd6fb6f1457e8dd77c9ebc36639bfa7c1a6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:49:31Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:49:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(doe): load the whole company register in the report drawers (#1517)\n\n* fix(doe): load the whole company register in the report drawers\n\nThe company picker in the \"Ný jafnréttisáætlun\" and salary report drawers\nlisted companies only down to the letter \"L\", so an admin could not file on\nbehalf of any company later in the alphabet.\n\nBoth drawers fetched a single page capped at `pageSize: 1000`. The API orders\nthat query by name ascending (`company.service.ts` falls to `order = [[''name'',\nsortDir]]` when no `sortBy` is sent), and nothing between the drawer and SQL\nclamps or raises the value — so the request became `ORDER BY name ASC LIMIT\n1000`. With ~1500 companies in the register, and the drawers deliberately\nwidening it further via `includeNotObliged` and `includeInactive`, the cut\nlanded around two thirds through the alphabet. Typing could not reach the\nmissing rows either: the field has no `q` param and react-select filters only\nwhat is already in memory.\n\nReplace the capped single request with a sweep that pages to completion, driven\nby the `paging.totalPages` the endpoint already returns. No hardcoded ceiling\nsurvives, so the selector cannot silently truncate again as the register grows.\n`PAGE_SIZE` only trades request count against payload size; at today''s size the\nsweep is three requests, which `httpBatchLink` sends as one HTTP call.\n\nThe sweep lives in a new `useAllCompanies` hook shared by both drawers, which\nalso removes the duplicated option mapping and inclusion-flag comment. The\nregister page''s own `useCompanies` is untouched — it paginates on purpose.\n\nNo API, DTO or guard changes.\n\n* refactor(doe): fetch the register through trpc queryOptions, not the raw client\n\nFollows the app''s existing convention. `useAllCompanies` now goes through\n`useTRPC()` + `trpc.company.list.queryOptions()` with two ordinary `useQuery`\ncalls, matching `useCompanies`, `useReports` and `useReviewers`. The previous\nversion reached for `useTRPCClient` and a hand-written `queryFn`, which bypassed\nthe query layer every other hook in the app goes through.\n\nA probe page reads `paging.totalItems` — the server''s own count of the filtered\nregister — and a second query, gated by `enabled`, asks for exactly that many\nrows. The size still comes from the server, so no hardcoded ceiling returns.\n\n`lib/trpc/client/trpc.ts` is reverted to its original contents; `useTRPCClient`\nis no longer needed.\n\nWhile the full fetch is in flight the hook returns an empty list rather than\nfalling back to the probe''s page, so a truncated list is never shown.\n\nTrade-off: the first 500 rows are fetched twice when the register exceeds one\npage, in exchange for staying on the conventional query path.\n\n* fix(doe): surface a failed company fetch in both report drawers\n\n`useAllCompanies` already computed `isError`, but neither drawer consumed it.\nOn a failed fetch the picker rendered empty with `isLoading` false and no\nmessage — visually identical to a register that genuinely has no companies.\n\nThis matters more since the hook deliberately refuses to fall back to the\nprobe''s page: a failure of the second, larger request empties the list even\nthough the first 500 rows arrived fine. That no-fallback rule is correct —\nshowing a truncated list is the bug being fixed — so the answer is to say what\nhappened rather than show a partial one.\n\nBoth drawers now render an inline error `AlertMessage` next to the selector,\nmatching how the equality drawer already surfaces PDF errors and how the salary\ndrawer surfaces a missing equality report.\n\nRaised in review of #1517.","tree":{"sha":"be1c453ddf0ae5b3006b44577e05690e7aeef375","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/be1c453ddf0ae5b3006b44577e05690e7aeef375"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/7f950cd6fb6f1457e8dd77c9ebc36639bfa7c1a6","verification":{"payload":"tree be1c453ddf0ae5b3006b44577e05690e7aeef375\nparent 8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1789483771 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789483771 +0000\n\nfix(doe): load the whole company register in the report drawers (#1517)\n\n* fix(doe): load the whole company register in the report drawers\n\nThe company picker in the \"Ný jafnréttisáætlun\" and salary report drawers\nlisted companies only down to the letter \"L\", so an admin could not file on\nbehalf of any company later in the alphabet.\n\nBoth drawers fetched a single page capped at `pageSize: 1000`. The API orders\nthat query by name ascending (`company.service.ts` falls to `order = [[''name'',\nsortDir]]` when no `sortBy` is sent), and nothing between the drawer and SQL\nclamps or raises the value — so the request became `ORDER BY name ASC LIMIT\n1000`. With ~1500 companies in the register, and the drawers deliberately\nwidening it further via `includeNotObliged` and `includeInactive`, the cut\nlanded around two thirds through the alphabet. Typing could not reach the\nmissing rows either: the field has no `q` param and react-select filters only\nwhat is already in memory.\n\nReplace the capped single request with a sweep that pages to completion, driven\nby the `paging.totalPages` the endpoint already returns. No hardcoded ceiling\nsurvives, so the selector cannot silently truncate again as the register grows.\n`PAGE_SIZE` only trades request count against payload size; at today''s size the\nsweep is three requests, which `httpBatchLink` sends as one HTTP call.\n\nThe sweep lives in a new `useAllCompanies` hook shared by both drawers, which\nalso removes the duplicated option mapping and inclusion-flag comment. The\nregister page''s own `useCompanies` is untouched — it paginates on purpose.\n\nNo API, DTO or guard changes.\n\n* refactor(doe): fetch the register through trpc queryOptions, not the raw client\n\nFollows the app''s existing convention. `useAllCompanies` now goes through\n`useTRPC()` + `trpc.company.list.queryOptions()` with two ordinary `useQuery`\ncalls, matching `useCompanies`, `useReports` and `useReviewers`. The previous\nversion reached for `useTRPCClient` and a hand-written `queryFn`, which bypassed\nthe query layer every other hook in the app goes through.\n\nA probe page reads `paging.totalItems` — the server''s own count of the filtered\nregister — and a second query, gated by `enabled`, asks for exactly that many\nrows. The size still comes from the server, so no hardcoded ceiling returns.\n\n`lib/trpc/client/trpc.ts` is reverted to its original contents; `useTRPCClient`\nis no longer needed.\n\nWhile the full fetch is in flight the hook returns an empty list rather than\nfalling back to the probe''s page, so a truncated list is never shown.\n\nTrade-off: the first 500 rows are fetched twice when the register exceeds one\npage, in exchange for staying on the conventional query path.\n\n* fix(doe): surface a failed company fetch in both report drawers\n\n`useAllCompanies` already computed `isError`, but neither drawer consumed it.\nOn a failed fetch the picker rendered empty with `isLoading` false and no\nmessage — visually identical to a register that genuinely has no companies.\n\nThis matters more since the hook deliberately refuses to fall back to the\nprobe''s page: a failure of the second, larger request empties the list even\nthough the first 500 rows arrived fine. That no-fallback rule is correct —\nshowing a truncated list is the bug being fixed — so the answer is to say what\nhappened rather than show a partial one.\n\nBoth drawers now render an inline error `AlertMessage` next to the selector,\nmatching how the equality drawer already surfaces PDF errors and how the salary\ndrawer surfaces a missing equality report.\n\nRaised in review of #1517.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqVr7CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAe6wQAI4lJB5SLACLbhJq5TYiUlRS\nBwL45W6oisTx4uCE4Q3BmVALKWTiv1usVreFJSzo4Y6mdX2+aqjKBD2+u3hOKBGp\nwp9MbFtPIr1bvA1OdUPt7AVYFvhbwVWv16PMqsQGXlzJ5ZOknZugetejGTSAdYcf\npLOVfojT+bWf8xaFPz77MnCrWCWVkRG6bi3J4gruj3x4srnWpF9NeKZz3tvXzkzo\nM2upICHns/38FwF+nEu/7S2vKG7Nqmera4KjIYTFAyjU4iQZTK9Nr1hjT+PYIM50\nwyxOo/PZYJzXuYDr1PFBjDj7lQmfiCh8PdGjwjhXvh7Cx3Ey0pj8csZbDAJFULVI\nq94EKDOiV3kIU8/wPemhRUT05pfhBzM9m35wm35Ol/TBSOwx4zLdV7kqeoYTdsO8\nxSQseFvB4SyfN9mHL9GwkLpRtIVnaDhauBEVG0QFwO6OBznzi8BzWqwNeDsYIW7i\nJ6zx4ADqNyPzyDjXpFvkqiuPjaixxHGJ0q7tfXCYilJN8ibRWTYFKtUVqvaAPrsn\nvQOCXakvONMqwysThIKMpri9inNanS9jfxIgLNJR21Are8az2RUy3E9cb1VGxGIg\nuTzziawXcWqBLBNlLE29C0KvT3M5q7b+2lV38tWkLOWLLJqc3nSKz5xWLnVXZAvs\nTuR/89SDG9EbJW72K8lR\n=lMR2\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T14:49:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/7f950cd6fb6f1457e8dd77c9ebc36639bfa7c1a6","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKDdmOTUwY2Q2ZmI2ZjE0NTdlOGRkNzdjOWViYzM2NjM5YmZhN2MxYTY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b","sha":"8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b"}],"sha":"7f950cd6fb6f1457e8dd77c9ebc36639bfa7c1a6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/7f950cd6fb6f1457e8dd77c9ebc36639bfa7c1a6"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/92530555?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/jonbjarnio","id":92530555,"login":"jonbjarnio","node_id":"U_kgDOBYPnew","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jonbjarnio","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T13:06:14Z","email":"dev.jonbjarni@gmail.com","name":"Jón Bjarni Ólafsson"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T13:06:14Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(doe): file salary reports against a stored scoring model (#1508)\n\n* feat(doe): give the partner API a company-scoped scoring model\nEvery salary report re-transmits its whole scoring model today: the criteria\ntree, every step description, and one assignment per role x job-based\nsub-criterion, all bound together by title strings. Nothing is company-scoped —\nno model carries a company_id — so a company that files annually sends the same\nstarfsmat every year.\nThat is survivable on the island.is surface, which has a UI to build it in, and\non the Excel surface, where the workbook carries it. It is not survivable on the\npartner API: a payroll vendor holds none of it and would have to build screens\nto collect the employer''s starfsmat before it could file anything.\nThese tables give the model a home. Authored once, referenced by id. Nothing\nexisting changes — the report_* criteria tree stays the per-report frozen\nsnapshot, which is what keeps a filed report interpretable after the company\nreworks its model, and is why these tables carry no version.\nTwo columns are deliberately absent. scoring_criterion has no weight: a\ncriterion''s weight is the sum of its own sub-criteria''s, and computeStepScore\nreads only the sub-criterion weight, so a stored criterion weight reaches no\nscore and can only disagree with the figures that do — today a criterion can\ndisplay 40% while its sub-criteria account for 10% of what actually scores,\nbecause the two sums are each checked against 100 and never against each other.\nDeriving it collapses the two weight rules into one. scoring_sub_criterion_step\nhas no score: it is (stepOrder / numSteps) x weight x SCORE_FACTOR, and asking a\ncaller to send it invites a vendor to file stig on a scale of their own.\nWrites may leave the model incomplete and still succeed. A model is built over\nmany calls and its weights do not reach 100 until the last sub-criterion lands,\nso refusing every intermediate state would make it impossible to author one.\nEvery response instead carries `validation` — status plus every reason at once,\nnot just the first — and the filing is what refuses. VALID means the model is\ncomplete, never that the next filing will succeed: two of the submission''s rules\nneed the filing''s employees and cannot be decided here.\nStep contiguity is validated because the derived score makes it load-bearing:\nwith orders 1, 2 and 5 the scale has three steps and the last scores 5/3 of its\nown maximum.\nThe new scoring:write scope is separate from the submit scopes because it is a\ndifferent act by a possibly different party — a vendor that only files needs\nreport:read to reference a model somebody else authored, and never this.\n\n* fix(doe): make the scoring model''s PATCH and list order behave\nTwo defects the first manual pass turned up.\n**PATCH refused every partial body.** The update DTOs declared their fields with\n`@ApiString({ required: false })`, which relaxes the published document and\nnothing else — class-validator still demanded them, so `{\"weight\": 30}` came\nback 400 with \"title must be a string\". The `ApiOptional*` decorators are the\nones that carry both halves. PATCH now does what its own service comment always\nsaid it did: an omitted key is left alone.\n**The tree came back in arbitrary order.** Nothing ordered the includes, so\nPostgres returned criteria and sub-criteria however it liked and the order\nshifted between identical calls — a caller rendering the list would watch it\nreshuffle. Ordered by insertion now. Steps stay ordered by `stepOrder`, which is\ntheir own meaning rather than an arbitrary tie-break.\n\n* docs(doe): put the scoring model''s contract in the published document\nThe idea the whole surface is built around — that a write succeeds even when it\nleaves the model incomplete, and the filing is what refuses — was written only\nin the controller''s class docstring. Nest does not emit class-level JSDoc into\nSwagger, so none of it reached the document an integrator actually reads: they\nsaw `validation: { status, reasons }` with no description on either field and\nnothing to suggest that a 201 carrying INVALID is the normal intermediate state\nrather than a failed call.\nFour gaps closed, all in the schema where a reader cannot miss them:\n- `status` carries the writes-succeed contract, and the warning that VALID means\n  the model is complete and *not* that the next filing will succeed — naming the\n  two employee-dependent rules that cannot be decided without a filing. That is\n  the likeliest way for an integration to get burned: model reads VALID, filing\n  400s, and nothing warned them.\n- `scope` documents all five enum values. They shipped as bare strings, so a\n  vendor routing reasons into their own UI had to guess what ROLE_ASSIGNMENTS\n  covered.\n- `message` says the text is Icelandic and written for the employer who has to\n  fix it, rather than for the integrator — it can be shown as-is.\n- `reasons` gains a worked example, and says it carries every reason at once.\nThe guide is deliberately untouched: it has no scoring-model section yet, and a\nwalkthrough written now could not reach VALID, since steps and roles have no\nendpoints. It gets written when the surface is complete.\n\n* test(doe): cover the scoring model''s rules and its ownership gate\nThe slice shipped with no specs, which left the validator — seven rules that\ndecide whether a model may file — held up by nothing but a manual pass.\n`validate-scoring-model.spec.ts` pins each rule and, more usefully, the things\nabout them that are easy to get wrong later:\n- sub-criterion weights total 100 **across the whole model**, not within each\n  criterion, so a model at 40/20/20/20 is valid though no criterion is at 100.\n  This is the rule most likely to be \"corrected\" into a per-criterion check.\n- the mandatory requirement is on the criterion *type*, so two criteria of one\n  type are fine and only an absent type is a reason.\n- a role must be assigned on every job-based sub-criterion and never on a\n  personal one, which is scored per employee.\n- step orders must be contiguous from 1, because the score is\n  (stepOrder / numSteps) x weight x SCORE_FACTOR — orders 1, 2, 5 give a scale\n  of three whose last step scores 5/3 of its own maximum. Out-of-sequence but\n  contiguous orders are fine; the sort is not the contract.\n- an empty model answers with all five reasons at once rather than the first,\n  and the list stays bounded.\n`scoring-model.service.spec.ts` covers the ownership gate (scoped by companyId\nin the query, so another company''s model is indistinguishable from a missing\none), criterion weight derived from its own sub-criteria, steps ordered by\nstepOrder, PATCH leaving omitted keys alone, and a write succeeding where it\nleaves the model unfit to file.\n\n* feat(doe): let a scoring model''s scales be written, whole\nSteps (þrep) hang off a sub-criterion and nothing else — a criterion is a label\nwith no scale of its own — so this is the last structural piece before roles.\n**A scale is replaced whole, not edited a step at a time.** Both reasons are\nstructural rather than stylistic. A þrep''s score is\n`(stepOrder / numSteps) x weight x SCORE_FACTOR`, so the orders must run 1..n\nwith no gaps; a caller adding one step at a time necessarily passes through\nstates where they do not, and no single call can repair that. And a scale has a\nminimum length, so the first step of a new one would always be invalid alone.\nTaking the array whole makes both impossible instead of merely detected: order\ncomes from position, so there is no order field to send and no gap to make.\nReplacing drops any role step assignment onto the old scale, through the FK\ncascade. That is deliberate and differs from the draft module, which re-homes\nassignments onto surviving siblings — it does that because a UI must not lose a\nuser''s work mid-edit. Here the caller is a program rebuilding the model, and a\ndropped assignment surfaces immediately as \"this job is missing an assignment\"\nrather than being silently re-pointed at a step nobody chose.\nAlso closes a gap the step work exposed: the validator never checked\nMIN_STEPS/MAX_STEPS, which `assertParsedPayloadIntegrity` enforces on every\nfiling path. A scale of one step, or of twelve, read VALID here and would have\nbeen refused at submit — precisely the \"previews clean, rejected at submit\"\nfailure this validation exists to prevent. Verified live: 1 and 9 steps both\nreport the bound, 2 and 8 pass.\n\n* feat(doe): give a scoring model its jobs, and their step assignments\nThe last structural piece. A model can now reach VALID: four mandatory criterion\ntypes, sub-criteria totalling 100, a scale on each, and a job assigned across\nall of them — verified end to end against a running server.\nJobs are ordinary CRUD; their **step assignments are written whole**, for the\nreason a scale is. Exactly one assignment per job per sub-criterion is a\nuniqueness the table itself holds, so a partial write cannot move a job from one\nþrep to another without deleting first, and every partial write risks colliding\nwith the constraint. Taking the set whole makes the uniqueness structural.\nThe split between what is reported and what is refused matters, and is the\ndistinction the whole surface turns on:\n- **Incomplete** — a job assigned on two of four sub-criteria — is accepted and\n  reported, like every other incompleteness here. The model is mid-authoring.\n- **Incoherent** — a step belonging to a different sub-criterion, a\n  sub-criterion outside the model, a personal sub-criterion (scored per\n  employee, never per job), or the same sub-criterion twice — is refused with a\n  400. None of those describe a model that could exist, so storing them would\n  persist a row nothing could ever score.\nValidation runs before the destroy, so a refused call leaves the previous\nassignments intact; confirmed live, the model stayed VALID through all four\nrefusals.\nAlso fixes an ambiguity the first complete run exposed: reasons named a\nsub-criterion by its own title, and those are not unique — a model whose criteria\neach hold a \"Menntun\" produced four identical \"vantar úthlutun fyrir „Menntun“\"\nlines naming nothing actionable. They now carry the parent, in the same\n`Ábyrgð / Mannaforráð` form the submission validator uses.\n\n* docs(doe): drop the X-Road contrast from the partner API description\nThe line explained this surface by naming what it is not, which only helps a\nreader who already knows the island.is channel exists — and the integrator this\ndocument is written for does not. What remains says what the API is and who it\nis for.\n\n* fix(doe): carry the scoring model onto the merged main\nTwo things #1483 changed underneath this branch.\n**The models no longer loaded under @swc/jest.** #1500 moved doe-modules and the\npartner API off ts-jest, and swc does not tolerate the circular import between a\nparent model and its child: `emitDecoratorMetadata` writes `design:type`\nreferencing the class, which is evaluated while the module is still\ninitialising, so the child threw `Cannot access ''ScoringSubCriterionModel''\nbefore initialization` at load. The repo already has the answer in\n`postcode.model.ts` — import the parent twice, a type-only alias for the\nproperty annotation and the value import for the decorator''s arrow, so the\nannotation never forces an eager reference. Applied to all five child models.\nArray-typed associations are unaffected: `design:type` for those is `Array`.\n**The scoring surface did not check the register.** `RequireActiveCompanyGuard`\nbecame part of `declaresAccess` in #1483, so the coverage spec now fails a chain\nthat authenticates without it — and it correctly failed for this controller,\nwhich had neither the decorator nor the guard. That is a real gap rather than a\nspec technicality: a company that has fallen off Jafnréttisstofa''s register\ncould author a starfsmat it would never be allowed to file against, which is\nprecisely the half-working surface the one-answer-everywhere decision rejected.\n\n* feat(doe): expand a stored scoring model into the payload the pipeline takes\nThe seam the whole exercise was for. A stored model plus a payroll extract\nbecomes exactly the `ParsedReportDto` the submission pipeline already receives,\nso validation, scoring, outlier detection, storage, the per-report snapshot and\nevery admin read are untouched and cannot fork per channel. What the partner\nsurface stops doing is asking a vendor to transmit the criteria tree and the\nrole matrix — the company''s, unchanged since last year, and not data a payroll\nsystem holds.\nTwo translations live here, both because the pipeline addresses by **title**\nwhile a stored model addresses by **id**.\nStep scores are derived rather than carried, through the same `computeStepScore`\nthe workbook parser uses, so a report filed on this channel sits on the same\nstig scale as one filed on any other. Asking a vendor for the number invited a\nreport scored on a scale of their own, which nothing downstream validates.\nIds become titles, and that is where the expansion refuses rather than\ntranslates: a job or sub-criterion outside the model, a step belonging to a\ndifferent sub-criterion, a personal assignment onto a job-based sub-criterion\n(scored per job, never per employee). None describe a filing that could exist.\nIt also refuses a model holding two sub-criteria with the same\n`(criterionTitle, subTitle)` pair, because the pipeline keys on exactly that and\nwould collapse them onto one row. Titles are unconstrained, so this is\nreachable — and the model validator now reports it as a reason too, since\nwithout that the model reads VALID and only the expansion refuses it, at filing.\nThat is the previews-clean-rejected-at-submit failure this validation exists to\nprevent, and it is the third instance of it we have found.\n`PartnerEmployeeDto` is the slim shape: fifteen payroll fields plus `roleId` and\n`personalSteps`. Everything but `personalSteps` is data a payroll system holds;\nthat one is the employer''s assessment, around a tenth of the weight, and is why\na vendor still needs somewhere to collect input — much less of one than a\nstarfsmat editor.\n\n* feat(doe): file a salary report against a stored scoring model\nThe seam, wired in. `SubmitPartnerSalaryReportDto` loses `parsed` and gains\n`scoringModelId` plus a flat `employees[]`; the analysis route takes the same\npair. The criteria tree, the þrep and the job step assignments stop crossing the\nwire entirely — they are the employer''s, unchanged since the last filing, and a\npayroll system does not hold them. A vendor now sends what it actually has.\n`PartnerSubmissionService` does the translation and nothing else, and it lives\nin the partner app rather than beside `ApplicationService` deliberately: putting\nit there would make `ApplicationCoreModule` import the scoring model and pull it\ninto island.is''s module graph for a concept that channel does not have. The same\nreasoning took `ReportExcelCoreModule` out of the shared module when the\nworkbook surface was dropped. Past the expansion it is the identical code every\nother channel runs — the submission rules, the equality gate, idempotent replay\nand event emission are not reachable from here and cannot fork per channel.\nThe preview expands through the same call as the submission, so a payload that\nanalyses clean is the payload that gets filed. Building the analysis tree any\nother way would have it answer a question the submission never asks, which is\nthe failure mode this whole round has kept finding.\n\n* docs(doe): bring the integrator guide and the ER diagram up to the scoring model\nThe guide still described the flow where a vendor builds the criteria tree into\nevery submission — it and the API had been disagreeing since the seam landed,\nand it is the document an integrator actually onboards from.\nSection B is now the payroll extract rather than the payload: what you send is a\nscoring model id and one row per employee, and the tree, the þrep and the job\nassignments do not cross the wire at all. B4 and B5 collapse into one step,\nbecause fetching the catalog is no longer part of filing — it is part of\nauthoring a model, which happens once. The steps renumber accordingly and the\nroute table gains the fifteen scoring-model endpoints.\nNew section C covers the model itself, and leads with whose work it is: not the\nvendor''s unless the employer asked, since authoring one is choosing\nsub-criteria, distributing weight (a policy decision about how a company values\nwork) and writing þrep descriptions. It states the rules a model must satisfy —\nincluding that sub-criterion weights total 100 across the *whole* model rather\nthan per criterion, which is the one most likely to be misread — and explains\nwhy writes succeed while a model is incomplete, and why VALID does not promise\nthe next filing succeeds.\nThe ER diagram gains the six `scoring_*` tables. The note above it says why they\nsit apart from the report graph with no FK back from a report: a filing\nmaterialises its own frozen copy, which is what lets a company rework its\nstarfsmat without moving the figures on a report already filed, and is why the\nmodel carries no version.\n\n* docs(doe): document the scoring tables in the db README\n`DIAGRAM.md` points here for full column lists, so the six `scoring_*` tables\nbeing absent left the diagram half-dangling. Verified against the live schema\nrather than the migration: columns, types, nullability, both unique constraints\nand every cascade.\nThe table entries carry the two absences, since those are what a reader will\notherwise assume are oversights: `scoring_criterion` has no weight, because a\ncriterion''s weight is the sum of its sub-criteria''s and a stored one could only\ndisagree with what actually scores; `scoring_sub_criterion_step` has no score,\nbecause it derives from the scale''s own length. `scoring_role_step` has no\n`updated_at` because row existence is the assignment.\nA conceptual **Scoring model (starfsmat)** section goes in beside the API-keys\none, where the rest of this document explains why a thing exists before listing\nits columns. The point it makes is the one a reader needs first: a filing takes\na frozen copy and there is no FK back, which is what lets a company rework its\nstarfsmat without moving the figures on a report already filed — and why the\nmodel needs no version.\n\n* fix(doe): act on kthorri''s review of #1508\nAll six must-fix, every should-fix, and the optional items that were real.\n**A negative weight reached filed scores.** `@ApiNumber` is `IsNumber()` and\nnothing else, and every weight rule — the validator''s and the filing gate''s —\ncompares only the sum against 100. `60, 60, -20` totals 100, reports VALID, and\nemits negative step scores, because `computeStepScore` is an unguarded\n`(order / numSteps) * weight * SCORE_FACTOR`: a negative weight inverts its\nsub-criterion''s scale so a higher þrep scores lower. That lands in\n`computeEmployeeScores`, an employee''s x-coordinate in the wage-gap regression,\nwith nothing raised anywhere. `@Min(0) @Max(100)` on both DTOs is the only thing\nthat can catch it — a sum check never will. The upper bound also made\n`weight: 100` reachable, so the column widens to `NUMERIC(7,4)`; at (6,4) a\nsingle sub-criterion carrying the whole distribution overflowed into a 500.\n**`VALID` was weaker than the gate it claimed parity with**, in three ways: the\ncapacity ceilings (`MAX_CRITERIA` is 5, and four mandatory types plus one\npersonal already sit at it), duplicate role titles, and duplicate criterion\ntitles — the pair check keyed on `(criterionTitle, subTitle)`, so two criteria\nboth titled \"Ábyrgð\" passed. The PR body called the duplication between the two\nrule sets a deliberate deferral; the review''s correction is the right one — the\nduplication was the safe half, the divergence was the defect.\nSo rather than adding three rules, `validateScoringModel` now runs the real gate\nas a backstop: it expands the model with no employees and folds\n`assertWithinCapacity` and `collectParsedPayloadIntegrity` into its reason list,\nfiltered to the scopes a model without employees can own. Both are exported for\nthis. Divergence is now structurally impossible rather than fixed once. It found\na defect immediately: a spec asserting \"two criteria of one type are fine\" was\nasserting something unfileable, because it reused the type name as the title —\nand the integrator guide said the same thing, documenting a path into a model\nthat could never be filed.\n**`setSteps` took an unbounded array** while its own description promised 2–8;\n`MIN_STEPS`/`MAX_STEPS` were imported into that file only to interpolate into\nthe sentence. One request could insert hundreds of thousands of þrep, and\n`deleteModel` loads the tree before destroying it, so the only cleanup path had\nto read what it could not read. `@ArrayMinSize`/`@ArrayMaxSize` here, on the\nassignment array, and on the employee extract — the last because\n`assertWithinCapacity` enforces the same ceiling only after the model is loaded\nand the payload expanded, so a deliberately O(1) guard was running last.\n**`SCORING_WRITE` was in the default scope set** while its own docblock three\nlines above said a filing-only vendor needs it never. Removed: a default key\ncould DELETE a company''s whole starfsmat by cascade.\nAlso: `separate: true` on both second-level collections, which were joining into\na cartesian product paid twice per mutation — with their ordering moved onto the\nincludes, since a top-level clause cannot reach a separately-fetched one, and\n`stepAssignments` gaining ordering it never had. `satisfies` instead of\n`as unknown as`, restoring the compile-time fidelity guarantee the assertion\ndiscarded. 404 declared on the thirteen routes that can answer it and 201 on the\nfour POSTs. The guide''s scopes table, its criteria rules and the db README''s\nscope list corrected.\nTests: the cross-check the review asked for — build a VALID model, expand it,\nassert the real validators accept it — which fails the moment the two rule sets\ndrift again and would have caught all three divergences in CI. Plus the\nownership gate, which was the only code enforcing company ownership before a\nfiling and was mocked away in every spec that touched it; tenant isolation on\nboth filing routes; a controller spec where there was none; and the ordering\npinned on the query rather than through `toDto`''s in-memory sort, which is why\ndeleting both `order` blocks used to leave the suite green.\n\n* fix(doe): act on the re-review of #1508\n\nEvery should-fix and every optional. Three of them were faults in the previous\nround''s fixes rather than new ground, which is the pattern worth naming: each\ntime, the named instance was fixed and its siblings were not swept for.\n\n**The dedupe in the backstop was dead code.** It compared rendered sentences\nwhile the two rule sets are worded differently by design, so it never matched\nand each double-covered fault was reported twice under two scopes. Rather than\nkey it on a rule identity, the duplication is gone at source: every rule the\ngate covers — the mandatory types, the personal cap, the weight total, the role\ncompleteness, the scale length, duplicate title pairs, \"at least one job\" — is\ndeleted from the hand-written set, and `runFilingGate` now runs\n`collectParsedPayloadSemantics` as well as the integrity half. What remains\nhand-written is only what the gate does not check. The gate''s\n`subCriterionLabel` renders the same `Ábyrgð / Mannaforráð` form these did, so\nthe better-labels argument for keeping them had already stopped being true.\n\n**The early return made the backstop worse than no backstop for the commonest\nedit there is.** Deleting a criterion strands the jobs assigned on its\nsub-criteria; the expansion refused; the gate never ran; and the\nmissing-mandatory-type reason it should have produced was never emitted. Found\nby the specs, not by reading. Unresolvable assignments and duplicate title pairs\nare now reported and then held back, and the gate runs over what is left —\ndropping only ever removes reasons, and each dropped one is stated first.\n\n**Scopes were assigned by where a message came from rather than what it names.**\nA 101-job overflow arrived labelled `CRITERIA` and every expansion failure\n`SUB_CRITERIA`, including role faults — against a DTO that sells `scope` as the\nfield a caller routes its UI on. Classified by content now, and the published\ndescriptions of all five values brought back in line with what each carries.\n\n`analyzeSalaryReport` was the fifth POST of the four that were fixed, and the\nworst to miss: a bare `@Post` answering 201 while documenting 200, with no 404\ndeclared even though a foreign `scoringModelId` is exactly the tenant-isolation\nrefusal. `@HttpCode(HttpStatus.OK)` and `include404`.\n\nRemoving `SCORING_WRITE` from the default scopes had a consequence I did not\nfollow through: `IssueApiKeyModal` has no scopes field, so every admin-issued\nkey silently lost the ability to author a starfsmat — and the admin path is the\nfallback for a company that has lost its key. The modal now offers filing-only\nor filing-plus-scoring, sends the set explicitly rather than relying on the\nserver default, and resets to filing-only on reopen, since carrying a previous\ngrant into the next key is the surprise the narrowing existed to avoid. The\nclient schema is regenerated so the fourth scope exists in its types.\n\n`stepAssignments` ordered on `createdAt` alone was an all-ties sort — one\n`bulkCreate` stamps one millisecond — so the reshuffle survived its own fix;\n`id` is the tiebreak now. `separate: true` moved up to `criteria` and `roles`,\nwhich are the two branches that actually multiply and which the comment had\nbeen blaming while the fix sat a level below them. `setSteps`'' empty-clear\nbranch and its spec are gone, closed by the `@ArrayMinSize` that made them\nunreachable. The expander''s role branch gained the `step.subCriterionId` check\nits employee branch already made — a cross-wired `stepId` resolved to a real\nþrep of the named sub-criterion at the other one''s order, scoring silently\nwrong, and nothing in that file prevented it.\n\nTests: `scoring-dto-bounds.spec.ts` runs class-validator over the DTOs, which no\nspec in this library did — so every bound added last round was pinned by a\nmanual check against a running server and nothing else. It found its own bug\nimmediately: a helper reading only top-level `constraints` reports an array of\ninvalid items as valid. And the test named for the weight fix asserted the\nopposite of its name — that negative scores *are* produced — which is true and\nuseful but is why the bound must live on the DTO, not evidence that it does. It\nis renamed for what it asserts; the bound is pinned in the new file. Its sibling\ncomputed 666.67 in a comment and asserted 250; both quantities are now asserted,\nthe second through `computeEmployeeScores`.\n\nDocs: the scope enum table in the db README (line 643 corrected while 707 was\ncorrected last round, in the same file), the weight precision after the\nwidening, the payroll field count, and the `create-api-key` description that\n`SCORING_WRITE`''s removal had made false.","tree":{"sha":"1e880492e809751d09d3b293fc8da316934b3509","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/1e880492e809751d09d3b293fc8da316934b3509"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b","verification":{"payload":"tree 1e880492e809751d09d3b293fc8da316934b3509\nparent e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884\nauthor Jón Bjarni Ólafsson <dev.jonbjarni@gmail.com> 1789477574 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789477574 +0000\n\nfeat(doe): file salary reports against a stored scoring model (#1508)\n\n* feat(doe): give the partner API a company-scoped scoring model\nEvery salary report re-transmits its whole scoring model today: the criteria\ntree, every step description, and one assignment per role x job-based\nsub-criterion, all bound together by title strings. Nothing is company-scoped —\nno model carries a company_id — so a company that files annually sends the same\nstarfsmat every year.\nThat is survivable on the island.is surface, which has a UI to build it in, and\non the Excel surface, where the workbook carries it. It is not survivable on the\npartner API: a payroll vendor holds none of it and would have to build screens\nto collect the employer''s starfsmat before it could file anything.\nThese tables give the model a home. Authored once, referenced by id. Nothing\nexisting changes — the report_* criteria tree stays the per-report frozen\nsnapshot, which is what keeps a filed report interpretable after the company\nreworks its model, and is why these tables carry no version.\nTwo columns are deliberately absent. scoring_criterion has no weight: a\ncriterion''s weight is the sum of its own sub-criteria''s, and computeStepScore\nreads only the sub-criterion weight, so a stored criterion weight reaches no\nscore and can only disagree with the figures that do — today a criterion can\ndisplay 40% while its sub-criteria account for 10% of what actually scores,\nbecause the two sums are each checked against 100 and never against each other.\nDeriving it collapses the two weight rules into one. scoring_sub_criterion_step\nhas no score: it is (stepOrder / numSteps) x weight x SCORE_FACTOR, and asking a\ncaller to send it invites a vendor to file stig on a scale of their own.\nWrites may leave the model incomplete and still succeed. A model is built over\nmany calls and its weights do not reach 100 until the last sub-criterion lands,\nso refusing every intermediate state would make it impossible to author one.\nEvery response instead carries `validation` — status plus every reason at once,\nnot just the first — and the filing is what refuses. VALID means the model is\ncomplete, never that the next filing will succeed: two of the submission''s rules\nneed the filing''s employees and cannot be decided here.\nStep contiguity is validated because the derived score makes it load-bearing:\nwith orders 1, 2 and 5 the scale has three steps and the last scores 5/3 of its\nown maximum.\nThe new scoring:write scope is separate from the submit scopes because it is a\ndifferent act by a possibly different party — a vendor that only files needs\nreport:read to reference a model somebody else authored, and never this.\n\n* fix(doe): make the scoring model''s PATCH and list order behave\nTwo defects the first manual pass turned up.\n**PATCH refused every partial body.** The update DTOs declared their fields with\n`@ApiString({ required: false })`, which relaxes the published document and\nnothing else — class-validator still demanded them, so `{\"weight\": 30}` came\nback 400 with \"title must be a string\". The `ApiOptional*` decorators are the\nones that carry both halves. PATCH now does what its own service comment always\nsaid it did: an omitted key is left alone.\n**The tree came back in arbitrary order.** Nothing ordered the includes, so\nPostgres returned criteria and sub-criteria however it liked and the order\nshifted between identical calls — a caller rendering the list would watch it\nreshuffle. Ordered by insertion now. Steps stay ordered by `stepOrder`, which is\ntheir own meaning rather than an arbitrary tie-break.\n\n* docs(doe): put the scoring model''s contract in the published document\nThe idea the whole surface is built around — that a write succeeds even when it\nleaves the model incomplete, and the filing is what refuses — was written only\nin the controller''s class docstring. Nest does not emit class-level JSDoc into\nSwagger, so none of it reached the document an integrator actually reads: they\nsaw `validation: { status, reasons }` with no description on either field and\nnothing to suggest that a 201 carrying INVALID is the normal intermediate state\nrather than a failed call.\nFour gaps closed, all in the schema where a reader cannot miss them:\n- `status` carries the writes-succeed contract, and the warning that VALID means\n  the model is complete and *not* that the next filing will succeed — naming the\n  two employee-dependent rules that cannot be decided without a filing. That is\n  the likeliest way for an integration to get burned: model reads VALID, filing\n  400s, and nothing warned them.\n- `scope` documents all five enum values. They shipped as bare strings, so a\n  vendor routing reasons into their own UI had to guess what ROLE_ASSIGNMENTS\n  covered.\n- `message` says the text is Icelandic and written for the employer who has to\n  fix it, rather than for the integrator — it can be shown as-is.\n- `reasons` gains a worked example, and says it carries every reason at once.\nThe guide is deliberately untouched: it has no scoring-model section yet, and a\nwalkthrough written now could not reach VALID, since steps and roles have no\nendpoints. It gets written when the surface is complete.\n\n* test(doe): cover the scoring model''s rules and its ownership gate\nThe slice shipped with no specs, which left the validator — seven rules that\ndecide whether a model may file — held up by nothing but a manual pass.\n`validate-scoring-model.spec.ts` pins each rule and, more usefully, the things\nabout them that are easy to get wrong later:\n- sub-criterion weights total 100 **across the whole model**, not within each\n  criterion, so a model at 40/20/20/20 is valid though no criterion is at 100.\n  This is the rule most likely to be \"corrected\" into a per-criterion check.\n- the mandatory requirement is on the criterion *type*, so two criteria of one\n  type are fine and only an absent type is a reason.\n- a role must be assigned on every job-based sub-criterion and never on a\n  personal one, which is scored per employee.\n- step orders must be contiguous from 1, because the score is\n  (stepOrder / numSteps) x weight x SCORE_FACTOR — orders 1, 2, 5 give a scale\n  of three whose last step scores 5/3 of its own maximum. Out-of-sequence but\n  contiguous orders are fine; the sort is not the contract.\n- an empty model answers with all five reasons at once rather than the first,\n  and the list stays bounded.\n`scoring-model.service.spec.ts` covers the ownership gate (scoped by companyId\nin the query, so another company''s model is indistinguishable from a missing\none), criterion weight derived from its own sub-criteria, steps ordered by\nstepOrder, PATCH leaving omitted keys alone, and a write succeeding where it\nleaves the model unfit to file.\n\n* feat(doe): let a scoring model''s scales be written, whole\nSteps (þrep) hang off a sub-criterion and nothing else — a criterion is a label\nwith no scale of its own — so this is the last structural piece before roles.\n**A scale is replaced whole, not edited a step at a time.** Both reasons are\nstructural rather than stylistic. A þrep''s score is\n`(stepOrder / numSteps) x weight x SCORE_FACTOR`, so the orders must run 1..n\nwith no gaps; a caller adding one step at a time necessarily passes through\nstates where they do not, and no single call can repair that. And a scale has a\nminimum length, so the first step of a new one would always be invalid alone.\nTaking the array whole makes both impossible instead of merely detected: order\ncomes from position, so there is no order field to send and no gap to make.\nReplacing drops any role step assignment onto the old scale, through the FK\ncascade. That is deliberate and differs from the draft module, which re-homes\nassignments onto surviving siblings — it does that because a UI must not lose a\nuser''s work mid-edit. Here the caller is a program rebuilding the model, and a\ndropped assignment surfaces immediately as \"this job is missing an assignment\"\nrather than being silently re-pointed at a step nobody chose.\nAlso closes a gap the step work exposed: the validator never checked\nMIN_STEPS/MAX_STEPS, which `assertParsedPayloadIntegrity` enforces on every\nfiling path. A scale of one step, or of twelve, read VALID here and would have\nbeen refused at submit — precisely the \"previews clean, rejected at submit\"\nfailure this validation exists to prevent. Verified live: 1 and 9 steps both\nreport the bound, 2 and 8 pass.\n\n* feat(doe): give a scoring model its jobs, and their step assignments\nThe last structural piece. A model can now reach VALID: four mandatory criterion\ntypes, sub-criteria totalling 100, a scale on each, and a job assigned across\nall of them — verified end to end against a running server.\nJobs are ordinary CRUD; their **step assignments are written whole**, for the\nreason a scale is. Exactly one assignment per job per sub-criterion is a\nuniqueness the table itself holds, so a partial write cannot move a job from one\nþrep to another without deleting first, and every partial write risks colliding\nwith the constraint. Taking the set whole makes the uniqueness structural.\nThe split between what is reported and what is refused matters, and is the\ndistinction the whole surface turns on:\n- **Incomplete** — a job assigned on two of four sub-criteria — is accepted and\n  reported, like every other incompleteness here. The model is mid-authoring.\n- **Incoherent** — a step belonging to a different sub-criterion, a\n  sub-criterion outside the model, a personal sub-criterion (scored per\n  employee, never per job), or the same sub-criterion twice — is refused with a\n  400. None of those describe a model that could exist, so storing them would\n  persist a row nothing could ever score.\nValidation runs before the destroy, so a refused call leaves the previous\nassignments intact; confirmed live, the model stayed VALID through all four\nrefusals.\nAlso fixes an ambiguity the first complete run exposed: reasons named a\nsub-criterion by its own title, and those are not unique — a model whose criteria\neach hold a \"Menntun\" produced four identical \"vantar úthlutun fyrir „Menntun“\"\nlines naming nothing actionable. They now carry the parent, in the same\n`Ábyrgð / Mannaforráð` form the submission validator uses.\n\n* docs(doe): drop the X-Road contrast from the partner API description\nThe line explained this surface by naming what it is not, which only helps a\nreader who already knows the island.is channel exists — and the integrator this\ndocument is written for does not. What remains says what the API is and who it\nis for.\n\n* fix(doe): carry the scoring model onto the merged main\nTwo things #1483 changed underneath this branch.\n**The models no longer loaded under @swc/jest.** #1500 moved doe-modules and the\npartner API off ts-jest, and swc does not tolerate the circular import between a\nparent model and its child: `emitDecoratorMetadata` writes `design:type`\nreferencing the class, which is evaluated while the module is still\ninitialising, so the child threw `Cannot access ''ScoringSubCriterionModel''\nbefore initialization` at load. The repo already has the answer in\n`postcode.model.ts` — import the parent twice, a type-only alias for the\nproperty annotation and the value import for the decorator''s arrow, so the\nannotation never forces an eager reference. Applied to all five child models.\nArray-typed associations are unaffected: `design:type` for those is `Array`.\n**The scoring surface did not check the register.** `RequireActiveCompanyGuard`\nbecame part of `declaresAccess` in #1483, so the coverage spec now fails a chain\nthat authenticates without it — and it correctly failed for this controller,\nwhich had neither the decorator nor the guard. That is a real gap rather than a\nspec technicality: a company that has fallen off Jafnréttisstofa''s register\ncould author a starfsmat it would never be allowed to file against, which is\nprecisely the half-working surface the one-answer-everywhere decision rejected.\n\n* feat(doe): expand a stored scoring model into the payload the pipeline takes\nThe seam the whole exercise was for. A stored model plus a payroll extract\nbecomes exactly the `ParsedReportDto` the submission pipeline already receives,\nso validation, scoring, outlier detection, storage, the per-report snapshot and\nevery admin read are untouched and cannot fork per channel. What the partner\nsurface stops doing is asking a vendor to transmit the criteria tree and the\nrole matrix — the company''s, unchanged since last year, and not data a payroll\nsystem holds.\nTwo translations live here, both because the pipeline addresses by **title**\nwhile a stored model addresses by **id**.\nStep scores are derived rather than carried, through the same `computeStepScore`\nthe workbook parser uses, so a report filed on this channel sits on the same\nstig scale as one filed on any other. Asking a vendor for the number invited a\nreport scored on a scale of their own, which nothing downstream validates.\nIds become titles, and that is where the expansion refuses rather than\ntranslates: a job or sub-criterion outside the model, a step belonging to a\ndifferent sub-criterion, a personal assignment onto a job-based sub-criterion\n(scored per job, never per employee). None describe a filing that could exist.\nIt also refuses a model holding two sub-criteria with the same\n`(criterionTitle, subTitle)` pair, because the pipeline keys on exactly that and\nwould collapse them onto one row. Titles are unconstrained, so this is\nreachable — and the model validator now reports it as a reason too, since\nwithout that the model reads VALID and only the expansion refuses it, at filing.\nThat is the previews-clean-rejected-at-submit failure this validation exists to\nprevent, and it is the third instance of it we have found.\n`PartnerEmployeeDto` is the slim shape: fifteen payroll fields plus `roleId` and\n`personalSteps`. Everything but `personalSteps` is data a payroll system holds;\nthat one is the employer''s assessment, around a tenth of the weight, and is why\na vendor still needs somewhere to collect input — much less of one than a\nstarfsmat editor.\n\n* feat(doe): file a salary report against a stored scoring model\nThe seam, wired in. `SubmitPartnerSalaryReportDto` loses `parsed` and gains\n`scoringModelId` plus a flat `employees[]`; the analysis route takes the same\npair. The criteria tree, the þrep and the job step assignments stop crossing the\nwire entirely — they are the employer''s, unchanged since the last filing, and a\npayroll system does not hold them. A vendor now sends what it actually has.\n`PartnerSubmissionService` does the translation and nothing else, and it lives\nin the partner app rather than beside `ApplicationService` deliberately: putting\nit there would make `ApplicationCoreModule` import the scoring model and pull it\ninto island.is''s module graph for a concept that channel does not have. The same\nreasoning took `ReportExcelCoreModule` out of the shared module when the\nworkbook surface was dropped. Past the expansion it is the identical code every\nother channel runs — the submission rules, the equality gate, idempotent replay\nand event emission are not reachable from here and cannot fork per channel.\nThe preview expands through the same call as the submission, so a payload that\nanalyses clean is the payload that gets filed. Building the analysis tree any\nother way would have it answer a question the submission never asks, which is\nthe failure mode this whole round has kept finding.\n\n* docs(doe): bring the integrator guide and the ER diagram up to the scoring model\nThe guide still described the flow where a vendor builds the criteria tree into\nevery submission — it and the API had been disagreeing since the seam landed,\nand it is the document an integrator actually onboards from.\nSection B is now the payroll extract rather than the payload: what you send is a\nscoring model id and one row per employee, and the tree, the þrep and the job\nassignments do not cross the wire at all. B4 and B5 collapse into one step,\nbecause fetching the catalog is no longer part of filing — it is part of\nauthoring a model, which happens once. The steps renumber accordingly and the\nroute table gains the fifteen scoring-model endpoints.\nNew section C covers the model itself, and leads with whose work it is: not the\nvendor''s unless the employer asked, since authoring one is choosing\nsub-criteria, distributing weight (a policy decision about how a company values\nwork) and writing þrep descriptions. It states the rules a model must satisfy —\nincluding that sub-criterion weights total 100 across the *whole* model rather\nthan per criterion, which is the one most likely to be misread — and explains\nwhy writes succeed while a model is incomplete, and why VALID does not promise\nthe next filing succeeds.\nThe ER diagram gains the six `scoring_*` tables. The note above it says why they\nsit apart from the report graph with no FK back from a report: a filing\nmaterialises its own frozen copy, which is what lets a company rework its\nstarfsmat without moving the figures on a report already filed, and is why the\nmodel carries no version.\n\n* docs(doe): document the scoring tables in the db README\n`DIAGRAM.md` points here for full column lists, so the six `scoring_*` tables\nbeing absent left the diagram half-dangling. Verified against the live schema\nrather than the migration: columns, types, nullability, both unique constraints\nand every cascade.\nThe table entries carry the two absences, since those are what a reader will\notherwise assume are oversights: `scoring_criterion` has no weight, because a\ncriterion''s weight is the sum of its sub-criteria''s and a stored one could only\ndisagree with what actually scores; `scoring_sub_criterion_step` has no score,\nbecause it derives from the scale''s own length. `scoring_role_step` has no\n`updated_at` because row existence is the assignment.\nA conceptual **Scoring model (starfsmat)** section goes in beside the API-keys\none, where the rest of this document explains why a thing exists before listing\nits columns. The point it makes is the one a reader needs first: a filing takes\na frozen copy and there is no FK back, which is what lets a company rework its\nstarfsmat without moving the figures on a report already filed — and why the\nmodel needs no version.\n\n* fix(doe): act on kthorri''s review of #1508\nAll six must-fix, every should-fix, and the optional items that were real.\n**A negative weight reached filed scores.** `@ApiNumber` is `IsNumber()` and\nnothing else, and every weight rule — the validator''s and the filing gate''s —\ncompares only the sum against 100. `60, 60, -20` totals 100, reports VALID, and\nemits negative step scores, because `computeStepScore` is an unguarded\n`(order / numSteps) * weight * SCORE_FACTOR`: a negative weight inverts its\nsub-criterion''s scale so a higher þrep scores lower. That lands in\n`computeEmployeeScores`, an employee''s x-coordinate in the wage-gap regression,\nwith nothing raised anywhere. `@Min(0) @Max(100)` on both DTOs is the only thing\nthat can catch it — a sum check never will. The upper bound also made\n`weight: 100` reachable, so the column widens to `NUMERIC(7,4)`; at (6,4) a\nsingle sub-criterion carrying the whole distribution overflowed into a 500.\n**`VALID` was weaker than the gate it claimed parity with**, in three ways: the\ncapacity ceilings (`MAX_CRITERIA` is 5, and four mandatory types plus one\npersonal already sit at it), duplicate role titles, and duplicate criterion\ntitles — the pair check keyed on `(criterionTitle, subTitle)`, so two criteria\nboth titled \"Ábyrgð\" passed. The PR body called the duplication between the two\nrule sets a deliberate deferral; the review''s correction is the right one — the\nduplication was the safe half, the divergence was the defect.\nSo rather than adding three rules, `validateScoringModel` now runs the real gate\nas a backstop: it expands the model with no employees and folds\n`assertWithinCapacity` and `collectParsedPayloadIntegrity` into its reason list,\nfiltered to the scopes a model without employees can own. Both are exported for\nthis. Divergence is now structurally impossible rather than fixed once. It found\na defect immediately: a spec asserting \"two criteria of one type are fine\" was\nasserting something unfileable, because it reused the type name as the title —\nand the integrator guide said the same thing, documenting a path into a model\nthat could never be filed.\n**`setSteps` took an unbounded array** while its own description promised 2–8;\n`MIN_STEPS`/`MAX_STEPS` were imported into that file only to interpolate into\nthe sentence. One request could insert hundreds of thousands of þrep, and\n`deleteModel` loads the tree before destroying it, so the only cleanup path had\nto read what it could not read. `@ArrayMinSize`/`@ArrayMaxSize` here, on the\nassignment array, and on the employee extract — the last because\n`assertWithinCapacity` enforces the same ceiling only after the model is loaded\nand the payload expanded, so a deliberately O(1) guard was running last.\n**`SCORING_WRITE` was in the default scope set** while its own docblock three\nlines above said a filing-only vendor needs it never. Removed: a default key\ncould DELETE a company''s whole starfsmat by cascade.\nAlso: `separate: true` on both second-level collections, which were joining into\na cartesian product paid twice per mutation — with their ordering moved onto the\nincludes, since a top-level clause cannot reach a separately-fetched one, and\n`stepAssignments` gaining ordering it never had. `satisfies` instead of\n`as unknown as`, restoring the compile-time fidelity guarantee the assertion\ndiscarded. 404 declared on the thirteen routes that can answer it and 201 on the\nfour POSTs. The guide''s scopes table, its criteria rules and the db README''s\nscope list corrected.\nTests: the cross-check the review asked for — build a VALID model, expand it,\nassert the real validators accept it — which fails the moment the two rule sets\ndrift again and would have caught all three divergences in CI. Plus the\nownership gate, which was the only code enforcing company ownership before a\nfiling and was mocked away in every spec that touched it; tenant isolation on\nboth filing routes; a controller spec where there was none; and the ordering\npinned on the query rather than through `toDto`''s in-memory sort, which is why\ndeleting both `order` blocks used to leave the suite green.\n\n* fix(doe): act on the re-review of #1508\n\nEvery should-fix and every optional. Three of them were faults in the previous\nround''s fixes rather than new ground, which is the pattern worth naming: each\ntime, the named instance was fixed and its siblings were not swept for.\n\n**The dedupe in the backstop was dead code.** It compared rendered sentences\nwhile the two rule sets are worded differently by design, so it never matched\nand each double-covered fault was reported twice under two scopes. Rather than\nkey it on a rule identity, the duplication is gone at source: every rule the\ngate covers — the mandatory types, the personal cap, the weight total, the role\ncompleteness, the scale length, duplicate title pairs, \"at least one job\" — is\ndeleted from the hand-written set, and `runFilingGate` now runs\n`collectParsedPayloadSemantics` as well as the integrity half. What remains\nhand-written is only what the gate does not check. The gate''s\n`subCriterionLabel` renders the same `Ábyrgð / Mannaforráð` form these did, so\nthe better-labels argument for keeping them had already stopped being true.\n\n**The early return made the backstop worse than no backstop for the commonest\nedit there is.** Deleting a criterion strands the jobs assigned on its\nsub-criteria; the expansion refused; the gate never ran; and the\nmissing-mandatory-type reason it should have produced was never emitted. Found\nby the specs, not by reading. Unresolvable assignments and duplicate title pairs\nare now reported and then held back, and the gate runs over what is left —\ndropping only ever removes reasons, and each dropped one is stated first.\n\n**Scopes were assigned by where a message came from rather than what it names.**\nA 101-job overflow arrived labelled `CRITERIA` and every expansion failure\n`SUB_CRITERIA`, including role faults — against a DTO that sells `scope` as the\nfield a caller routes its UI on. Classified by content now, and the published\ndescriptions of all five values brought back in line with what each carries.\n\n`analyzeSalaryReport` was the fifth POST of the four that were fixed, and the\nworst to miss: a bare `@Post` answering 201 while documenting 200, with no 404\ndeclared even though a foreign `scoringModelId` is exactly the tenant-isolation\nrefusal. `@HttpCode(HttpStatus.OK)` and `include404`.\n\nRemoving `SCORING_WRITE` from the default scopes had a consequence I did not\nfollow through: `IssueApiKeyModal` has no scopes field, so every admin-issued\nkey silently lost the ability to author a starfsmat — and the admin path is the\nfallback for a company that has lost its key. The modal now offers filing-only\nor filing-plus-scoring, sends the set explicitly rather than relying on the\nserver default, and resets to filing-only on reopen, since carrying a previous\ngrant into the next key is the surprise the narrowing existed to avoid. The\nclient schema is regenerated so the fourth scope exists in its types.\n\n`stepAssignments` ordered on `createdAt` alone was an all-ties sort — one\n`bulkCreate` stamps one millisecond — so the reshuffle survived its own fix;\n`id` is the tiebreak now. `separate: true` moved up to `criteria` and `roles`,\nwhich are the two branches that actually multiply and which the comment had\nbeen blaming while the fix sat a level below them. `setSteps`'' empty-clear\nbranch and its spec are gone, closed by the `@ArrayMinSize` that made them\nunreachable. The expander''s role branch gained the `step.subCriterionId` check\nits employee branch already made — a cross-wired `stepId` resolved to a real\nþrep of the named sub-criterion at the other one''s order, scoring silently\nwrong, and nothing in that file prevented it.\n\nTests: `scoring-dto-bounds.spec.ts` runs class-validator over the DTOs, which no\nspec in this library did — so every bound added last round was pinned by a\nmanual check against a running server and nothing else. It found its own bug\nimmediately: a helper reading only top-level `constraints` reports an array of\ninvalid items as valid. And the test named for the weight fix asserted the\nopposite of its name — that negative scores *are* produced — which is true and\nuseful but is why the bound must live on the DTO, not evidence that it does. It\nis renamed for what it asserts; the bound is pinned in the new file. Its sibling\ncomputed 666.67 in a comment and asserted 250; both quantities are now asserted,\nthe second through `computeEmployeeScores`.\n\nDocs: the scope enum table in the db README (line 643 corrected while 707 was\ncorrected last round, in the same file), the weight precision after the\nwidening, the payroll field count, and the `create-api-key` description that\n`SCORING_WRITE`''s removal had made false.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqULGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4PkQAACIdHa1Q7KpG01Jn1Ll15Hn\ni73kHcnDxQGHYh3v9K9RsZeNuxb3v330YcNx4jLgoiBiylCfzqdPpQ+cbczIyMwG\nu2RYvJ10+XfSoqeTycWqzyKhH1JF54L/hVWzfIlcXMA+4LOsIeJlXObhMi2f7csO\nDkOdETXGpxiDjNtwoy4o6ZCk/LBWuhQ2+wL13DYq6Wh/1kHubtAYt/kz0g4a4WvD\n0YPg0bIHY1dtH/qKOEdJ4NXFoy9oKM3fpp79W9u6OtK5ypZb0vuE8WJbpYnlNmII\nxYbPBiZhYms2CPCpRAOBbtjpbHjY1ZxYjp7GhbzfFzAxNcMIUFZ2d/WkKfCq8hUN\ns0K54Ow8pd4CSM43Ltlu0KHttVq+eF2ILgrOFx2FF4tfGFRDXC4al7KSBmBJ26tM\nVUknbUpOT2sqAR8X8GANHag04e9zkbNXjJdsxso4bT8n/zm2f9W/pHIhtx7nlwET\nSyCELbHOBtMTn7h0CmT/dPVvryt8d3pYrcuxsDKQfWpAOVLrWGAD6HxrjUFyZjdd\nT0tgRsvIKZA2dgU17n1RozrpgHblGclmwQ0Jn1EuFk4+6lUnz8styINGeVLzLQvd\nLRP2VIo5q3N52PfHlHz03Vx4cplpHgLK8RMTEukkScXEzeGY8HWPXzGZSRT6EmNb\nbmioD7CHt9Oc+sEr2uOf\n=EpuR\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:06:15Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKDhlNzk4NDIwODU1YTNmODk3YjM5MmNjYTlkMDU1NmEwNTY4N2JlMWI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884","sha":"e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884"}],"sha":"8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/8e798420855a3f897b392cca9d0556a05687be1b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T10:53:06Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T10:53:06Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test(regulations): run regulations-api through @swc/jest (#1510)\n\nRe-measured after prior local-only measurement (7.7s vs 7.1s) undersold this\nproject''s CI cost (28.5-29.7s cold). Cold local delta holds at roughly\nthe same small proportion (~9.3s -> ~8.4s), but the config change is a\nzero-risk one-line diff -- no NestJS decorators and no cyclic Model-typed\nSequelize associations here (plain scalar @Column fields only), so none of\nthe decorator-metadata TDZ class of bugs that gated every other migration\napplies.","tree":{"sha":"7d83bcc08ce533367d1f324f9906fb7ba9446706","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/7d83bcc08ce533367d1f324f9906fb7ba9446706"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884","verification":{"payload":"tree 7d83bcc08ce533367d1f324f9906fb7ba9446706\nparent 89b59778e093cccce47688ba12ebc37df781cf28\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1789469586 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789469586 +0000\n\ntest(regulations): run regulations-api through @swc/jest (#1510)\n\nRe-measured after prior local-only measurement (7.7s vs 7.1s) undersold this\nproject''s CI cost (28.5-29.7s cold). Cold local delta holds at roughly\nthe same small proportion (~9.3s -> ~8.4s), but the config change is a\nzero-risk one-line diff -- no NestJS decorators and no cyclic Model-typed\nSequelize associations here (plain scalar @Column fields only), so none of\nthe decorator-metadata TDZ class of bugs that gated every other migration\napplies.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqSOSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAT9AQAGgHf5OSelkuUTaf7cK950oa\nwnHkYao/UA/0iynYUmXWvphRV1nEk/tH0qOwg+/gckqrvJ0V+RLN0+ufEnuYMiKZ\nC3bRFho3NCQ4m5P8bhW315NnS99zX955mCOA5HhNUUks8IXAXYkgaZODtASkHtul\nmE5uf+xNHTB1X5uV3b99CU4vJHfxP81LCKtGZlJnY/SZc2/Vfmva6tcb+0uKs8rn\ncJclr6jJDNc7+dix13vsA3Pjn+QAxCRxzOFPoPeWh1Bh4rPELGXtXrXTIKVnqra5\np50ZXOujcOJ4UW98thCR6hjJxl7uIyAVxBpxej7HzMTYJjxcobjn9yo2VfRZ79Ey\nedPPre4ZpP0vClDBxUludfHUvHAm7DEwb1Y+HTzR/L08wBkaV7NgkSdnF3LBt7Rp\nBDrpI0F43MNbOSc+U6LTBhd6a9Pxq1LgOrO56R9QiT4JA01ubfODO/Z3Io+kDtxx\n2tr2v4led4v/q8a656SKM/5JxMrcBqRuXm+CZurEXVN9+flh4esnW3xvk+jQrAmp\nGBvtEZjhQ634aA3G5nUrB8HxZrb2LLG9pcGFx/m1etq+/iohvJ6BTdiHx1EofTZF\nDkODw603+EuHTL5utzo/GgvOfh+xncKrjIyi1Xigut5k4fox+3fHsVg0HgeUarfY\nstRhwyVo/4chrBVtf08K\n=py6T\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T10:53:06Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884","node_id":"C_kwDOKxEuldoAKGU1OTIwNDYwODQ5NTlkOTJlNTljOTQxMmZhNzhhMTA2Yjg3ZTY4ODQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DMR-is/dmr.is/commit/89b59778e093cccce47688ba12ebc37df781cf28","sha":"89b59778e093cccce47688ba12ebc37df781cf28","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/89b59778e093cccce47688ba12ebc37df781cf28"}],"sha":"e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/e592046084959d92e59c9412fa78a106b87e6884"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9976684?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kthorri","id":9976684,"login":"kthorri","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjk5NzY2ODQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kthorri","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/commits/89b59778e093cccce47688ba12ebc37df781cf28/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T10:52:51Z","email":"kristinn.thorri@gmail.com","name":"Kristinn Þorri"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T10:52:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs(lg): correct the eager-site table in models.md (#1509)\n\ndocs(lg): correct the eager-site table in models.md - the three sites are fine\n\nThe \"What actually resolves today\" table added in #1505 was wrong. It reported\ncourtDistrict, settlement and signature on AdvertDto as resolving to undefined,\ni.e. shipping a broken Swagger schema. They are not broken.\n\nAdvertDto is PickType(AdvertDetailedDto, [...14 props]). All seven eager\n@Api*Dto sites live on AdvertDetailedDto, and probed there every one resolves to\nits real class. The generated client matches the pick list exactly.\n\nThe error was in the probe: it read `metadata?.type` and reported \"undefined\"\nboth when a property has no metadata at all (not picked onto this class) and\nwhen a property''s metadata has an undefined type (genuinely broken). Those are\ndifferent findings. Probing the PickType subclass returned the former and it was\nreported as the latter.\n\nReplaces that section with how to verify a site correctly, and records the\nPickType trap so the next audit does not repeat it.\n\nWhat does still hold, verified again here: importing a leaf model before\nadvert.model.ts throws \"Cannot access ''StatusDto'' before initialization\". That\nis a latent crash hazard, not a live schema defect, and remains tracked in #1506.\n\nDocs only.","tree":{"sha":"4bf9b5a2d5735f2da4cfbe2df5451b4fcdc5b957","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/trees/4bf9b5a2d5735f2da4cfbe2df5451b4fcdc5b957"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DMR-is/dmr.is/git/commits/89b59778e093cccce47688ba12ebc37df781cf28","verification":{"payload":"tree 4bf9b5a2d5735f2da4cfbe2df5451b4fcdc5b957\nparent c21222712f6dbe0d9839e59c39427e458c000da7\nauthor Kristinn Þorri <kristinn.thorri@gmail.com> 1789469571 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789469571 +0000\n\ndocs(lg): correct the eager-site table in models.md (#1509)\n\ndocs(lg): correct the eager-site table in models.md - the three sites are fine\n\nThe \"What actually resolves today\" table added in #1505 was wrong. It reported\ncourtDistrict, settlement and signature on AdvertDto as resolving to undefined,\ni.e. shipping a broken Swagger schema. They are not broken.\n\nAdvertDto is PickType(AdvertDetailedDto, [...14 props]). All seven eager\n@Api*Dto sites live on AdvertDetailedDto, and probed there every one resolves to\nits real class. The generated client matches the pick list exactly.\n\nThe error was in the probe: it read `metadata?.type` and reported \"undefined\"\nboth when a property has no metadata at all (not picked onto this class) and\nwhen a property''s metadata has an undefined type (genuinely broken). Those are\ndifferent findings. Probing the PickType subclass returned the former and it was\nreported as the latter.\n\nReplaces that section with how to verify a site correctly, and records the\nPickType trap so the next audit does not repeat it.\n\nWhat does still hold, verified again here: importing a leaf model before\nadvert.model.ts throws \"Cannot access ''StatusDto'' before initialization\". That\nis a latent crash hazard, not a live schema defect, and remains tracked in #1506.\n\nDocs only.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqSODCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAHcEQACQW+mNSvrpIEtoBykOQdK5B\n02oDo9g6wRTp9nNv6Vt5SJuiSR3r5gQybWeTn7awoXZ0cXa4jWEsstoyT02MagML\ngsXqqTIQ+HSpCUZByo6momtfr7jX2MGRvQ8ZLees+0XRPQ9NWnVsqrVbnKJQ9LhM\nCG7CPqJME54qcdHtLviiK06tHhSxCvgyZ/4PXB4NofoZhB3YdolpDCYvXEfup+gF\nwGlCF4hDaUwm16E4X9TUuOCCNwwI5I+rw0pQQjdIWfpF2DqYL